**How it works:** `tft.initR(INITR_BLACKTAB)` sends the correct initialization command sequence for the common black-tab variant of the ST7735 — using the wrong tab type causes washed-out or incorrect colors. The loop animates a dot by drawing it in magenta then immediately redrawing it in black to erase, which is far faster than calling `fillScreen` on every frame.

The MFRC522 runs on 3.3V and is not 5V tolerant, so it connects directly to the ESP32's hardware SPI bus (VSPI) without any level shifting.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SCK | GPIO 18 (VSPI SCK) |
| MISO | GPIO 19 (VSPI MISO) |
| MOSI | GPIO 23 (VSPI MOSI) |
| SDA (SS/CS) | GPIO 5 |
| RST | GPIO 22 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------------+          +-------------------+
|        ESP32         |          |     MFRC522       |
|                      |          |                   |
|  3.3V  |------------|----------| VCC               |
|  GND   |------------|----------| GND               |
|  GPIO18|------------|----------| SCK               |
|  GPIO19|------------|----------| MISO              |
|  GPIO23|------------|----------| MOSI              |
|  GPIO5 |------------|----------| SDA (CS)          |
|  GPIO22|------------|----------| RST               |
+----------------------+          +-------------------+
```

Keep wires short and avoid routing SPI lines near high-frequency sources, as the MFRC522 is sensitive to noise on the SPI bus.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <SPI.h>
#include <MFRC522.h>

#define SS_PIN  5
#define RST_PIN 22

MFRC522 rfid(SS_PIN, RST_PIN);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  SPI.begin();          // Initializes VSPI with default pins (SCK=18, MISO=19, MOSI=23)
  rfid.PCD_Init();      // Initialize the MFRC522
  Serial.println("MFRC522 RFID Reader Ready");
  Serial.println("Scan a card or tag...");
}

void loop() {
  // Check for a new card present and readable
  if (!rfid.PICC_IsNewCardPresent() || !rfid.PICC_ReadCardSerial()) {
    return;
  }

  Serial.print("Tag UID: ");
  for (byte i = 0; i < rfid.uid.size; i++) {
    if (rfid.uid.uidByte[i] < 0x10) {
      Serial.print("0");  // Leading zero for single-digit hex bytes
    }
    Serial.print(rfid.uid.uidByte[i], HEX);
    if (i < rfid.uid.size - 1) Serial.print(":");
  }
  Serial.println();

  // Print card type
  MFRC522::PICC_Type piccType = rfid.PICC_GetType(rfid.uid.sak);
  Serial.print("Card type: ");
  Serial.println(rfid.PICC_GetTypeName(piccType));

  // Halt the card and stop encryption to allow reading new cards
  rfid.PICC_HaltA();
  rfid.PCD_StopCrypto1();

  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** The two-step check `PICC_IsNewCardPresent()` followed by `PICC_ReadCardSerial()` is critical — the first detects a card in the RF field while the second actually selects it and loads its UID into `rfid.uid`; skipping either call will result in missed or corrupted reads. Calling `PICC_HaltA()` and `PCD_StopCrypto1()` at the end of each read resets the reader's state so it can detect the next card presentation instead of continuously re-reading the same tag.

The MAX6675 uses a read-only SPI interface (no MOSI needed) and outputs a 12-bit temperature value, so only SCK, CS, and MISO lines are required from the ESP32.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SCK | GPIO 18 (SPI SCK) |
| CS | GPIO 5 (any free GPIO) |
| SO (MISO) | GPIO 19 (SPI MISO) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +---------------------+          +-------------------+
        |        ESP32        |          |     MAX6675       |
        |                     |          |                   |
        |      GPIO 18 (SCK) |--------->| SCK               |
        |     GPIO 19 (MISO) |<---------| SO                |
        |       GPIO 5  (CS) |--------->| CS                |
        |               3.3V |--------->| VCC               |
        |               GND  |--------->| GND               |
        +---------------------+          |                   |
                                         | T+ ]-- K-type     |
                                         | T- ]-- thermocouple|
                                         +-------------------+
```
The MAX6675 is strictly 3.3V–5V tolerant on VCC but its logic outputs are compatible with 3.3V ESP32 inputs; powering from the ESP32's 3.3V rail works reliably.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <SPI.h>

// Pin definitions
const int CS_PIN  = 5;
const int SCK_PIN = 18;
const int MISO_PIN = 19;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(CS_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(CS_PIN, HIGH); // deselect by default

  // Initialize SPI: MAX6675 supports up to 4.3 MHz, mode 0, MSB first
  SPI.begin(SCK_PIN, MISO_PIN, -1, CS_PIN); // -1 = no MOSI
  Serial.println("MAX6675 Thermocouple Reader");
}

double readThermocouple() {
  uint16_t rawValue = 0;

  SPI.beginTransaction(SPISettings(1000000, MSBFIRST, SPI_MODE0));
  digitalWrite(CS_PIN, LOW);
  delayMicroseconds(1); // CS assertion time

  // MAX6675 sends 16 bits; temperature is bits 14:3 (12-bit value)
  rawValue = SPI.transfer16(0x0000);

  digitalWrite(CS_PIN, HIGH);
  SPI.endTransaction();

  // Bit 2 is the open thermocouple fault flag (1 = no thermocouple connected)
  if (rawValue & 0x04) {
    Serial.println("ERROR: Thermocouple not connected!");
    return -1.0;
  }

  // Shift right by 3 to get the 12-bit temperature, each LSB = 0.25°C
  rawValue >>= 3;
  return rawValue * 0.25;
}

void loop() {
  double tempC = readThermocouple();
  if (tempC >= 0) {
    Serial.print("Temperature: ");
    Serial.print(tempC, 2);
    Serial.println(" °C");
  }
  delay(1000); // MAX6675 needs ~220ms between conversions minimum
}
```

**How it works:** The raw 16-bit SPI word from the MAX6675 encodes the temperature in bits 14 through 3, so right-shifting by 3 and multiplying by 0.25 converts the 12-bit count directly to degrees Celsius. Bit 2 is checked first as an open-circuit fault flag — if it is set, the thermocouple is disconnected and the reading is invalid.

The SIM800L operates at 3.7–4.2V (not 3.3V or 5V directly), so it needs its own regulated power supply capable of supplying at least 2A peak current during transmission bursts; a dedicated LiPo or buck converter is strongly recommended rather than the ESP32's onboard regulators.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | External 4.0V regulated supply (2A capable) |
| GND | GND (shared with ESP32 GND) |
| TXD | GPIO16 (ESP32 RX2) |
| RXD | GPIO17 (ESP32 TX2) |
| RST | GPIO5 (any free GPIO) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------------+          +-------------------+
|        ESP32         |          |     SIM800L       |
|                      |          |                   |
|   GPIO17 (TX2) ------|--------->| RXD               |
|   GPIO16 (RX2) <-----|----------| TXD               |
|         GPIO5  ------|--------->| RST               |
|            GND |-----|----------| GND               |
|                      |          | VCC               |
+----------------------+          +--------+----------+
                                           |
                                  +--------+----------+
                                  | 4.0V Buck/LiPo    |
                                  | Supply (2A min)   |
                                  +-------------------+
```
Never power the SIM800L from the ESP32's 3.3V or USB 5V pins — the module's transmit current spikes can exceed 2A and will crash or damage the ESP32's regulator.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// SIM800L connected to ESP32 UART2
#define SIM800_TX 17   // ESP32 TX2 -> SIM800L RXD
#define SIM800_RX 16   // ESP32 RX2 <- SIM800L TXD
#define SIM800_RST 5   // Optional hardware reset pin

HardwareSerial sim800(2); // Use ESP32 UART2

void sendAT(const char* cmd, unsigned long timeout = 2000) {
  sim800.println(cmd);
  unsigned long start = millis();
  while (millis() - start < timeout) {
    while (sim800.available()) {
      Serial.write(sim800.read());
    }
  }
}

void resetSIM800() {
  pinMode(SIM800_RST, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(SIM800_RST, LOW);
  delay(100);
  digitalWrite(SIM800_RST, HIGH);
  delay(3000); // Wait for module to boot
}

void sendSMS(const char* phoneNumber, const char* message) {
  sendAT("AT");                    // Check comms
  sendAT("AT+CMGF=1");            // Set SMS text mode

  String cmd = "AT+CMGS=\"";
  cmd += phoneNumber;
  cmd += "\"";
  sim800.println(cmd);
  delay(500);

  sim800.print(message);           // Send message body
  delay(100);
  sim800.write(26);                // Send Ctrl+Z to finalize SMS (ASCII 26)
  delay(5000);                     // Wait for send confirmation

  // Print any response
  while (sim800.available()) {
    Serial.write(sim800.read());
  }
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  sim800.begin(9600, SERIAL_8N1, SIM800_RX, SIM800_TX);

  Serial.println("Resetting SIM800L...");
  resetSIM800();

  Serial.println("Waiting for network registration...");
  sendAT("AT+CREG?", 5000);        // Check network registration
  sendAT("AT+CSQ");                // Check signal quality

  Serial.println("Sending SMS...");
  sendSMS("+11234567890", "Hello from ESP32 and SIM800L!");
  Serial.println("SMS sent (if no error shown above).");
}

void loop() {
  // Relay SIM800L responses to Serial monitor
  while (sim800.available()) {
    Serial.write(sim800.read());
  }
  while (Serial.available()) {
    sim800.write(Serial.read());
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The critical line `sim800.write(26)` sends ASCII character 26 (Ctrl+Z), which is the SIM800L's required end-of-message signal that triggers the actual SMS transmission — without it, the module waits indefinitely. Using `HardwareSerial sim800(2)` assigns ESP32's second hardware UART to the module, which is important because software serial at 9600 baud is unreliable for the SIM800L's longer AT response strings.

The HC-05 operates at 3.3V logic but accepts a 5V supply on VCC, so its TX output is safe to connect directly to the ESP32 RX pin, while the HC-05 RX pin needs a voltage divider since it is not 5V-tolerant on logic input when powered from 5V — here we power it from the ESP32's 3.3V rail to keep logic levels safe and avoid a divider.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| TX | GPIO16 (RX2) |
| RX | GPIO17 (TX2) |
| EN/KEY | (leave unconnected for normal mode) |
| STATE | (optional, leave unconnected) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
|        ESP32        |          |      HC-05        |
|                     |          |                   |
|  3.3V ─────────────────────── VCC                 |
|  GND  ─────────────────────── GND                 |
|  GPIO16 (RX2) ──────────────── TX                 |
|  GPIO17 (TX2) ──────────────── RX                 |
|                     |          |                   |
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
```
Powering the HC-05 from the ESP32's 3.3V pin keeps all UART logic levels at 3.3V, avoiding any risk of over-voltage on the ESP32's GPIO inputs.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// Use UART2 on the ESP32 to communicate with HC-05
// This frees up UART0 (Serial) for debug output via USB
#define BT_RX_PIN 16  // ESP32 RX2 <- HC-05 TX
#define BT_TX_PIN 17  // ESP32 TX2 -> HC-05 RX
#define BT_BAUD   9600 // Default HC-05 baud rate

void setup() {
  // USB serial for debug monitoring
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Serial.println("HC-05 Bluetooth UART bridge ready");

  // Hardware serial port 2 for HC-05
  Serial2.begin(BT_BAUD, SERIAL_8N1, BT_RX_PIN, BT_TX_PIN);
}

void loop() {
  // Forward data from Bluetooth (HC-05) to USB Serial (PC monitor)
  if (Serial2.available()) {
    String received = Serial2.readStringUntil('\n');
    received.trim();
    Serial.print("[BT RX]: ");
    Serial.println(received);

    // Echo the received message back over Bluetooth
    Serial2.print("Echo: ");
    Serial2.println(received);
  }

  // Forward data typed in USB Serial monitor to Bluetooth device
  if (Serial.available()) {
    String toSend = Serial.readStringUntil('\n');
    toSend.trim();
    Serial2.println(toSend);
    Serial.print("[BT TX]: ");
    Serial.println(toSend);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** `Serial2.begin(BT_BAUD, SERIAL_8N1, BT_RX_PIN, BT_TX_PIN)` explicitly maps UART2 to GPIO16/17 rather than the default pins, which is required on ESP32 since UART peripheral pins are configurable via the IO mux. Using a dedicated hardware UART (`Serial2`) instead of SoftwareSerial ensures reliable timing at 9600 baud without blocking the main loop or risking data corruption.

The R307 fingerprint sensor communicates over UART at 3.3V logic levels, so it connects directly to the ESP32's hardware Serial2 peripheral without any level shifting.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC (red) | 3.3V |
| GND (black) | GND |
| TX (green) | GPIO16 (RX2) |
| RX (white) | GPIO17 (TX2) |
| WAKEUP (brown) | 3.3V (tie high to keep active) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +---------------------+          +-------------------+
        |        ESP32        |          |   R307 Fingerprint|
        |                     |          |                   |
        |      3.3V o---------+----------o VCC               |
        |       GND o---------+----------o GND               |
        |  GPIO16   o---------+----------o TX                |
        | (RX2)     |          |          |                   |
        |  GPIO17   o---------+----------o RX                |
        | (TX2)     |          |          |                   |
        |      3.3V o---------+----------o WAKEUP            |
        +---------------------+          +-------------------+
```
The R307 operates at 3.3V logic and should not be powered from 5V on the signal lines; confirm your module's VCC silkscreen before applying power.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Adafruit_Fingerprint.h>

// R307 connected to ESP32 Serial2: RX=GPIO16, TX=GPIO17
HardwareSerial fpSerial(2);
Adafruit_Fingerprint finger(&fpSerial);

// Set to the ID slot you want to enroll into (1-127)
uint8_t enrollID = 1;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  fpSerial.begin(57600, SERIAL_8N1, 16, 17);

  delay(500);
  Serial.println("R307 Fingerprint Sensor Demo");

  finger.begin(57600);

  if (finger.verifyPassword()) {
    Serial.println("Fingerprint sensor found!");
  } else {
    Serial.println("Fingerprint sensor NOT found. Check wiring.");
    while (true) { delay(1000); }
  }

  Serial.print("Sensor contains ");
  finger.getTemplateCount();
  Serial.print(finger.templateCount);
  Serial.println(" templates.");

  Serial.println("\n--- ENROLL MODE ---");
  enrollFingerprint(enrollID);

  Serial.println("\n--- VERIFY MODE ---");
  Serial.println("Place any enrolled finger to verify...");
}

void loop() {
  int result = getFingerprintID();
  if (result >= 0) {
    Serial.print("Match found! ID #");
    Serial.print(result);
    Serial.print("  Confidence: ");
    Serial.println(finger.confidence);
  } else if (result == -1) {
    Serial.println("No match found.");
  }
  delay(1500);
}

// Returns matched ID on success, -1 on no match, -2 on error
int getFingerprintID() {
  uint8_t p = finger.getImage();
  if (p == FINGERPRINT_NOFINGER) return -3; // No finger present
  if (p != FINGERPRINT_OK) return -2;

  p = finger.image2Tz();
  if (p != FINGERPRINT_OK) return -2;

  p = finger.fingerSearch();
  if (p == FINGERPRINT_OK) {
    return finger.fingerID;
  } else if (p == FINGERPRINT_NOTFOUND) {
    return -1;
  }
  return -2;
}

bool enrollFingerprint(uint8_t id) {
  Serial.print("Enrolling fingerprint as ID #");
  Serial.println(id);

  // First image capture
  Serial.println("Place finger on sensor...");
  uint8_t p = -1;
  while (p != FINGERPRINT_OK) {
    p = finger.getImage();
    if (p == FINGERPRINT_OK)        Serial.println("Image taken.");
    else if (p == FINGERPRINT_NOFINGER) delay(200);
    else { Serial.println("Image error."); return false; }
  }

  p = finger.image2Tz(1);
  if (p != FINGERPRINT_OK) { Serial.println("image2Tz(1) failed."); return false; }

  Serial.println("Remove finger.");
  delay(2000);
  while (finger.getImage() != FINGERPRINT_NOFINGER) { delay(200); }

  // Second image capture
  Serial.println("Place same finger again...");
  p = -1;
  while (p != FINGERPRINT_OK) {
    p = finger.getImage();
    if (p == FINGERPRINT_OK)        Serial.println("Image taken.");
    else if (p == FINGERPRINT_NOFINGER) delay(200);
    else { Serial.println("Image error."); return false; }
  }

  p = finger.image2Tz(2);
  if (p != FINGERPRINT_OK) { Serial.println("image2Tz(2) failed."); return false; }

  p = finger.createModel();
  if (p != FINGERPRINT_OK) { Serial.println("Prints did not match."); return false; }

  p = finger.storeModel(id);
  if (p == FINGERPRINT_OK) {
    Serial.println("Fingerprint enrolled successfully!");
    return true;
  }
  Serial.println("Store model failed.");
  return false;
}
```

**How it works:** The `finger.image2Tz(1)` and `image2Tz(2)` calls convert two separate finger scans into characteristic templates stored in the sensor's two internal buffers, and `createModel()` merges them — requiring two consistent captures is what makes enrollment robust against single-scan noise. During verification, `fingerSearch()` compares a freshly captured template against all stored models on the sensor's onboard flash and returns the matching ID and a confidence score directly, so no fingerprint data ever needs to be processed on the ESP32 itself.

The SG90 servo runs on 5V but its signal line is 3.3V-compatible, so the ESP32 can drive it directly using the LEDC PWM peripheral without a level shifter.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC (Red) | 5V (VIN or external 5V supply) |
| GND (Brown/Black) | GND |
| Signal (Orange/Yellow) | GPIO 18 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +------------------+          +-------------------+
        |      ESP32       |          |    SG90 Servo     |
        |                  |          |                   |
        |         GPIO 18  |--------->| Signal (Orange)   |
        |                  |          |                   |
        |              5V  |--------->| VCC (Red)         |
        |                  |          |                   |
        |             GND  |--------->| GND (Brown)       |
        +------------------+          +-------------------+
```
If powering multiple servos or under heavy load, use an external 5V supply for VCC and share only GND with the ESP32 to avoid brownouts.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// SG90 servo connected to GPIO 18
const int SERVO_PIN   = 18;

// LEDC (PWM) configuration
const int LEDC_CHANNEL    = 0;
const int LEDC_FREQ_HZ    = 50;      // Standard servo frequency: 50 Hz
const int LEDC_RESOLUTION = 16;      // 16-bit resolution for fine control

// SG90 pulse widths in microseconds
// 0°  = ~544 µs, 180° = ~2400 µs
const int PULSE_MIN_US  = 544;
const int PULSE_MAX_US  = 2400;

// Convert a pulse width (µs) to a 16-bit LEDC duty value
uint32_t pulseToDuty(int pulseUs) {
  // Period in µs at 50 Hz = 20000 µs
  // duty = (pulseUs / 20000) * (2^16 - 1)
  return (uint32_t)((pulseUs / 20000.0f) * 65535);
}

// Write an angle (0–180°) to the servo
void setAngle(int angle) {
  angle = constrain(angle, 0, 180);
  int pulseUs = map(angle, 0, 180, PULSE_MIN_US, PULSE_MAX_US);
  ledcWrite(LEDC_CHANNEL, pulseToDuty(pulseUs));
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  // Configure LEDC channel
  ledcSetup(LEDC_CHANNEL, LEDC_FREQ_HZ, LEDC_RESOLUTION);
  ledcAttachPin(SERVO_PIN, LEDC_CHANNEL);

  Serial.println("Servo sweep starting...");
}

void loop() {
  // Sweep from 0° to 180°
  for (int angle = 0; angle <= 180; angle += 1) {
    setAngle(angle);
    delay(15);
  }

  // Sweep back from 180° to 0°
  for (int angle = 180; angle >= 0; angle -= 1) {
    setAngle(angle);
    delay(15);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** Using 16-bit LEDC resolution instead of the typical 8-bit gives much finer duty-cycle steps, which translates to smoother, more precise servo positioning. The `pulseToDuty` function converts a real pulse-width in microseconds directly to the LEDC duty count, keeping the math tied to the servo's actual timing spec rather than relying on library magic.

A passive buzzer requires a PWM or toggled digital signal to produce sound, unlike active buzzers which buzz on DC power alone — so we drive it with a GPIO capable of generating a square wave.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| + (positive) | GPIO 25 |
| - (negative) | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+------------------+          +-------------------+
|      ESP32       |          |   Passive Buzzer  |
|                  |          |                   |
|        GPIO 25  >|--------->|  + (positive)     |
|                  |          |                   |
|            GND  >|--------->|  - (negative)     |
+------------------+          +-------------------+
```
A 100Ω resistor in series with the positive leg is optional but recommended to limit current and protect the GPIO pin.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int BUZZER_PIN = 25;

// Play a tone at the given frequency (Hz) for the given duration (ms)
void playTone(int pin, int frequency, int duration) {
  int halfPeriodUs = 1000000 / (frequency * 2);
  long cycles = (long)frequency * duration / 1000;
  for (long i = 0; i < cycles; i++) {
    digitalWrite(pin, HIGH);
    delayMicroseconds(halfPeriodUs);
    digitalWrite(pin, LOW);
    delayMicroseconds(halfPeriodUs);
  }
}

void setup() {
  pinMode(BUZZER_PIN, OUTPUT);

  // Play a simple melody: C4, E4, G4
  int notes[]    = {262, 330, 392};
  int durations[] = {300, 300, 500};

  for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    playTone(BUZZER_PIN, notes[i], durations[i]);
    delay(100); // short gap between notes
  }

  digitalWrite(BUZZER_PIN, LOW); // ensure buzzer is off after melody
}

void loop() {
  // Nothing to do — melody plays once at startup
}
```

**How it works:** The `playTone` function manually toggles the GPIO pin at a rate matching the desired frequency, generating the square wave that causes the passive buzzer's diaphragm to vibrate and produce sound. The number of cycles is calculated from frequency × duration so the note plays for exactly the requested milliseconds regardless of pitch.

A relay module typically requires a control signal from a GPIO pin, and most 5V relay modules include an onboard optocoupler that accepts a 3.3V logic signal from the ESP32 without level-shifting.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V (VIN) |
| GND | GND |
| IN (Signal) | GPIO 26 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------------+          +-------------------+
|        ESP32         |          |   Relay Module    |
|                      |          |                   |
|    5V (VIN) o--------+----------o VCC               |
|        GND o---------+----------o GND               |
|     GPIO26 o---------+----------o IN (Signal)       |
|                      |          |                   |
|                      |          |  [NO / COM / NC]  |
+----------------------+          +-------------------+
```
Power the relay module from the ESP32's 5V (VIN) pin when USB is connected, since the relay coil typically requires 5V; the IN signal pin is safely driven by 3.3V logic on most optocoupler-equipped modules.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int RELAY_PIN = 26;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(RELAY_PIN, OUTPUT);
  // Most relay modules are active-LOW; start with relay OFF
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, HIGH);
  Serial.println("Relay toggle demo started");
}

void loop() {
  // Turn relay ON (active-LOW: pull signal LOW to energize coil)
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, LOW);
  Serial.println("Relay ON");
  delay(2000);

  // Turn relay OFF
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, HIGH);
  Serial.println("Relay OFF");
  delay(2000);
}
```

**How it works:** Most single-channel relay modules use active-LOW logic through their optocoupler, so writing `LOW` to the IN pin energizes the coil and closes the NO (Normally Open) contact — this is why the pin is initialized `HIGH` in `setup()` to ensure the relay starts in the OFF state.

WS2812 strips use a single-wire timed digital protocol, so only one GPIO is needed for data — but the strip must share a common ground with the ESP32, and a 300–500 ohm series resistor on the data line is strongly recommended to protect against signal ringing.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC (5V) | 5V (external supply recommended for long strips) |
| GND | GND |
| DIN (Data In) | GPIO 5 (via 330Ω series resistor) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +------------------+          +-----------------------------+
        |      ESP32       |          |   WS2812 LED Strip          |
        |                  |          |                             |
        |          GPIO 5  |---[330Ω]---| DIN                       |
        |                  |          |                             |
        |            GND  |----------| GND                         |
        |                  |          |                             |
        +------------------+          | VCC |---[5V External PSU]  |
                                      +-----------------------------+
```
For more than a few LEDs, power the strip VCC from a dedicated 5V supply rather than the ESP32's onboard regulator, and tie that supply's GND to ESP32 GND.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <FastLED.h>

#define LED_PIN     5
#define NUM_LEDS    30
#define LED_TYPE    WS2812
#define COLOR_ORDER GRB
#define BRIGHTNESS  80

CRGB leds[NUM_LEDS];

void setup() {
  FastLED.addLeds<LED_TYPE, LED_PIN, COLOR_ORDER>(leds, NUM_LEDS);
  FastLED.setBrightness(BRIGHTNESS);
}

void loop() {
  // Rainbow wave animation
  static uint8_t hue = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < NUM_LEDS; i++) {
    leds[i] = CHSV(hue + (i * 256 / NUM_LEDS), 255, 255);
  }
  FastLED.show();
  hue++;
  delay(20);
}
```

**How it works:** `FastLED.addLeds<WS2812, LED_PIN, GRB>()` configures the library to bit-bang the WS2812 timing protocol on GPIO 5 with GRB byte order (WS2812s send green before red, unlike most conventions). The `CHSV` color type lets us step through hues with simple integer arithmetic, producing a smooth rainbow wave without any floating-point math.

A 4x4 matrix keypad uses 8 GPIO lines (4 rows driven as outputs, 4 columns read as inputs with pull-ups) so no additional protocol chip or bus is needed — just direct digital I/O.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| R1 (Row 1) | GPIO 13 |
| R2 (Row 2) | GPIO 12 |
| R3 (Row 3) | GPIO 14 |
| R4 (Row 4) | GPIO 27 |
| C1 (Col 1) | GPIO 26 |
| C2 (Col 2) | GPIO 25 |
| C3 (Col 3) | GPIO 33 |
| C4 (Col 4) | GPIO 32 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------------+          +---------------------+
|          ESP32          |          |   4x4 Matrix Keypad |
|                         |          |                     |
|  GPIO13 (Row 1) --------|----------| R1                  |
|  GPIO12 (Row 2) --------|----------| R2                  |
|  GPIO14 (Row 3) --------|----------| R3                  |
|  GPIO27 (Row 4) --------|----------| R4                  |
|                         |          |                     |
|  GPIO26 (Col 1) --------|----------| C1                  |
|  GPIO25 (Col 2) --------|----------| C2                  |
|  GPIO33 (Col 3) --------|----------| C3                  |
|  GPIO32 (Col 4) --------|----------| C4                  |
+-------------------------+          +---------------------+
```

No external resistors are required because the ESP32's internal pull-up resistors are enabled on the column pins in firmware.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// Row pins driven LOW one at a time to scan
const int rowPins[4] = {13, 12, 14, 27};
// Column pins read with internal pull-ups enabled
const int colPins[4] = {26, 25, 33, 32};

// Key map matching physical keypad layout
const char keys[4][4] = {
  {'1', '2', '3', 'A'},
  {'4', '5', '6', 'B'},
  {'7', '8', '9', 'C'},
  {'*', '0', '#', 'D'}
};

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  // Rows as OUTPUT, start HIGH (inactive)
  for (int r = 0; r < 4; r++) {
    pinMode(rowPins[r], OUTPUT);
    digitalWrite(rowPins[r], HIGH);
  }

  // Columns as INPUT with pull-up so undriven cols read HIGH
  for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {
    pinMode(colPins[c], INPUT_PULLUP);
  }
}

char scanKeypad() {
  for (int r = 0; r < 4; r++) {
    // Pull this row LOW to activate it
    digitalWrite(rowPins[r], LOW);

    for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {
      if (digitalRead(colPins[c]) == LOW) {
        // Debounce: wait for release before returning
        delay(20);
        while (digitalRead(colPins[c]) == LOW);
        digitalWrite(rowPins[r], HIGH);
        return keys[r][c];
      }
    }

    // Deactivate row before moving to next
    digitalWrite(rowPins[r], HIGH);
  }
  return 0; // No key pressed
}

void loop() {
  char key = scanKeypad();
  if (key != 0) {
    Serial.print("Key pressed: ");
    Serial.println(key);
  }
  delay(10);
}
```

**How it works:** The scan pulls each row LOW one at a time — because columns are held HIGH by pull-ups, a pressed key creates a LOW-to-LOW path that the `digitalRead` on the column detects as `LOW`, uniquely identifying the row/column intersection. The wait-for-release loop inside the keypress detection prevents the same press from being reported multiple times due to bouncing.

A rotary encoder outputs two quadrature digital signals (CLK and DT) that must be read on interrupt-capable GPIO pins for reliable edge detection, while the push button (SW) uses a standard digital input with internal pull-up.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC (+ or V+) | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| CLK (A) | GPIO 18 |
| DT (B) | GPIO 19 |
| SW (button) | GPIO 21 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +----------------------+          +-------------------------+
        |         ESP32        |          |    Rotary Encoder       |
        |                      |          |   (with push button)    |
        |              GPIO 18 |----------| CLK (A)                 |
        |              GPIO 19 |----------| DT  (B)                 |
        |              GPIO 21 |----------| SW  (button)            |
        |                 3.3V |----------| VCC                     |
        |                  GND |----------| GND                     |
        +----------------------+          +-------------------------+
```
All three signal pins rely on the ESP32's internal pull-up resistors, so no external resistors are needed for this module.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

#define CLK_PIN 18
#define DT_PIN  19
#define SW_PIN  21

volatile int encoderPosition = 0;
volatile bool buttonPressed = false;

int lastCLKState;
unsigned long lastDebounceTime = 0;
const unsigned long debounceDelay = 50;

void IRAM_ATTR handleEncoder() {
  int clkState = digitalRead(CLK_PIN);
  int dtState  = digitalRead(DT_PIN);
  if (clkState != lastCLKState) {
    if (dtState != clkState) {
      encoderPosition++;
    } else {
      encoderPosition--;
    }
  }
  lastCLKState = clkState;
}

void IRAM_ATTR handleButton() {
  unsigned long now = millis();
  if ((now - lastDebounceTime) > debounceDelay) {
    buttonPressed = true;
    lastDebounceTime = now;
  }
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  pinMode(CLK_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);
  pinMode(DT_PIN,  INPUT_PULLUP);
  pinMode(SW_PIN,  INPUT_PULLUP);

  lastCLKState = digitalRead(CLK_PIN);

  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(CLK_PIN), handleEncoder, CHANGE);
  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(SW_PIN),  handleButton,  FALLING);
}

void loop() {
  static int lastPosition = 0;

  if (encoderPosition != lastPosition) {
    Serial.print("Encoder position: ");
    Serial.println(encoderPosition);
    lastPosition = encoderPosition;
  }

  if (buttonPressed) {
    buttonPressed = false;
    Serial.println("Button pressed!");
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The `IRAM_ATTR` attribute places both ISRs in fast IRAM so they execute reliably during interrupts; inside `handleEncoder`, comparing the DT pin state against the CLK state on every CHANGE edge is the classic quadrature decode trick that determines rotation direction without needing a state table.

The potentiometer's wiper (middle pin) connects to one of the ESP32's ADC-capable GPIO pins, with the two outer pins tied to 3.3V and GND to form a simple voltage divider.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| Left (outer) | 3.3V |
| Wiper (middle) | GPIO34 (ADC1_CH6) |
| Right (outer) | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------+          +------------------+
|   ESP32        |          |  Potentiometer   |
|                |          |                  |
|   3.3V o-------+----------o Left (outer)     |
|                |          |                  |
|  GPIO34 o------+----------o Wiper (middle)   |
|                |          |                  |
|    GND o-------+----------o Right (outer)    |
+----------------+          +------------------+
```
Use 3.3V (not 5V) as the reference so the wiper voltage never exceeds the ESP32 ADC's maximum input of 3.3V and avoids damaging the GPIO pin.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int POT_PIN = 34;       // ADC1_CH6 -- input-only, ADC-safe pin
const int ADC_MAX  = 4095;    // ESP32 ADC is 12-bit
const float V_REF  = 3.3f;    // reference voltage

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  // GPIO34 is input-only by default; no pinMode call needed,
  // but setting it explicitly is harmless
  pinMode(POT_PIN, INPUT);
  analogReadResolution(12);   // explicitly set 12-bit resolution
  analogSetAttenuation(ADC_11db); // allows reading up to ~3.3V
}

void loop() {
  int   rawValue  = analogRead(POT_PIN);
  float voltage   = (rawValue / (float)ADC_MAX) * V_REF;
  float percent   = (rawValue / (float)ADC_MAX) * 100.0f;

  Serial.print("Raw: ");
  Serial.print(rawValue);
  Serial.print("  Voltage: ");
  Serial.print(voltage, 3);
  Serial.print(" V  Position: ");
  Serial.print(percent, 1);
  Serial.println("%");

  delay(200);
}
```

**How it works:** `analogSetAttenuation(ADC_11db)` is the critical call — it configures the ESP32's ADC input range to span the full 0–3.3V rather than the default ~1V, so the potentiometer's full sweep maps cleanly across all 4096 ADC steps. GPIO34 is chosen because it is an input-only pin with no internal pull-up/pull-down that could skew the voltage divider reading.

An LDR forms a voltage divider with a fixed resistor so the ESP32's ADC pin sees a voltage proportional to light intensity — no special protocol needed, just an analog read.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| LDR leg 1 | 3.3V |
| LDR leg 2 | GPIO34 (ADC) and one leg of 10kΩ resistor |
| 10kΩ resistor other leg | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +3.3V
          |
        [LDR]
          |
          +--------> GPIO34 (ADC input)
          |
        [10kΩ]
          |
         GND

  +------------------+
  |      ESP32       |
  |                  |
  |  3.3V >----------+--- LDR leg 1
  |                  |
  | GPIO34 >---------+--- LDR leg 2 / top of 10kΩ
  |                  |
  |    GND >---------+--- bottom of 10kΩ
  +------------------+
```
GPIO34 is input-only on the ESP32 and safe for analog reads; avoid GPIO pins above 33 that are tied to internal flash.

**Code**
```cpp
// LDR voltage divider on GPIO34 (ADC1 channel 6)
// LDR between 3.3V and ADC pin; 10kΩ pull-down to GND

const int LDR_PIN = 34;        // ADC-capable, input-only pin
const int ADC_MAX  = 4095;     // ESP32 ADC is 12-bit
const float V_REF  = 3.3f;     // reference voltage

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  // No pinMode needed for analog input on ESP32
  analogReadResolution(12);    // set 12-bit resolution (0-4095)
}

void loop() {
  int   rawValue   = analogRead(LDR_PIN);
  float voltage    = (rawValue / (float)ADC_MAX) * V_REF;
  // Higher voltage → less resistance → brighter light
  int   brightness = map(rawValue, 0, ADC_MAX, 0, 100); // 0-100%

  Serial.print("Raw ADC: ");
  Serial.print(rawValue);
  Serial.print("  Voltage: ");
  Serial.print(voltage, 2);
  Serial.print(" V  Brightness: ");
  Serial.print(brightness);
  Serial.println("%");

  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** The LDR and 10 kΩ resistor form a voltage divider — as light increases the LDR's resistance drops, raising the voltage at GPIO34 and producing a higher ADC reading. Calling `analogReadResolution(12)` ensures the full 12-bit range (0–4095) is used, giving finer light-level granularity than the default 10-bit mode.

Capacitive soil moisture sensors output an analog voltage that varies with soil water content, so we connect the signal pin to one of the ESP32's ADC-capable GPIO pins (GPIO34 is input-only and ideal for analog reads).

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| AOUT (Signal) | GPIO34 (ADC1_CH6) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------------+          +-------------------------+
|          ESP32          |          | Capacitive Soil Sensor  |
|                         |          |                         |
|                    3.3V |----------| VCC                     |
|                     GND |----------| GND                     |
|                  GPIO34 |----------| AOUT                    |
+-------------------------+          +-------------------------+
```
Note: use only 3.3V to power this sensor with an ESP32, as the ADC input pins are not 5V tolerant.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int SENSOR_PIN = 34;       // ADC1 channel, input-only GPIO
const int DRY_VALUE   = 3200;    // raw ADC reading in completely dry soil (calibrate for your sensor)
const int WET_VALUE   = 1200;    // raw ADC reading in fully saturated soil (calibrate for your sensor)

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  // GPIO34 is input-only by default; no pinMode needed, but explicit is fine:
  pinMode(SENSOR_PIN, INPUT);
  analogReadResolution(12);      // ESP32 ADC: 12-bit resolution (0-4095)
  analogSetAttenuation(ADC_11db); // full 0-3.3V input range
  Serial.println("Capacitive Soil Moisture Sensor Ready");
}

void loop() {
  int rawValue = analogRead(SENSOR_PIN);

  // Map raw value to 0-100% moisture (note inverted: lower raw = wetter)
  int moisturePercent = map(rawValue, DRY_VALUE, WET_VALUE, 0, 100);
  moisturePercent = constrain(moisturePercent, 0, 100);

  Serial.print("Raw ADC: ");
  Serial.print(rawValue);
  Serial.print("  |  Moisture: ");
  Serial.print(moisturePercent);
  Serial.println("%");

  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** The ADC reading is inverted relative to moisture — a wetter sensor produces a lower voltage — so `map()` is called with `DRY_VALUE` as the low end and `WET_VALUE` as the high end to flip the scale into an intuitive 0–100% range. `analogSetAttenuation(ADC_11db)` is essential because it extends the ESP32's ADC input range to the full 0–3.3V, preventing the sensor's output from being clipped at the default ~1V ceiling.

The A4988 driver takes two digital control signals (STEP and DIR) from the ESP32, while its motor outputs (1A/1B and 2A/2B) connect directly to the bipolar stepper coils — keeping the motor's higher-current path entirely on the driver side.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VMOT | 8–35V motor supply (external) |
| GND (motor) | Motor supply GND |
| VDD | 3.3V |
| GND (logic) | GND |
| STEP | GPIO 18 |
| DIR | GPIO 19 |
| SLEEP | 3.3V (pulled high to enable) |
| RESET | 3.3V (tied to SLEEP) |
| MS1 | GND (full-step mode) |
| MS2 | GND (full-step mode) |
| MS3 | GND (full-step mode) |
| 1A, 1B | Stepper coil A (two wires) |
| 2A, 2B | Stepper coil B (two wires) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
 +---------------------+         +----------------------+
 |        ESP32        |         |       A4988          |
 |                     |         |                      |
 |           GPIO18 ---+---STEP--+-- STEP               |
 |           GPIO19 ---+---DIR---+-- DIR                |
 |              3.3V --+----+----+-- VDD                |
 |                     |    +----+-- SLEEP              |
 |                     |    +----+-- RESET              |
 |               GND --+----+----+-- GND (logic)        |
 |                     |    +----+-- MS1,MS2,MS3        |
 +---------------------+         |                      |
                                 |   VMOT <-- 12V supply|
                                 |   GND  <-- supply GND|
                                 |                      |
                                 |  1A ]--+  Coil A     |
                                 |  1B ]--+  (stepper)  |
                                 |  2A ]--+  Coil B     |
                                 |  2B ]--+  (stepper)  |
                                 +----------------------+
```
Place a 100µF capacitor across VMOT and GND close to the driver to suppress voltage spikes when the motor coils switch.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int STEP_PIN = 18;
const int DIR_PIN  = 19;

// Number of steps per revolution depends on your motor (typically 200 for 1.8-degree motors)
const int STEPS_PER_REV = 200;

// Delay between step pulses in microseconds (controls speed)
// 2000 µs = ~150 RPM at full step for a 200-step motor
const int STEP_DELAY_US = 2000;

void setup() {
  pinMode(STEP_PIN, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(DIR_PIN,  OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(STEP_PIN, LOW);
  digitalWrite(DIR_PIN,  LOW);  // Initial direction: clockwise
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Serial.println("A4988 Stepper Motor Control Ready");
}

void stepMotor(int steps, bool clockwise, int delayUs) {
  digitalWrite(DIR_PIN, clockwise ? HIGH : LOW);
  // A4988 requires at minimum 1 µs setup time after DIR change before STEP pulse
  delayMicroseconds(5);

  for (int i = 0; i < steps; i++) {
    digitalWrite(STEP_PIN, HIGH);
    delayMicroseconds(10);       // Minimum HIGH pulse width: 1 µs; 10 µs is safe
    digitalWrite(STEP_PIN, LOW);
    delayMicroseconds(delayUs);  // Controls motor speed
  }
}

void loop() {
  Serial.println("One revolution clockwise...");
  stepMotor(STEPS_PER_REV, true, STEP_DELAY_US);
  delay(1000);

  Serial.println("One revolution counter-clockwise...");
  stepMotor(STEPS_PER_REV, false, STEP_DELAY_US);
  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** Each LOW→HIGH→LOW pulse on the STEP pin advances the motor exactly one step, so speed is entirely determined by `delayUs` between pulses — making speed changes as simple as adjusting that single value without any library overhead. The 5 µs guard delay after setting DIR ensures the A4988 has latched the new direction before the first STEP pulse arrives, preventing the driver from stepping the wrong way on direction reversals.

The VS1838B outputs a single digital signal line, so only one GPIO is needed beyond power — choosing a pin with interrupt capability lets us capture precise pulse timings using the IRremote library.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| OUT | GPIO 15 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
  +----------------+          +-------------------+
  |   VS1838B IR   |          |       ESP32       |
  |   Receiver     |          |                   |
  |                |          |                   |
  |   VCC o--------+----------+ 3.3V              |
  |   GND o--------+----------+ GND               |
  |   OUT o--------+----------+ GPIO 15           |
  |                |          |                   |
  +----------------+          +-------------------+
```
The VS1838B runs on 3.3V and is safe to connect directly to the ESP32 — do not power it from 5V on this board as the output logic level would be mismatched.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <IRremote.hpp>

const int IR_RECEIVE_PIN = 15;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Serial.println("IR Receiver Ready");

  // Initialize the IR receiver on the specified pin
  // The third argument enables the built-in LED blink feedback on receive
  IrReceiver.begin(IR_RECEIVE_PIN, ENABLE_LED_FEEDBACK);
}

void loop() {
  if (IrReceiver.decode()) {
    // Print the protocol and decoded command value
    Serial.print("Protocol : ");
    Serial.println(IrReceiver.decodedIRData.protocol);

    Serial.print("Address  : 0x");
    Serial.println(IrReceiver.decodedIRData.address, HEX);

    Serial.print("Command  : 0x");
    Serial.println(IrReceiver.decodedIRData.command, HEX);

    Serial.print("Raw value: 0x");
    Serial.println(IrReceiver.decodedIRData.decodedRawData, HEX);

    // Check if this is a repeat code (button held down)
    if (IrReceiver.decodedIRData.flags & IRDATA_FLAGS_IS_REPEAT) {
      Serial.println("(Repeat code)");
    }

    Serial.println("---");

    // Resume receiver to be ready for the next signal
    IrReceiver.resume();
  }
}
```

**How it works:** `IrReceiver.begin()` sets up a hardware timer interrupt internally to sample the GPIO at microsecond precision, which is why any interrupt-capable pin works — the library handles all the pulse-width measurement automatically. Calling `IrReceiver.resume()` after each decode is essential; without it the library keeps the last result and will never capture a new signal.

