**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <HX711.h>

// Pin definitions
const int DOUT_PIN = 21;
const int SCK_PIN  = 22;

// Calibration factor: adjust this value for your specific load cell
// Positive or negative depending on load cell orientation
float calibrationFactor = -7050.0;

HX711 scale;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Serial.println("HX711 Load Cell Example");

  scale.begin(DOUT_PIN, SCK_PIN);

  Serial.println("Taring scale (remove all weight)...");
  delay(2000);
  scale.tare();  // Reset scale to zero with nothing on it
  Serial.println("Tare complete.");

  scale.set_scale(calibrationFactor);
  Serial.println("Scale ready. Place weight on load cell.");
}

void loop() {
  if (scale.is_ready()) {
    float weight = scale.get_units(10);  // Average of 10 readings
    Serial.print("Weight: ");
    Serial.print(weight, 2);
    Serial.println(" kg");
  } else {
    Serial.println("HX711 not ready");
  }
  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** `scale.tare()` captures the current raw ADC value as the zero-point offset so that subsequent readings return net weight rather than absolute force; `set_scale(calibrationFactor)` then divides every raw reading by that factor to convert raw counts into real-world units — you find the right calibration factor by placing a known weight and tuning until the reported value matches.

The YF-S201 outputs a digital pulse signal whose frequency is proportional to flow rate, so it connects to a single interrupt-capable GPIO on the ESP32 for accurate pulse counting without polling overhead.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC (Red) | 5V (VIN) |
| GND (Black) | GND |
| Signal (Yellow) | GPIO 18 (with 10kΩ pull-up to 3.3V) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +------------------+          +-------------------+
        |      ESP32       |          |   YF-S201 Sensor  |
        |                  |          |                   |
        |          5V/VIN  |----------| VCC (Red)         |
        |              GND |----------| GND (Black)       |
        |                  |          |                   |
        |         GPIO 18  |----+-----| Signal (Yellow)   |
        |                  |   |      +-------------------+
        |           3.3V   |---/
        |                  |  10kΩ
        +------------------+
```
The signal line must be pulled up to 3.3V (not 5V) via a 10kΩ resistor since the ESP32 GPIOs are not 5V tolerant, even though the sensor itself is powered at 5V.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

#define FLOW_SENSOR_PIN 18

// YF-S201 datasheet: ~7.5 pulses per second per liter/minute
// So frequency (Hz) / 7.5 = flow rate in L/min
const float PULSES_PER_LITER = 450.0; // 7.5 pulses/sec * 60 sec = 450 pulses/liter

volatile uint32_t pulseCount = 0;
uint32_t lastMeasureTime = 0;
float totalLiters = 0.0;

void IRAM_ATTR flowPulseISR() {
  pulseCount++;
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(FLOW_SENSOR_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);
  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(FLOW_SENSOR_PIN), flowPulseISR, FALLING);
  lastMeasureTime = millis();
  Serial.println("YF-S201 Water Flow Sensor Ready");
}

void loop() {
  uint32_t currentTime = millis();

  // Calculate every 1 second
  if (currentTime - lastMeasureTime >= 1000) {
    // Disable interrupt while reading shared variable
    detachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(FLOW_SENSOR_PIN));
    uint32_t pulses = pulseCount;
    pulseCount = 0;
    attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(FLOW_SENSOR_PIN), flowPulseISR, FALLING);

    float elapsedSeconds = (currentTime - lastMeasureTime) / 1000.0;
    lastMeasureTime = currentTime;

    // Flow rate in L/min: (pulses per second / 7.5)
    float flowRateLPM = (pulses / elapsedSeconds) / 7.5;

    // Volume this interval in liters
    float intervalLiters = pulses / PULSES_PER_LITER;
    totalLiters += intervalLiters;

    Serial.print("Flow Rate: ");
    Serial.print(flowRateLPM, 2);
    Serial.print(" L/min  |  Total Volume: ");
    Serial.print(totalLiters, 4);
    Serial.println(" L");
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The ISR marked `IRAM_ATTR` runs from internal RAM so it responds instantly to each falling edge pulse without flash access delays; the brief `detachInterrupt`/`reattach` pattern around the read of `pulseCount` prevents a race condition where the ISR could increment the counter mid-read, corrupting the measurement.

The MQ-2 outputs a variable analog voltage proportional to gas concentration, so it connects to one of the ESP32's ADC-capable pins (avoid ADC2 pins when Wi-Fi is active).

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V |
| GND | GND |
| AO (Analog Out) | GPIO34 (ADC1_CH6) |
| DO (Digital Out) | Not connected (optional: any free GPIO) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +------------------+         +-------------------+
        |      MQ-2        |         |       ESP32       |
        |                  |         |                   |
        |  VCC  o----------+-------->o 5V                |
        |  GND  o----------+-------->o GND               |
        |  AO   o----------+-------->o GPIO34 (ADC1_CH6) |
        |  DO   o (unused) |         |                   |
        +------------------+         +-------------------+
```
The MQ-2 heater element requires 5V at up to 150mA, but its analog output signal is typically 0–4V, which is within the ESP32 ADC's safe input range of 0–3.3V — keep readings below full-scale to avoid overvoltage on the pin.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int MQ2_PIN = 34;       // ADC1_CH6, safe to use with Wi-Fi
const int ADC_RESOLUTION = 4095; // ESP32 ADC is 12-bit
const float VCC = 3.3f;

// MQ-2 load resistance on board (typically 10k ohm)
const float RL = 10.0f;

// Sensor resistance in clean air (calibrate empirically; ~9.8 typical)
const float R0 = 9.8f;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  analogReadResolution(12); // Set ESP32 ADC to 12-bit
  Serial.println("MQ-2 Gas Sensor Initialized");
  Serial.println("Warming up sensor (30 seconds)...");
  delay(30000); // Allow sensor heater to stabilize
}

float readRsOverR0() {
  int rawADC = analogRead(MQ2_PIN);
  float voltage = (rawADC / (float)ADC_RESOLUTION) * VCC;

  // Avoid division by zero
  if (voltage < 0.01f) return -1.0f;

  // RS = ((VCC - Vout) / Vout) * RL
  float rs = ((VCC - voltage) / voltage) * RL;
  return rs / R0;
}

void loop() {
  int rawValue = analogRead(MQ2_PIN);
  float voltage = (rawValue / (float)ADC_RESOLUTION) * VCC;
  float rsR0ratio = readRsOverR0();

  Serial.print("Raw ADC: ");
  Serial.print(rawValue);
  Serial.print("  |  Voltage: ");
  Serial.print(voltage, 3);
  Serial.print(" V  |  RS/R0: ");

  if (rsR0ratio >= 0) {
    Serial.print(rsR0ratio, 3);
    if (rsR0ratio < 1.5f) {
      Serial.println("  --> HIGH GAS CONCENTRATION DETECTED!");
    } else {
      Serial.println("  --> Air quality OK");
    }
  } else {
    Serial.println("N/A (sensor output too low)");
  }

  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** The RS/R0 ratio is the key measurement — RS is the sensor's resistance calculated from the output voltage divider, and dividing by the baseline clean-air resistance R0 gives a normalized value that drops toward or below 1.0 as gas concentration rises. The 30-second warm-up delay in `setup()` is critical because the MQ-2's heater must reach operating temperature before its readings become meaningful.

The flame sensor module's digital output pin goes directly to a GPIO since it outputs a clean logic-level signal, making a dedicated interrupt pin ideal for fast fire detection.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| DO (Digital Out) | GPIO 23 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+---------------------+          +----------------------+
|      ESP32          |          |   Flame Sensor Module|
|                     |          |                      |
|              3.3V  |----------| VCC                  |
|               GND  |----------| GND                  |
|           GPIO 23  |----------| DO                   |
+---------------------+          +----------------------+
```
Most flame sensor modules operate at 3.3V and are safe to use directly with ESP32 GPIO pins — do not connect VCC to 5V unless the module datasheet explicitly supports it.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int FLAME_PIN = 23;
const int LED_PIN   = 2;   // onboard LED for visual alert

void IRAM_ATTR flameISR() {
  // Interrupt fires when flame is detected (DO goes LOW on most modules)
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH);
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(FLAME_PIN, INPUT);
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);

  // Attach interrupt on falling edge (DO pulls LOW when flame detected)
  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(FLAME_PIN), flameISR, FALLING);

  Serial.println("Flame sensor ready. Monitoring for fire...");
}

void loop() {
  int flameState = digitalRead(FLAME_PIN);

  if (flameState == LOW) {
    Serial.println("WARNING: Flame detected!");
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH);
  } else {
    Serial.println("No flame detected.");
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  }

  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** The interrupt on the FALLING edge gives an immediate hardware-level response the instant the sensor pulls DO LOW, while the `loop()` polling provides a readable status update every 500 ms — the `IRAM_ATTR` attribute ensures the ISR is stored in fast IRAM so it executes reliably without flash latency.

