**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int TOUCH_PIN = 4;
bool lastState = LOW;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(TOUCH_PIN, INPUT);
  Serial.println("TTP223 Capacitive Touch Sensor Ready");
}

void loop() {
  bool currentState = digitalRead(TOUCH_PIN);

  if (currentState != lastState) {
    if (currentState == HIGH) {
      Serial.println("Touch detected!");
    } else {
      Serial.println("Touch released.");
    }
    lastState = currentState;
  }

  delay(20); // simple debounce delay
}
```

**How it works:** The sketch tracks the previous sensor state in `lastState` and only prints a message when the state changes, giving clean edge-triggered events rather than flooding the serial monitor while the pad is held. A 20 ms polling delay acts as a lightweight debounce since the TTP223 already has built-in capacitive filtering, but avoids CPU-spinning in a tight loop.

A small vibration motor draws more current than an ESP32 GPIO can safely supply, so an NPN transistor (e.g. 2N2222 or PN2222) is used as a low-side switch, with a flyback diode across the motor to suppress voltage spikes when switching.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| Motor (+) lead | 5V (or 3.3V) |
| Motor (−) lead | Transistor Collector |
| Flyback diode anode | Motor (−) lead / Collector |
| Flyback diode cathode | Motor (+) lead / 5V |
| Transistor Base (via 1kΩ resistor) | GPIO 18 |
| Transistor Emitter | GND |
| 1kΩ resistor other end | GPIO 18 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
  +---------------------+          +-------------------+
  |        ESP32        |          |  Vibration Motor  |
  |                     |          |   (−)      (+)    |
  |           GPIO 18 o-+--[1kΩ]--+-->Base      |      |
  |                     |          | NPN 2N2222  +------+---> 5V
  |               GND o-+----------+-->Emitter   |
  +---------------------+          |   Collector-+--[D1 1N4148, cathode to 5V]
                                   +-------------------+

  Motor (+) -----> 5V rail
  Motor (−) -----> Collector of NPN
  1N4148 diode: anode at Collector/Motor(−), cathode at Motor(+)/5V
```
Always include the flyback (freewheeling) diode across the motor terminals to protect the transistor from inductive kickback when the motor is switched off.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int MOTOR_PIN = 18;  // Controls NPN transistor base via 1kΩ resistor

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(MOTOR_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(MOTOR_PIN, LOW);  // Ensure motor is off at startup
}

void loop() {
  // Buzz motor for 500ms
  Serial.println("Motor ON");
  digitalWrite(MOTOR_PIN, HIGH);
  delay(500);

  // Stop motor for 1 second
  Serial.println("Motor OFF");
  digitalWrite(MOTOR_PIN, LOW);
  delay(1000);

  // Short double-buzz pattern
  for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
    digitalWrite(MOTOR_PIN, HIGH);
    delay(100);
    digitalWrite(MOTOR_PIN, LOW);
    delay(150);
  }

  delay(1500);  // Pause before repeating
}
```

**How it works:** Writing `HIGH` to GPIO 18 forward-biases the NPN transistor's base through the 1kΩ current-limiting resistor, saturating the transistor and completing the motor's ground path — the ESP32 GPIO never carries the motor's load current directly. The flyback diode is critical because inductive motors generate a reverse-polarity voltage spike the instant current is interrupted, which would otherwise destroy the transistor.

The PN532 requires its interface selection pins (HSU/I2C/SPI mode pins) to be set correctly before powering on, and I2C mode is chosen here because it only needs two signal lines shared with other peripherals on the bus.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | GPIO 21 |
| SCL | GPIO 22 |
| IRQ | GPIO 4 |
| RSTO | GPIO 5 |
| SEL0 (HSU/I2C sel) | GND (sets I2C mode) |
| SEL1 (HSU/I2C sel) | 3.3V (sets I2C mode) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +----------------+          +-------------------+
        |     ESP32      |          |   PN532 NFC       |
        |                |          |                   |
        |    3.3V  o-----+----------+o VCC              |
        |     GND  o-----+----------+o GND              |
        |  GPIO 21 o-----+----------+o SDA              |
        |  GPIO 22 o-----+----------+o SCL              |
        |   GPIO 4 o-----+----------+o IRQ              |
        |   GPIO 5 o-----+----------+o RSTO             |
        |                |          |o SEL0 ---[GND]    |
        |                |          |o SEL1 ---[3.3V]   |
        +----------------+          +-------------------+
```
The PN532 runs on 3.3V and is not 5V tolerant on its logic pins, so do not connect it to 5V.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_PN532.h>

// IRQ and RESET pins
#define PN532_IRQ   4
#define PN532_RESET 5

// I2C SDA=21, SCL=22 are ESP32 defaults used by Wire automatically
Adafruit_PN532 nfc(PN532_IRQ, PN532_RESET);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Serial.println("PN532 NFC Reader - I2C");

  Wire.begin(21, 22); // SDA, SCL
  nfc.begin();

  uint32_t versiondata = nfc.getFirmwareVersion();
  if (!versiondata) {
    Serial.println("Didn't find PN532 board. Check wiring!");
    while (1); // halt
  }

  Serial.print("Found chip PN5");
  Serial.println((versiondata >> 24) & 0xFF, HEX);
  Serial.print("Firmware ver. ");
  Serial.print((versiondata >> 16) & 0xFF, DEC);
  Serial.print('.');
  Serial.println((versiondata >> 8) & 0xFF, DEC);

  // Configure to read RFID tags
  nfc.SAMConfig();
  Serial.println("Waiting for an NFC card...");
}

void loop() {
  uint8_t success;
  uint8_t uid[7];     // Buffer to store the returned UID
  uint8_t uidLength;  // Length of the UID (4 or 7 bytes for Mifare)

  // Wait up to 1 second for a card
  success = nfc.readPassiveTargetID(PN532_MIFARE_ISO14443A, uid, &uidLength, 1000);

  if (success) {
    Serial.print("Found a card! UID Length: ");
    Serial.print(uidLength, DEC);
    Serial.println(" bytes");
    Serial.print("UID Value: ");
    for (uint8_t i = 0; i < uidLength; i++) {
      if (uid[i] < 0x10) Serial.print("0"); // leading zero
      Serial.print(uid[i], HEX);
      Serial.print(" ");
    }
    Serial.println();
    delay(1000); // debounce: wait before reading again
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The `nfc.SAMConfig()` call puts the PN532's Security Access Module into normal mode, which is a mandatory initialization step before any card reads will work. `readPassiveTargetID` uses the IRQ pin internally to signal when a card is detected rather than polling over I2C continuously, which keeps the bus efficient and avoids missed detections.

Both SG90 servos share power and ground rails while each gets its own dedicated PWM-capable GPIO pin, since the ESP32's LEDC peripheral can drive servo-standard 50 Hz signals on nearly any output pin.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| Pan Servo VCC | 5V (Vin) |
| Pan Servo GND | GND |
| Pan Servo Signal | GPIO 13 |
| Tilt Servo VCC | 5V (Vin) |
| Tilt Servo GND | GND |
| Tilt Servo Signal | GPIO 12 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +------------------+          +-------------------+
        |     ESP32        |          |  Pan SG90 Servo   |
        |                  |          |                   |
        |   GPIO 13 PWM |--+--------->| Signal (Orange)   |
        |                  |          | VCC (Red)    <----+--+
        |         5V/Vin|--+----------+                   |  |
        |                  |          | GND (Brown)  <----+--+--+
        |            GND|--+-----+    +-------------------+  |  |
        |                  |     |                           |  |
        |   GPIO 12 PWM |--+-+   |    +-------------------+  |  |
        |                  | |   |    | Tilt SG90 Servo   |  |  |
        |                  | +-->+--->| Signal (Orange)   |  |  |
        +------------------+     |    | VCC (Red)    <----+--+  |
                                 |    |                   |     |
                                 |    | GND (Brown)  <----+-----+
                                 +----+                   |
                                      +-------------------+
```
SG90 servos can draw up to 500 mA stall current each, so power them from the ESP32's 5V/Vin pin (sourced from USB) rather than the 3.3V rail to avoid brownouts.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// LEDC PWM settings
const int PAN_PIN   = 13;
const int TILT_PIN  = 12;

const int PAN_CHANNEL  = 0;
const int TILT_CHANNEL = 1;

const int PWM_FREQ       = 50;   // 50 Hz for standard servos
const int PWM_RESOLUTION = 16;   // 16-bit resolution (0-65535)

// SG90 pulse width: ~500us (0 deg) to ~2400us (180 deg) at 50Hz period = 20ms
// duty = (pulse_us / 20000) * 65535
const int SERVO_MIN_DUTY = (int)((500.0  / 20000.0) * 65535);  // ~1638
const int SERVO_MAX_DUTY = (int)((2400.0 / 20000.0) * 65535);  // ~7864

// Map angle (0-180) to LEDC duty count
int angleToDuty(int angle) {
  return map(angle, 0, 180, SERVO_MIN_DUTY, SERVO_MAX_DUTY);
}

void setServo(int channel, int angle) {
  angle = constrain(angle, 0, 180);
  ledcWrite(channel, angleToDuty(angle));
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  // Configure LEDC channels for each servo
  ledcSetup(PAN_CHANNEL,  PWM_FREQ, PWM_RESOLUTION);
  ledcSetup(TILT_CHANNEL, PWM_FREQ, PWM_RESOLUTION);

  ledcAttachPin(PAN_PIN,  PAN_CHANNEL);
  ledcAttachPin(TILT_PIN, TILT_CHANNEL);

  // Centre both servos on startup
  setServo(PAN_CHANNEL,  90);
  setServo(TILT_CHANNEL, 90);
  delay(500);
}

void loop() {
  // Sweep pan left to right
  for (int angle = 0; angle <= 180; angle += 5) {
    setServo(PAN_CHANNEL, angle);
    delay(30);
  }
  // Sweep tilt up then down
  for (int angle = 0; angle <= 90; angle += 5) {
    setServo(TILT_CHANNEL, angle);
    delay(30);
  }
  for (int angle = 90; angle >= 0; angle -= 5) {
    setServo(TILT_CHANNEL, angle);
    delay(30);
  }
  // Sweep pan right to left
  for (int angle = 180; angle >= 0; angle -= 5) {
    setServo(PAN_CHANNEL, angle);
    delay(30);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The ESP32 lacks hardware PWM timers in the Arduino Servo library sense, so `ledcSetup` / `ledcWrite` from the LEDC peripheral are used instead — 16-bit resolution at 50 Hz gives fine enough granularity (~0.3 µs per step) to position the SG90 accurately without jitter. The `angleToDuty` function converts a human-readable 0–180° value into the corresponding 16-bit duty count by scaling between the measured 500 µs and 2400 µs pulse widths for this specific servo model.

The L298N accepts 5V logic signals and its onboard 5V regulator can supply the ESP32, but the ESP32 GPIO outputs 3.3V — fortunately the L298N input threshold is low enough that 3.3V logic reliably drives it.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| L298N IN1 | GPIO 25 |
| L298N IN2 | GPIO 26 |
| L298N ENA | GPIO 27 (PWM) |
| L298N GND | GND |
| L298N +5V | 5V (Vin) |
| L298N OUT1 | DC Motor terminal A |
| L298N OUT2 | DC Motor terminal B |
| L298N +12V | External motor supply (7–12V) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
 +---------------------+         +-------------------+
 |        ESP32        |         |      L298N        |
 |                     |         |                   |
 |      GPIO25 (IN1)---|-------->| IN1               |
 |      GPIO26 (IN2)---|-------->| IN2               |
 |      GPIO27 (ENA)---|-------->| ENA               |
 |                     |         |                   |
 |         GND |-------+---------| GND               |
 |         5V  |---------------->| +5V               |
 |                     |         |                   |
 |                     |         | +12V |<---- [Ext 12V Supply]
 |                     |         |                   |
 |                     |         | OUT1 |---+
 |                     |         | OUT2 |---+----[ DC Motor ]
 +---------------------+         +-------------------+
```
Ensure the external motor power supply ground is shared with the L298N GND and ESP32 GND for a common reference.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// L298N control pins
const int IN1 = 25;
const int IN2 = 26;
const int ENA = 27;

// PWM settings
const int pwmChannel = 0;
const int pwmFreq    = 1000;  // 1 kHz
const int pwmRes     = 8;     // 8-bit resolution (0–255)

void motorForward(int speed) {
  digitalWrite(IN1, HIGH);
  digitalWrite(IN2, LOW);
  ledcWrite(pwmChannel, speed);
}

void motorBackward(int speed) {
  digitalWrite(IN1, LOW);
  digitalWrite(IN2, HIGH);
  ledcWrite(pwmChannel, speed);
}

void motorStop() {
  digitalWrite(IN1, LOW);
  digitalWrite(IN2, LOW);
  ledcWrite(pwmChannel, 0);
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  pinMode(IN1, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(IN2, OUTPUT);

  // Configure LEDC PWM on ENA pin
  ledcSetup(pwmChannel, pwmFreq, pwmRes);
  ledcAttachPin(ENA, pwmChannel);

  motorStop();
}

void loop() {
  Serial.println("Forward at 75% speed");
  motorForward(191);   // 191/255 ≈ 75%
  delay(3000);

  Serial.println("Stop");
  motorStop();
  delay(1000);

  Serial.println("Backward at 50% speed");
  motorBackward(127);  // 127/255 ≈ 50%
  delay(3000);

  Serial.println("Stop");
  motorStop();
  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** The ESP32's `ledcWrite` drives the L298N's ENA pin with a PWM signal to set motor speed via duty cycle (0–255), while IN1/IN2 logic levels determine rotation direction — HIGH/LOW spins forward, LOW/HIGH spins backward, and both LOW coasts the motor to a stop.

Each HC-SR04 needs its own TRIG and ECHO pin pair since they operate independently over a simple digital pulse interface — sharing trigger lines would make it impossible to fire them individually.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| Sensor 1 VCC | 5V |
| Sensor 1 GND | GND |
| Sensor 1 TRIG | GPIO 5 |
| Sensor 1 ECHO | GPIO 18 |
| Sensor 2 VCC | 5V |
| Sensor 2 GND | GND |
| Sensor 2 TRIG | GPIO 19 |
| Sensor 2 ECHO | GPIO 21 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------------+          +-------------------+
|          ESP32          |          |  HC-SR04 Sensor 1 |
|                         |          |                   |
|          GPIO5  (TRIG1)-|--------->| TRIG              |
|         GPIO18  (ECHO1)-|<---------| ECHO              |
|                     5V  |--------->| VCC               |
|                    GND  |--------->| GND               |
|                         |          +-------------------+
|                         |
|                         |          +-------------------+
|                         |          |  HC-SR04 Sensor 2 |
|                         |          |                   |
|         GPIO19  (TRIG2)-|--------->| TRIG              |
|         GPIO21  (ECHO2)-|<---------| ECHO              |
|                     5V  |--------->| VCC               |
|                    GND  |--------->| GND               |
+-------------------------+          +-------------------+
```

The HC-SR04 ECHO pin outputs 5V logic, but ESP32 GPIO inputs are only 3.3V tolerant — in practice many setups work at short distances, but for reliability consider a voltage divider (e.g. 1kΩ and 2kΩ) on each ECHO line.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// Sensor 1 pins
const int TRIG1 = 5;
const int ECHO1 = 18;

// Sensor 2 pins
const int TRIG2 = 19;
const int ECHO2 = 21;

const long TIMEOUT_US = 30000; // ~5 meter max range timeout

float readDistanceCm(int trigPin, int echoPin) {
  // Send a 10us HIGH pulse on TRIG
  digitalWrite(trigPin, LOW);
  delayMicroseconds(2);
  digitalWrite(trigPin, HIGH);
  delayMicroseconds(10);
  digitalWrite(trigPin, LOW);

  // Measure pulse duration on ECHO
  long duration = pulseIn(echoPin, HIGH, TIMEOUT_US);

  if (duration == 0) {
    return -1.0; // timeout / no object detected
  }

  // Speed of sound = 343 m/s = 0.0343 cm/us
  // Distance = (duration / 2) * 0.0343
  return (duration / 2.0) * 0.0343;
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  pinMode(TRIG1, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(ECHO1, INPUT);
  pinMode(TRIG2, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(ECHO2, INPUT);

  Serial.println("Dual HC-SR04 Distance Sensor Ready");
}

void loop() {
  float dist1 = readDistanceCm(TRIG1, ECHO1);
  delay(10); // brief pause between sensors to avoid acoustic crosstalk
  float dist2 = readDistanceCm(TRIG2, ECHO2);

  Serial.print("Sensor 1: ");
  if (dist1 < 0) {
    Serial.print("Out of range");
  } else {
    Serial.print(dist1, 1);
    Serial.print(" cm");
  }

  Serial.print("  |  Sensor 2: ");
  if (dist2 < 0) {
    Serial.print("Out of range");
  } else {
    Serial.print(dist2, 1);
    Serial.print(" cm");
  }

  Serial.println();
  delay(200);
}
```

**How it works:** The `pulseIn()` call with a timeout prevents the loop from hanging indefinitely if no echo returns (object too far or absent), returning 0 which is mapped to -1 cm as a sentinel value. The 10 ms delay between firing the two sensors prevents one sensor's residual ultrasonic burst from being picked up by the other sensor's ECHO pin (acoustic crosstalk).

E-paper displays use SPI with two extra control lines (DC and BUSY) in addition to the standard CS and RST pins, so plan for five GPIOs beyond the shared SPI bus.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SCK | GPIO 18 (SPI SCK) |
| MOSI | GPIO 23 (SPI MOSI) |
| CS | GPIO 5 |
| DC | GPIO 17 |
| RST | GPIO 16 |
| BUSY | GPIO 4 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
 +---------------------------+         +--------------------+
 |          ESP32            |         | 2.9" e-Paper (SPI) |
 |                           |         |                    |
 |  3.3V -------------------|---------|--- VCC             |
 |  GND  -------------------|---------|--- GND             |
 |  GPIO18 (SCK)  ----------|---------|--- SCK             |
 |  GPIO23 (MOSI) ----------|---------|--- MOSI            |
 |  GPIO5  (CS)   ----------|---------|--- CS              |
 |  GPIO17 (DC)   ----------|---------|--- DC              |
 |  GPIO16 (RST)  ----------|---------|--- RST             |
 |  GPIO4  (BUSY) ----------|---------|--- BUSY            |
 +---------------------------+         +--------------------+
```
E-paper displays run on 3.3V logic only — do not connect any pin to 5V or you risk damaging the display controller.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <SPI.h>
#include <GxEPD2_BW.h>
#include <Fonts/FreeMonoBold9pt7b.h>

// Pin definitions
#define EPD_CS   5
#define EPD_DC   17
#define EPD_RST  16
#define EPD_BUSY 4

// GxEPD2 display object for a common 2.9" 296x128 display (GDEH029A1 / similar)
// Adjust the display class if your panel uses a different driver IC
GxEPD2_BW<GxEPD2_290, GxEPD2_290::HEIGHT> display(
  GxEPD2_290(EPD_CS, EPD_DC, EPD_RST, EPD_BUSY)
);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  // Initialize the display (performs hardware reset and full init sequence)
  display.init(115200);

  // Set rotation: 1 = landscape
  display.setRotation(1);

  // Clear to white
  display.clearScreen();

  // --- Draw "Hello, e-Paper!" centered on the screen ---
  display.setFont(&FreeMonoBold9pt7b);
  display.setTextColor(GxEPD_BLACK);

  // Use a paged drawing callback to save RAM
  display.setFullWindow();
  display.firstPage();
  do {
    display.fillScreen(GxEPD_WHITE);
    int16_t tbx, tby;
    uint16_t tbw, tbh;
    const char* msg = "Hello, e-Paper!";
    display.getTextBounds(msg, 0, 0, &tbx, &tby, &tbw, &tbh);
    uint16_t x = (display.width()  - tbw) / 2 - tbx;
    uint16_t y = (display.height() + tbh) / 2 - tby;
    display.setCursor(x, y);
    display.print(msg);
  } while (display.nextPage());

  // Power down the display panel to prevent burn-in
  display.powerOff();
  Serial.println("Display update complete.");
}

void loop() {
  // E-paper only needs to refresh when content changes; nothing to do here
}
```

**How it works:** The GxEPD2 library's paged drawing loop (`firstPage` / `nextPage`) redraws the framebuffer in small chunks so the ESP32's limited RAM is never overwhelmed by a full-panel buffer. Calling `display.powerOff()` after the refresh is essential for e-paper longevity — leaving the panel powered with a static image causes unnecessary stress to the electrophoretic elements.

A common cathode RGB LED needs three separate PWM-capable GPIO pins — one per color channel — with current-limiting resistors on each anode to protect the LED from the ESP32's 3.3V output.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| R (Red anode) | GPIO 25 via 100Ω resistor |
| G (Green anode) | GPIO 26 via 100Ω resistor |
| B (Blue anode) | GPIO 27 via 100Ω resistor |
| Cathode (common) | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------+          +-------------------+
|   ESP32        |          |  RGB LED (CC)     |
|                |          |                   |
|   GPIO25 ------+--[100Ω]--+-- R (Red)         |
|                |          |                   |
|   GPIO26 ------+--[100Ω]--+-- G (Green)       |
|                |          |                   |
|   GPIO27 ------+--[100Ω]--+-- B (Blue)        |
|                |          |                   |
|      GND ------+----------+-- Cathode         |
+----------------+          +-------------------+
```
Use at least 100Ω resistors on each channel; with 3.3V and a typical LED forward voltage of ~2V, this limits current to a safe ~13mA per channel.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// GPIO pins for each color channel
const int PIN_RED   = 25;
const int PIN_GREEN = 26;
const int PIN_BLUE  = 27;

// LEDC PWM channels (ESP32 has 16 hardware PWM channels)
const int CH_RED   = 0;
const int CH_GREEN = 1;
const int CH_BLUE  = 2;

const int PWM_FREQ       = 5000;  // 5 kHz
const int PWM_RESOLUTION = 8;     // 8-bit: values 0-255

void setColor(uint8_t r, uint8_t g, uint8_t b) {
  ledcWrite(CH_RED,   r);
  ledcWrite(CH_GREEN, g);
  ledcWrite(CH_BLUE,  b);
}

void setup() {
  // Attach each PWM channel to its GPIO
  ledcSetup(CH_RED,   PWM_FREQ, PWM_RESOLUTION);
  ledcSetup(CH_GREEN, PWM_FREQ, PWM_RESOLUTION);
  ledcSetup(CH_BLUE,  PWM_FREQ, PWM_RESOLUTION);

  ledcAttachPin(PIN_RED,   CH_RED);
  ledcAttachPin(PIN_GREEN, CH_GREEN);
  ledcAttachPin(PIN_BLUE,  CH_BLUE);
}

void loop() {
  // Cycle through some example colors
  setColor(255, 0,   0);    delay(1000); // Red
  setColor(0,   255, 0);    delay(1000); // Green
  setColor(0,   0,   255);  delay(1000); // Blue
  setColor(255, 255, 0);    delay(1000); // Yellow
  setColor(0,   255, 255);  delay(1000); // Cyan
  setColor(255, 0,   255);  delay(1000); // Magenta
  setColor(255, 255, 255);  delay(1000); // White

  // Smooth red fade
  for (int brightness = 0; brightness <= 255; brightness++) {
    setColor(brightness, 0, 0);
    delay(8);
  }
  for (int brightness = 255; brightness >= 0; brightness--) {
    setColor(brightness, 0, 0);
    delay(8);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The ESP32's `ledcSetup` / `ledcAttachPin` API maps independent hardware PWM timers (channels 0–2) to each GPIO, so all three color channels can be driven simultaneously at different duty cycles without software overhead. Passing an 8-bit value (0–255) to `ledcWrite` sets the duty cycle proportionally, allowing any RGB color to be mixed by blending the three channel intensities.

The MPU6050 is an I2C device, so it connects to the M5Stack Core2's Port A Grove connector (GPIO32/GPIO33) rather than the internal I2C bus reserved for the AXP192 and touch controller.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to M5Stack Core2 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V (Grove Port A) |
| GND | GND (Grove Port A) |
| SDA | GPIO32 (Port A SDA) |
| SCL | GPIO33 (Port A SCL) |
| AD0 | GND (sets I2C address to 0x68) |
| INT | Not connected (optional) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------------+          +-------------------+
|      M5Stack Core2      |          |     MPU6050       |
|                         |          |                   |
|  Port A Grove           |          |                   |
|  [3.3V] ---------------+----------+-- VCC             |
|  [GND]  ---------------+----------+-- GND             |
|  [GPIO32 SDA] ---------+----------+-- SDA             |
|  [GPIO33 SCL] ---------+----------+-- SCL             |
|                         |          |                   |
|  (internal I2C:         |          |-- AD0 --- GND     |
|   GPIO21/22 reserved)   |          |   (addr=0x68)     |
+-------------------------+          +-------------------+
```

The MPU6050 operates at 3.3V logic and is compatible with the Core2's GPIO levels; do not connect VCC to 5V as sustained 5V may stress the module depending on the board variant.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <M5Core2.h>
#include <Wire.h>

// MPU6050 I2C address with AD0 tied to GND
#define MPU6050_ADDR 0x68

// Register addresses
#define REG_PWR_MGMT_1  0x6B
#define REG_ACCEL_XOUT_H 0x3B
#define REG_GYRO_XOUT_H  0x43

void writeRegister(uint8_t reg, uint8_t value) {
  Wire.beginTransmission(MPU6050_ADDR);
  Wire.write(reg);
  Wire.write(value);
  Wire.endTransmission();
}

int16_t readWord(uint8_t reg) {
  Wire.beginTransmission(MPU6050_ADDR);
  Wire.write(reg);
  Wire.endTransmission(false);
  Wire.requestFrom(MPU6050_ADDR, 2);
  int16_t val = (Wire.read() << 8) | Wire.read();
  return val;
}

void setup() {
  // Initialize M5Stack Core2 (also starts Serial)
  M5.begin();

  // Start I2C on Port A pins (GPIO32=SDA, GPIO33=SCL)
  Wire.begin(32, 33);

  // Wake MPU6050 from sleep mode (default is sleep on power-up)
  writeRegister(REG_PWR_MGMT_1, 0x00);
  delay(100);

  M5.Lcd.setTextSize(2);
  M5.Lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
  M5.Lcd.println("MPU6050 Ready");
  Serial.println("MPU6050 initialized");
}

void loop() {
  // Read accelerometer (raw, ±2g default, LSB = 16384/g)
  int16_t ax = readWord(REG_ACCEL_XOUT_H);
  int16_t ay = readWord(REG_ACCEL_XOUT_H + 2);
  int16_t az = readWord(REG_ACCEL_XOUT_H + 4);

  // Read gyroscope (raw, ±250°/s default, LSB = 131 LSB/°/s)
  int16_t gx = readWord(REG_GYRO_XOUT_H);
  int16_t gy = readWord(REG_GYRO_XOUT_H + 2);
  int16_t gz = readWord(REG_GYRO_XOUT_H + 4);

  // Convert to physical units
  float accelX = ax / 16384.0f;
  float accelY = ay / 16384.0f;
  float accelZ = az / 16384.0f;
  float gyroX  = gx / 131.0f;
  float gyroY  = gy / 131.0f;
  float gyroZ  = gz / 131.0f;

  // Display on LCD
  M5.Lcd.fillScreen(BLACK);
  M5.Lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
  M5.Lcd.setTextSize(2);
  M5.Lcd.printf("Accel (g):\n");
  M5.Lcd.printf(" X: %.2f\n", accelX);
  M5.Lcd.printf(" Y: %.2f\n", accelY);
  M5.Lcd.printf(" Z: %.2f\n", accelZ);
  M5.Lcd.printf("Gyro (d/s):\n");
  M5.Lcd.printf(" X: %.1f\n", gyroX);
  M5.Lcd.printf(" Y: %.1f\n", gyroY);
  M5.Lcd.printf(" Z: %.1f\n", gyroZ);

  // Also print to Serial
  Serial.printf("Accel: %.2f, %.2f, %.2f g | Gyro: %.1f, %.1f, %.1f deg/s\n",
                accelX, accelY, accelZ, gyroX, gyroY, gyroZ);

  M5.update();
  delay(200);
}
```

**How it works:** Writing `0x00` to the `PWR_MGMT_1` register is critical because the MPU6050 powers up in sleep mode by default and will return zeros for all sensor data until explicitly woken. The `Wire.begin(32, 33)` call overrides the default I2C pins to use Port A, keeping the internal bus on GPIO21/22 free for the Core2's power and touch ICs.

Port B on the M5Stack Core2 exposes GPIO26 (a DAC-capable, PWM-capable pin) and GPIO36 (input-only), so GPIO26 is the natural choice for the servo's PWM signal line.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to M5Stack Core2 |
|---|---|
| SG90 Red (VCC) | 5V (Grove Port B) |
| SG90 Brown (GND) | GND (Grove Port B) |
| SG90 Orange (Signal) | GPIO26 (Port B pin2) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
|   M5Stack Core2   |          |    SG90 Servo     |
|                   |          |                   |
|  Port B           |          |                   |
|  5V       o-------+----------+o VCC  (Red)       |
|  GND      o-------+----------+o GND  (Brown)     |
|  GPIO26   o-------+----------+o Signal (Orange)  |
|  (GPIO36) |          |          |                   |
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
```
Note: The SG90 can draw surge current on startup; if the servo causes instability, power it from an external 5V supply sharing a common GND with the Core2 instead of drawing from the Grove port.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <M5Core2.h>
#include <ESP32Servo.h>

// SG90 signal wire connected to Port B GPIO26
const int SERVO_PIN = 26;

Servo myServo;

void setup() {
    M5.begin();
    M5.Lcd.setTextSize(2);
    M5.Lcd.setCursor(10, 10);
    M5.Lcd.println("SG90 Servo Demo");

    // Allocate a hardware timer channel for the servo on GPIO26
    ESP32PWM::allocateTimer(0);
    myServo.setPeriodHertz(50);          // Standard 50 Hz PWM for SG90
    myServo.attach(SERVO_PIN, 500, 2400); // Min/max pulse width in microseconds
}

void loop() {
    // Sweep from 0 to 180 degrees
    for (int angle = 0; angle <= 180; angle += 5) {
        myServo.write(angle);
        M5.Lcd.fillRect(10, 40, 300, 30, BLACK);
        M5.Lcd.setCursor(10, 40);
        M5.Lcd.printf("Angle: %d deg", angle);
        delay(20);
    }
    // Sweep back from 180 to 0 degrees
    for (int angle = 180; angle >= 0; angle -= 5) {
        myServo.write(angle);
        M5.Lcd.fillRect(10, 40, 300, 30, BLACK);
        M5.Lcd.setCursor(10, 40);
        M5.Lcd.printf("Angle: %d deg", angle);
        delay(20);
    }
}
```

**How it works:** The `ESP32Servo` library uses one of the ESP32's hardware timer channels to generate a precise 50 Hz PWM signal; calling `attach()` with explicit 500–2400 µs pulse-width bounds instead of the defaults is important because the SG90's actual range extends slightly beyond the Arduino standard 544–2400 µs range, giving you full 0–180° travel without binding.

Port C on the M5Stack Core2 exposes a hardware UART on GPIO13 (RX) and GPIO14 (TX), making it a natural fit for UART-bridged RFID modules that wrap MFRC522 communication in a serial protocol.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to M5Stack Core2 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V (Grove Port C) |
| GND | GND (Grove Port C) |
| TX | GPIO13 (Port C RX) |
| RX | GPIO14 (Port C TX) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------------+          +----------------------+
|      M5Stack Core2      |          |  MFRC522 UART Module |
|                         |          |                      |
|  Port C (Grove UART)    |          |                      |
|  GPIO14 / TX  ----------+----------+-- RX                 |
|  GPIO13 / RX  ----------+----------+-- TX                 |
|  3.3V         ----------+----------+-- VCC                |
|  GND          ----------+----------+-- GND                |
+-------------------------+          +----------------------+
```

The MFRC522 UART bridge module operates at 3.3V logic — do not supply 5V or you risk damaging the module.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <M5Core2.h>

// Port C UART pins
#define RFID_RX_PIN 13
#define RFID_TX_PIN 14
#define RFID_BAUD   9600

// UART-bridged MFRC522 command/response bytes (common serial RFID module protocol)
#define CMD_GET_UID    0x02
#define FRAME_HEAD     0xAB
#define FRAME_END      0xCD

HardwareSerial rfidSerial(2); // Use UART2 on ESP32

void setup() {
  M5.begin();
  M5.Lcd.setTextSize(2);
  M5.Lcd.println("RFID UART Reader");
  M5.Lcd.println("Scan a card...");

  rfidSerial.begin(RFID_BAUD, SERIAL_8N1, RFID_RX_PIN, RFID_TX_PIN);
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Serial.println("MFRC522 UART bridge ready");
}

void sendReadCommand() {
  // Build a minimal read-UID command frame: HEAD | LEN | CMD | END
  uint8_t cmd[] = {FRAME_HEAD, 0x01, CMD_GET_UID, FRAME_END};
  rfidSerial.write(cmd, sizeof(cmd));
}

bool readUID(uint8_t* uid, uint8_t* uidLen) {
  if (rfidSerial.available() < 6) return false;

  uint8_t buf[16];
  int len = rfidSerial.readBytes(buf, 16);

  // Expect response: HEAD | LEN | STATUS | UID bytes... | END
  if (len < 6 || buf[0] != FRAME_HEAD || buf[len - 1] != FRAME_END) return false;
  if (buf[2] != 0x00) return false; // STATUS != OK

  *uidLen = buf[1] - 1; // subtract STATUS byte from payload length
  for (int i = 0; i < *uidLen && i < 7; i++) {
    uid[i] = buf[3 + i];
  }
  return true;
}

void loop() {
  M5.update();

  sendReadCommand();
  delay(200);

  uint8_t uid[7];
  uint8_t uidLen = 0;

  if (readUID(uid, &uidLen)) {
    String uidStr = "UID: ";
    for (int i = 0; i < uidLen; i++) {
      if (uid[i] < 0x10) uidStr += "0";
      uidStr += String(uid[i], HEX);
      if (i < uidLen - 1) uidStr += ":";
    }
    uidStr.toUpperCase();
    Serial.println(uidStr);
    M5.Lcd.fillScreen(BLACK);
    M5.Lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
    M5.Lcd.setTextSize(2);
    M5.Lcd.println("Card Detected!");
    M5.Lcd.println(uidStr);
    delay(1000);
  }

  // Flush any leftover bytes before next poll
  while (rfidSerial.available()) rfidSerial.read();

  delay(300);
}
```

**How it works:** `HardwareSerial rfidSerial(2)` instantiates ESP32's UART2 peripheral and `rfidSerial.begin(...)` maps it to the Port C GPIO pins at runtime, which is necessary because the M5Stack Core2 uses UART0 internally and UART1 is reserved — UART2 is the only free hardware UART. The frame parser checks for the `0xAB` header and `0xCD` tail bytes before trusting any payload, preventing garbled serial data from being misinterpreted as a valid card UID.

Port B on the M5Stack Core2 provides GPIO36 (input-only) and GPIO26, so the OneWire data line is routed to GPIO26 with a 4.7kΩ pull-up resistor to 3.3V.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to M5Stack Core2 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V (Grove Port B) |
| GND | GND (Grove Port B) |
| DATA | GPIO26 (Port B pin2) via 4.7kΩ pull-up to 3.3V |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
|   M5Stack Core2   |          |     DS18B20       |
|                   |          |                   |
|  Port B           |          |                   |
|  3.3V  -----------|----------| VCC               |
|  GND   -----------|----------| GND               |
|  GPIO26 ----------|----+-----| DATA              |
|                   |    |     +-------------------+
+-------------------+    |
                         R 4.7kΩ
                         |
                        3.3V
```

Note: GPIO36 on Port B is input-only and cannot drive the OneWire bus; always use GPIO26 for the DS18B20 data line.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <M5Core2.h>
#include <OneWire.h>
#include <DallasTemperature.h>

#define ONE_WIRE_BUS 26  // Port B pin2 (GPIO26)

OneWire oneWire(ONE_WIRE_BUS);
DallasTemperature sensors(&oneWire);

void setup() {
  M5.begin();
  M5.Lcd.setTextSize(2);
  M5.Lcd.setTextColor(WHITE, BLACK);
  M5.Lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
  M5.Lcd.println("DS18B20 Temp");

  sensors.begin();

  int deviceCount = sensors.getDeviceCount();
  M5.Lcd.printf("Devices: %d\n", deviceCount);
  delay(1000);
}

void loop() {
  M5.update();

  sensors.requestTemperatures();
  float tempC = sensors.getTempCByIndex(0);

  M5.Lcd.setCursor(0, 60);
  if (tempC == DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C) {
    M5.Lcd.println("Sensor error!   ");
  } else {
    M5.Lcd.printf("Temp: %.2f C   \n", tempC);
  }

  Serial.printf("Temperature: %.2f C\n", tempC);
  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** `sensors.requestTemperatures()` triggers all DS18B20 devices on the bus to perform a conversion simultaneously, and `getTempCByIndex(0)` then retrieves the result from the first (index 0) sensor — checking against `DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C` catches wiring faults or a missing pull-up resistor before displaying garbage data.

Port B's two GPIO pins (GPIO36 and GPIO26) are used for TRIG and ECHO, but GPIO36 is input-only on the ESP32, so TRIG goes to GPIO26 and ECHO goes to GPIO36.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to M5Stack Core2 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V (Grove Port B) |
| GND | GND (Grove Port B) |
| TRIG | GPIO26 (Port B pin2) |
| ECHO | GPIO36 (Port B pin1) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------------+          +-------------------+
|      M5Stack Core2      |          |     HC-SR04       |
|                         |          |                   |
|  Port B (Grove)         |          |                   |
|  5V  -------------------+----------+--- VCC            |
|  GND -------------------+----------+--- GND            |
|  GPIO26 ----------------+----------+--- TRIG           |
|  GPIO36 ----------------+----------+--- ECHO           |
+-------------------------+          +-------------------+
```
Note: GPIO36 on the ESP32 is an input-only pin with no internal pull-up, which is fine here since ECHO is always driven by the sensor.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <M5Core2.h>

#define TRIG_PIN 26
#define ECHO_PIN 36

void setup() {
  M5.begin();
  M5.Lcd.setTextSize(2);
  pinMode(TRIG_PIN, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(ECHO_PIN, INPUT);
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, LOW);
  Serial.begin(115200);
}

float readDistanceCm() {
  // Send 10us trigger pulse
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, LOW);
  delayMicroseconds(2);
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, HIGH);
  delayMicroseconds(10);
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, LOW);

  // Measure echo pulse duration (timeout after 30ms ~ 5m range)
  long duration = pulseIn(ECHO_PIN, HIGH, 30000);

  if (duration == 0) {
    return -1.0; // No echo received (out of range or error)
  }

  // Convert duration to distance: speed of sound ~343 m/s
  // distance (cm) = duration (us) * 0.0343 / 2
  return (duration * 0.0343f) / 2.0f;
}

void loop() {
  M5.update();
  float distance = readDistanceCm();

  M5.Lcd.fillScreen(BLACK);
  M5.Lcd.setCursor(10, 80);

  if (distance < 0) {
    M5.Lcd.setTextColor(RED);
    M5.Lcd.print("Out of range");
    Serial.println("Out of range");
  } else {
    M5.Lcd.setTextColor(GREEN);
    M5.Lcd.printf("%.1f cm", distance);
    Serial.printf("Distance: %.1f cm\n", distance);
  }

  delay(200);
}
```

**How it works:** `pulseIn()` with a 30,000 µs timeout prevents the sketch from blocking indefinitely when no echo returns (e.g., target too far or absent), returning 0 in that case which is mapped to -1 for error handling. The factor of 2 in the distance formula accounts for the round-trip travel of the ultrasonic pulse to the target and back.

The BME280 uses I2C with a default address of 0x76 (or 0x77 if SDO is pulled high), so it shares the ESP32's standard I2C bus on GPIO 21 (SDA) and GPIO 22 (SCL) without consuming any extra pins.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | GPIO 21 |
| SCL | GPIO 22 |
| SDO | GND (sets I2C address to 0x76) |
| CSB | 3.3V (forces I2C mode) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +----------------+          +-------------------+
        |   BME280       |          |      ESP32        |
        |                |          |                   |
        |  VCC  o--------+----------o 3.3V              |
        |  GND  o--------+----------o GND               |
        |  SDA  o--------+----------o GPIO 21 (SDA)     |
        |  SCL  o--------+----------o GPIO 22 (SCL)     |
        |  SDO  o---+    |          |                   |
        |            |   |          +-------------------+
        |  CSB  o--[3.3V] |         
        |       to GND    |         
        +----------------+          
```

Keep VCC at 3.3V — the BME280 is not 5V tolerant on its I/O lines.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_Sensor.h>
#include <Adafruit_BME280.h>

#define BME280_I2C_ADDRESS 0x76
#define SEA_LEVEL_PRESSURE_HPA 1013.25

Adafruit_BME280 bme;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Wire.begin(21, 22); // SDA, SCL

  if (!bme.begin(BME280_I2C_ADDRESS, &Wire)) {
    Serial.println("Could not find BME280 sensor! Check wiring and I2C address.");
    while (1) delay(10);
  }

  // Set sensor to normal (continuous) mode with standard oversampling
  bme.setSampling(
    Adafruit_BME280::MODE_NORMAL,
    Adafruit_BME280::SAMPLING_X16,  // temperature
    Adafruit_BME280::SAMPLING_X16,  // pressure
    Adafruit_BME280::SAMPLING_X16,  // humidity
    Adafruit_BME280::FILTER_X16,
    Adafruit_BME280::STANDBY_MS_500
  );

  Serial.println("BME280 initialized successfully.");
}

void loop() {
  float temperature = bme.readTemperature();          // Celsius
  float humidity    = bme.readHumidity();             // %RH
  float pressure    = bme.readPressure() / 100.0F;   // hPa
  float altitude    = bme.readAltitude(SEA_LEVEL_PRESSURE_HPA); // meters

  Serial.print("Temperature: "); Serial.print(temperature); Serial.println(" °C");
  Serial.print("Humidity:    "); Serial.print(humidity);    Serial.println(" %");
  Serial.print("Pressure:    "); Serial.print(pressure);    Serial.println(" hPa");
  Serial.print("Altitude:    "); Serial.print(altitude);    Serial.println(" m");
  Serial.println("---");

  delay(2000);
}
```

**How it works:** Passing `&Wire` explicitly to `bme.begin()` ensures the library uses the correct I2C peripheral instance initialized with our chosen SDA/SCL pins, which matters on ESP32 since it can remap I2C to any GPIO. The pressure reading is divided by 100 to convert from Pascals (the raw unit returned by the library) to the more human-readable hectopascals (hPa).

The MPU6050 uses I2C and runs on 3.3V, so it connects directly to the ESP32's default I2C bus (SDA=GPIO21, SCL=GPIO22) without any level shifting needed.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | GPIO21 (SDA) |
| SCL | GPIO22 (SCL) |
| AD0 | GND (sets I2C address to 0x68) |
| INT | (optional) any free GPIO |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +----------------+          +-------------------+
        |    ESP32       |          |     MPU6050       |
        |                |          |                   |
        |   3.3V o-------+----------o VCC               |
        |    GND o-------+----------o GND               |
        |  GPIO21 o------+----------o SDA               |
        |  GPIO22 o------+----------o SCL               |
        |                |    GND --o AD0               |
        +----------------+          +-------------------+
```
The AD0 pin pulled to GND sets the I2C address to 0x68; tying it to 3.3V instead would shift the address to 0x69, allowing two MPU6050s on the same bus.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>

const uint8_t MPU6050_ADDR = 0x68;
const uint8_t PWR_MGMT_1   = 0x6B;
const uint8_t ACCEL_XOUT_H = 0x3B;
const uint8_t GYRO_XOUT_H  = 0x43;

void writeMPU(uint8_t reg, uint8_t value) {
  Wire.beginTransmission(MPU6050_ADDR);
  Wire.write(reg);
  Wire.write(value);
  Wire.endTransmission();
}

int16_t read16(uint8_t reg) {
  Wire.beginTransmission(MPU6050_ADDR);
  Wire.write(reg);
  Wire.endTransmission(false);
  Wire.requestFrom(MPU6050_ADDR, (uint8_t)2);
  return (Wire.read() << 8) | Wire.read();
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Wire.begin(21, 22); // SDA=GPIO21, SCL=GPIO22

  // Wake the MPU6050 from sleep (default power-on state is sleep)
  writeMPU(PWR_MGMT_1, 0x00);
  delay(100);

  // Verify device is present
  Wire.beginTransmission(MPU6050_ADDR);
  if (Wire.endTransmission() != 0) {
    Serial.println("MPU6050 not found! Check wiring.");
    while (true);
  }
  Serial.println("MPU6050 initialized.");
}

void loop() {
  // Raw accelerometer values (±2g default, 16384 LSB/g)
  int16_t ax = read16(ACCEL_XOUT_H);
  int16_t ay = read16(ACCEL_XOUT_H + 2);
  int16_t az = read16(ACCEL_XOUT_H + 4);

  // Raw gyroscope values (±250 °/s default, 131 LSB/°/s)
  int16_t gx = read16(GYRO_XOUT_H);
  int16_t gy = read16(GYRO_XOUT_H + 2);
  int16_t gz = read16(GYRO_XOUT_H + 4);

  // Convert to physical units
  float ax_g = ax / 16384.0f;
  float ay_g = ay / 16384.0f;
  float az_g = az / 16384.0f;
  float gx_ds = gx / 131.0f;
  float gy_ds = gy / 131.0f;
  float gz_ds = gz / 131.0f;

  Serial.printf("Accel (g):  X=%.3f  Y=%.3f  Z=%.3f\n", ax_g, ay_g, az_g);
  Serial.printf("Gyro (°/s): X=%.2f  Y=%.2f  Z=%.2f\n\n", gx_ds, gy_ds, gz_ds);

  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** Writing `0x00` to the `PWR_MGMT_1` register is essential because the MPU6050 boots into sleep mode by default and will return all zeros until explicitly woken. Each axis occupies two consecutive registers (high byte then low byte), so offsetting `ACCEL_XOUT_H` by 2 and 4 steps through X, Y, and Z without defining separate register constants.

The DS3231 uses I2C and runs on 3.3V, so it connects directly to the ESP32's default I2C bus (GPIO 21/22) without any level shifting.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | GPIO 21 |
| SCL | GPIO 22 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +------------------+          +-------------------+
        |      ESP32       |          |   DS3231 RTC      |
        |                  |          |                   |
        |        3.3V -----+----------+---- VCC           |
        |         GND -----+----------+---- GND           |
        |   GPIO 21  -----+----------+---- SDA           |
        |  (SDA)          |          |                   |
        |   GPIO 22  -----+----------+---- SCL           |
        |  (SCL)          |          |                   |
        +------------------+          +-------------------+
```
The DS3231 module typically includes onboard pull-up resistors on SDA and SCL, so no external pull-ups are needed in most cases.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>

#define DS3231_I2C_ADDRESS 0x68

// Converts BCD-encoded byte from DS3231 registers to decimal
uint8_t bcdToDec(uint8_t val) {
  return ((val / 16) * 10) + (val % 16);
}

// Converts decimal to BCD for writing to DS3231 registers
uint8_t decToBcd(uint8_t val) {
  return ((val / 10) * 16) + (val % 10);
}

void setTime(uint8_t sec, uint8_t min, uint8_t hour,
             uint8_t day, uint8_t date, uint8_t month, uint8_t year) {
  Wire.beginTransmission(DS3231_I2C_ADDRESS);
  Wire.write(0x00);             // Start at register 0 (seconds)
  Wire.write(decToBcd(sec));
  Wire.write(decToBcd(min));
  Wire.write(decToBcd(hour));   // 24-hour mode
  Wire.write(decToBcd(day));    // Day of week (1=Sunday)
  Wire.write(decToBcd(date));
  Wire.write(decToBcd(month));
  Wire.write(decToBcd(year));   // Last two digits of year
  Wire.endTransmission();
}

void readTime(uint8_t &sec, uint8_t &min, uint8_t &hour,
              uint8_t &day, uint8_t &date, uint8_t &month, uint8_t &year) {
  Wire.beginTransmission(DS3231_I2C_ADDRESS);
  Wire.write(0x00); // Set register pointer to 0
  Wire.endTransmission();

  Wire.requestFrom(DS3231_I2C_ADDRESS, 7);
  sec   = bcdToDec(Wire.read() & 0x7F); // Mask bit 7 (oscillator stop flag)
  min   = bcdToDec(Wire.read());
  hour  = bcdToDec(Wire.read() & 0x3F); // Mask bits 6-7 (12/24 hr flags)
  day   = bcdToDec(Wire.read());
  date  = bcdToDec(Wire.read());
  month = bcdToDec(Wire.read() & 0x1F); // Mask century bit
  year  = bcdToDec(Wire.read());
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Wire.begin(21, 22); // SDA=GPIO21, SCL=GPIO22

  // Set time once: 12:30:00, Monday, June 9, 2025
  // Comment this out after first upload to avoid resetting time on every boot
  setTime(0, 30, 12, 2, 9, 6, 25);

  Serial.println("DS3231 RTC initialized.");
}

void loop() {
  uint8_t sec, min, hour, day, date, month, year;
  readTime(sec, min, hour, day, date, month, year);

  Serial.printf("Time: %02d:%02d:%02d  Date: 20%02d-%02d-%02d\n",
                hour, min, sec, year, month, date);

  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** The DS3231 stores all time values in BCD (binary-coded decimal) format, so the `bcdToDec` and `decToBcd` helpers are essential for converting between the register encoding and human-readable numbers. The bitwise masks applied when reading seconds (`& 0x7F`), hours (`& 0x3F`), and month (`& 0x1F`) strip status/control flag bits that share those registers, preventing corrupted time values.

The ADS1115 uses I2C and runs on 3.3V, so it connects directly to the ESP32's default I2C bus pins (GPIO 21 SDA, GPIO 22 SCL) without any level shifting.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VDD | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | GPIO 21 (SDA) |
| SCL | GPIO 22 (SCL) |
| ADDR | GND (sets I2C address to 0x48) |
| A0 | Analog signal input (e.g. potentiometer wiper) |
| A1–A3 | NC or additional analog inputs |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +-------------------------+
        |          ESP32          |
        |                         |
        |   3.3V |----------+    |
        |   GND  |--------+ |    |
        |  GPIO21|----+   | |    |
        |  GPIO22|-+  |   | |    |
        +-----------|--+--+-+----+
                    |  |  | |
                    |  |  | |
        +-----------+--+--+-+----+
        |  SCL SDA GND VDD ADDR  |
        |       ADS1115          |
        |  A0  A1  A2  A3        |
        |   |                    |
        +---+--------------------+
            |
        [Analog Input Signal]
```

The ADDR pin pulled to GND sets the I2C address to 0x48; pulling it to VDD, SDA, or SCL selects 0x49, 0x4A, or 0x4B respectively, allowing up to four ADS1115 modules on one bus.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_ADS1X15.h>

Adafruit_ADS1115 ads;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Wire.begin(21, 22); // SDA, SCL

  if (!ads.begin(0x48)) {
    Serial.println("Failed to initialize ADS1115. Check wiring and address!");
    while (1);
  }

  // Set gain to +/-4.096V range (1 bit = 0.125 mV)
  ads.setGain(GAIN_ONE);

  Serial.println("ADS1115 initialized.");
}

void loop() {
  int16_t raw = ads.readADC_SingleEnded(0); // Read channel A0
  float voltage = ads.computeVolts(raw);    // Convert raw to volts

  Serial.print("A0 Raw: ");
  Serial.print(raw);
  Serial.print("  Voltage: ");
  Serial.print(voltage, 4);
  Serial.println(" V");

  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** The `setGain(GAIN_ONE)` call configures the ADS1115's programmable gain amplifier for a ±4.096V full-scale range, giving 0.125 mV per LSB — calling `computeVolts()` uses this gain setting internally to convert the raw 16-bit signed integer into a human-readable voltage rather than requiring manual scaling math.

The NRF24L01 runs on 3.3V only and uses SPI with two extra control pins (CE and CSN), so all lines must stay at 3.3V logic — the ESP32's native 3.3V GPIO levels are a perfect match.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V (NOT 5V) |
| GND | GND |
| SCK | GPIO 18 (SPI SCK) |
| MISO | GPIO 19 (SPI MISO) |
| MOSI | GPIO 23 (SPI MOSI) |
| CSN | GPIO 5 (SPI CS) |
| CE | GPIO 4 (any free GPIO) |
| IRQ | (optional) GPIO 2 (interrupt-capable GPIO) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +------------------+          +-------------------+
        |      ESP32       |          |    NRF24L01       |
        |                  |          |                   |
        |       3.3V |-----+----------| VCC               |
        |        GND |-----+----------| GND               |
        |   GPIO18   |-----+----------| SCK               |
        |   GPIO19   |-----+----------| MISO              |
        |   GPIO23   |-----+----------| MOSI              |
        |   GPIO5    |-----+----------| CSN               |
        |   GPIO4    |-----+----------| CE                |
        |   GPIO2    |-----+----------| IRQ  (optional)   |
        +------------------+          +-------------------+
```
The NRF24L01 is strictly 3.3V — connecting VCC to 5V will permanently damage the module; also consider adding a 10µF capacitor across VCC and GND on the module to stabilize its notoriously noisy power draw.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <SPI.h>
#include <RF24.h>

// CE = GPIO4, CSN = GPIO5
RF24 radio(4, 5);

// Address for the communication pipe (both TX and RX must match)
const byte address[6] = "00001";

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  if (!radio.begin()) {
    Serial.println("NRF24L01 not detected! Check wiring.");
    while (1) {}
  }

  // Set power level and data rate for short-range reliability
  radio.setPALevel(RF24_PA_LOW);
  radio.setDataRate(RF24_250KBPS);

  // Open a writing pipe to the target address
  radio.openWritingPipe(address);

  // Disable auto-acknowledge for this simple broadcast example
  radio.stopListening();

  Serial.println("NRF24L01 initialized. Starting transmit loop...");
}

void loop() {
  const char message[] = "Hello ESP32!";

  bool success = radio.write(&message, sizeof(message));

  if (success) {
    Serial.println("Transmission successful!");
  } else {
    Serial.println("Transmission failed.");
  }

  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** The `RF24` constructor takes CE and CSN pin numbers separately from the SPI bus because CE controls RX/TX mode switching while CSN is the standard SPI chip-select — the library manages the ESP32's hardware SPI automatically once `radio.begin()` is called. Setting `RF24_250KBPS` improves range and link reliability at the cost of throughput, which matters most for small, infrequent messages like this one.

The MAX7219 uses SPI but only in write mode (no MISO needed), so only SCK, MOSI, and a chip-select line are required alongside 3.3V/5V power — the MAX7219 itself runs its logic at 5V but its data input is 3.3V tolerant on most modules.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V |
| GND | GND |
| DIN (MOSI) | GPIO 23 (VSPI MOSI) |
| CLK (SCK) | GPIO 18 (VSPI SCK) |
| CS (LOAD) | GPIO 5 (VSPI CS) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------------+          +---------------------+
|        ESP32         |          |  MAX7219 LED Matrix  |
|                      |          |                      |
|    5V o--------------------------o VCC                  |
|   GND o--------------------------o GND                  |
|  GPIO23 (MOSI) o----------------o DIN                  |
|  GPIO18 (SCK)  o----------------o CLK                  |
|  GPIO5  (CS)   o----------------o CS/LOAD              |
+----------------------+          +---------------------+
```
The MAX7219 module typically includes a 10uF and 100nF decoupling capacitor on-board, but adding a 100nF cap between VCC and GND close to the chip is recommended for stability.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <SPI.h>

#define CS_PIN 5

// MAX7219 register addresses
#define REG_NOOP        0x00
#define REG_DIGIT0      0x01
#define REG_DECODE_MODE 0x09
#define REG_INTENSITY   0x0A
#define REG_SCAN_LIMIT  0x0B
#define REG_SHUTDOWN    0x0C
#define REG_TEST        0x0F

// 8x8 smiley face bitmap (one byte per row, MSB = leftmost column)
const uint8_t smiley[8] = {
  0b00111100,
  0b01000010,
  0b10100101,
  0b10000001,
  0b10100101,
  0b10011001,
  0b01000010,
  0b00111100
};

// A second frame: heart shape
const uint8_t heart[8] = {
  0b00000000,
  0b01100110,
  0b11111111,
  0b11111111,
  0b01111110,
  0b00111100,
  0b00011000,
  0b00000000
};

void maxWrite(uint8_t reg, uint8_t data) {
  digitalWrite(CS_PIN, LOW);
  SPI.transfer(reg);
  SPI.transfer(data);
  digitalWrite(CS_PIN, HIGH);
}

void initMAX7219() {
  maxWrite(REG_TEST, 0x00);        // disable test mode
  maxWrite(REG_DECODE_MODE, 0x00); // no BCD decode, raw bitmap mode
  maxWrite(REG_SCAN_LIMIT, 0x07);  // scan all 8 rows
  maxWrite(REG_INTENSITY, 0x05);   // mid brightness (0x00-0x0F)
  maxWrite(REG_SHUTDOWN, 0x01);    // normal operation (not shutdown)
}

void displayBitmap(const uint8_t bitmap[8]) {
  for (uint8_t row = 0; row < 8; row++) {
    maxWrite(REG_DIGIT0 + row, bitmap[row]);
  }
}

void clearDisplay() {
  for (uint8_t row = 0; row < 8; row++) {
    maxWrite(REG_DIGIT0 + row, 0x00);
  }
}

void setup() {
  pinMode(CS_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(CS_PIN, HIGH);

  SPI.begin(18, -1, 23, 5); // SCK, MISO (unused=-1), MOSI, SS
  SPI.beginTransaction(SPISettings(1000000, MSBFIRST, SPI_MODE0));

  initMAX7219();
  clearDisplay();
}

void loop() {
  // Display smiley face
  displayBitmap(smiley);
  delay(1000);

  // Scroll transition: shift rows down one at a time
  for (int shift = 0; shift < 8; shift++) {
    for (uint8_t row = 0; row < 8; row++) {
      uint8_t outgoing = (row >= shift) ? smiley[row - shift] : 0x00; // not used after first frame
      uint8_t incoming = (row + (8 - shift) < 8) ? heart[row + (8 - shift)] : heart[row - shift];
      // Blend: show outgoing scrolling off, incoming scrolling in
      uint8_t val = (row < (8 - shift)) ? smiley[row + shift] : heart[row - (8 - shift)];
      maxWrite(REG_DIGIT0 + row, val);
    }
    delay(80);
  }

  // Display heart
  displayBitmap(heart);
  delay(1000);

  // Scroll back to smiley
  for (int shift = 0; shift < 8; shift++) {
    for (uint8_t row = 0; row < 8; row++) {
      uint8_t val = (row < (8 - shift)) ? heart[row + shift] : smiley[row - (8 - shift)];
      maxWrite(REG_DIGIT0 + row, val);
    }
    delay(80);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The MAX7219 requires `REG_DECODE_MODE` to be set to `0x00` so it accepts raw 8-bit row bitmaps instead of BCD-encoded digit data — skipping this step results in a blank or garbled display. The scroll effect is achieved by slicing the outgoing and incoming bitmaps by a shifting row offset each frame rather than using hardware scrolling, since the MAX7219 has no built-in scroll register.

The ST7735 runs on 3.3V logic and uses hardware SPI, so it maps directly onto the ESP32's VSPI bus with a couple of extra GPIO lines for the display's DC and reset control signals.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SCL (SCK) | GPIO 18 (VSPI SCK) |
| SDA (MOSI) | GPIO 23 (VSPI MOSI) |
| CS | GPIO 5 (VSPI CS) |
| DC (A0) | GPIO 2 |
| RES (RST) | GPIO 4 |
| BLK (backlight) | 3.3V (or GPIO for PWM control) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +------------------+          +-------------------+
        |      ESP32       |          |  ST7735 1.8" TFT  |
        |                  |          |                   |
        |       3.3V o-----+----------+o VCC              |
        |         GND o----+----------+o GND              |
        |   GPIO18 SCK o---+----------+o SCL              |
        |  GPIO23 MOSI o---+----------+o SDA              |
        |    GPIO5  CS o---+----------+o CS               |
        |    GPIO2  DC o---+----------+o DC               |
        |   GPIO4 RST  o---+----------+o RES              |
        |       3.3V o-----+----------+o BLK              |
        +------------------+          +-------------------+
```

Do not supply 5V to the ST7735 — it is a 3.3V-only device and overvoltage will permanently damage the display controller.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>
#include <Adafruit_ST7735.h>
#include <SPI.h>

// Pin definitions
#define TFT_CS   5
#define TFT_DC   2
#define TFT_RST  4

// Create display object using hardware SPI
Adafruit_ST7735 tft = Adafruit_ST7735(TFT_CS, TFT_DC, TFT_RST);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  // Initialize with initR for the common 1.8" red-tab ST7735 module
  tft.initR(INITR_BLACKTAB);

  // Rotate landscape if desired (0=portrait, 1=landscape, etc.)
  tft.setRotation(1);

  // Fill background black
  tft.fillScreen(ST77XX_BLACK);

  // Draw a filled rectangle
  tft.fillRect(10, 10, 60, 40, ST77XX_BLUE);

  // Draw a circle outline
  tft.drawCircle(120, 50, 30, ST77XX_RED);

  // Draw a filled triangle
  tft.fillTriangle(20, 90, 60, 90, 40, 60, ST77XX_GREEN);

  // Print some text
  tft.setTextColor(ST77XX_WHITE);
  tft.setTextSize(2);
  tft.setCursor(10, 105);
  tft.println("ESP32 SPI");

  tft.setTextColor(ST77XX_YELLOW);
  tft.setTextSize(1);
  tft.setCursor(10, 125);
  tft.println("ST7735 TFT Demo");
}

void loop() {
  // Cycle background color to show continuous operation
  delay(2000);
  tft.fillScreen(ST77XX_RED);
  tft.setTextColor(ST77XX_WHITE);
  tft.setTextSize(2);
