```

**How it works:** The `initR(INITR_BLACKTAB)` call sends the specific initialization command sequence for the most common 1.8" ST7735 variant — using the wrong tab constant (GREENTAB, REDTAB, etc.) can cause color shifts or incorrect pixel mapping, so matching it to your physical module's label matters. The Adafruit_ST7735 constructor binds to the hardware VSPI bus automatically when no MISO pin is specified, keeping transfers fast without needing to configure the SPI bus manually.

The MFRC522 operates at 3.3V and is not 5V tolerant, so it connects directly to the ESP32's hardware SPI bus (VSPI) without any level shifting needed.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC (3.3V) | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SCK | GPIO 18 (VSPI SCK) |
| MISO | GPIO 19 (VSPI MISO) |
| MOSI | GPIO 23 (VSPI MOSI) |
| SDA (SS/CS) | GPIO 5 |
| RST | GPIO 22 |
| IRQ | Not connected |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
  +----------------------+          +-------------------+
  |        ESP32         |          |     MFRC522       |
  |                      |          |                   |
  |        3.3V o--------+----------o VCC               |
  |         GND o--------+----------o GND               |
  |  GPIO18 SCK o--------+----------o SCK               |
  | GPIO19 MISO o--------+----------o MISO              |
  | GPIO23 MOSI o--------+----------o MOSI              |
  |      GPIO5  o--------+----------o SDA (CS)          |
  |      GPIO22 o--------+----------o RST               |
  |                      |          |     IRQ (NC)      |
  +----------------------+          +-------------------+
```

Keep 3.3V supply to the MFRC522 — connecting VCC to 5V will permanently damage the module.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <SPI.h>
#include <MFRC522.h>

#define SS_PIN  5
#define RST_PIN 22

MFRC522 rfid(SS_PIN, RST_PIN);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  SPI.begin(18, 19, 23, SS_PIN); // SCK, MISO, MOSI, SS
  rfid.PCD_Init();
  Serial.println("MFRC522 RFID Reader Ready");
  Serial.println("Place a card near the reader...");
}

void loop() {
  // Look for a new card
  if (!rfid.PICC_IsNewCardPresent()) {
    return;
  }

  // Verify that we can read the card
  if (!rfid.PICC_ReadCardSerial()) {
    return;
  }

  Serial.print("Card UID: ");
  for (byte i = 0; i < rfid.uid.size; i++) {
    if (rfid.uid.uidByte[i] < 0x10) {
      Serial.print("0"); // leading zero for single-digit hex
    }
    Serial.print(rfid.uid.uidByte[i], HEX);
    if (i < rfid.uid.size - 1) {
      Serial.print(":");
    }
  }
  Serial.println();

  // Print card type
  MFRC522::PICC_Type piccType = rfid.PICC_GetType(rfid.uid.sak);
  Serial.print("Card Type: ");
  Serial.println(rfid.PICC_GetTypeName(piccType));

  // Halt the card and stop encryption to allow re-reading
  rfid.PICC_HaltA();
  rfid.PCD_StopCrypto1();

  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** `SPI.begin(18, 19, 23, SS_PIN)` explicitly assigns the VSPI bus pins rather than relying on defaults, ensuring correct mapping on all ESP32 variants. Calling `rfid.PICC_HaltA()` followed by `rfid.PCD_StopCrypto1()` after each read is essential — without halting, the same card will be detected repeatedly in every loop iteration without ever releasing the bus.

The MAX6675 uses a read-only SPI interface (no MOSI needed) and outputs a 12-bit temperature value, so only SCK, CS, and MISO lines are required alongside power.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SCK | GPIO 18 (SPI SCK) |
| CS | GPIO 5 (chip select) |
| SO (MISO) | GPIO 19 (SPI MISO) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------+          +-------------------+
|   ESP32        |          |   MAX6675         |
|                |          |                   |
|   3.3V  -------+----------+-- VCC             |
|   GND   -------+----------+-- GND             |
|  GPIO18 -------+----------+-- SCK             |
|   GPIO5 -------+----------+-- CS              |
|  GPIO19 -------+----------+-- SO (MISO)       |
|                |          |                   |
|                |          |   T+  <-- thermocouple
|                |          |   T-  <-- thermocouple
+----------------+          +-------------------+
```
The MAX6675 is strictly a 3.3V device on its VCC pin; do not connect it to 5V.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <SPI.h>

// Pin definitions
const int CS_PIN  = 5;
const int SCK_PIN = 18;
const int SO_PIN  = 19;  // MISO — MAX6675 is read-only, no MOSI needed

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(CS_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(CS_PIN, HIGH);  // deselect chip at startup
  SPI.begin(SCK_PIN, SO_PIN, -1, CS_PIN);  // MOSI = -1 (unused)
  delay(500);  // allow MAX6675 to stabilize after power-on
  Serial.println("MAX6675 Thermocouple Reader Ready");
}

double readThermocouple() {
  digitalWrite(CS_PIN, LOW);
  delayMicroseconds(10);  // CS setup time per datasheet

  // MAX6675 sends 16 bits: bit 15 is dummy sign, bits 14-3 are 12-bit temp data
  uint16_t raw = SPI.transfer16(0x0000);

  digitalWrite(CS_PIN, HIGH);

  // Bit 2 is the fault indicator — if set, thermocouple is disconnected
  if (raw & 0x0004) {
    Serial.println("ERROR: Thermocouple not connected!");
    return -1.0;
  }

  // Extract bits 14:3 (12 bits) and multiply by 0.25 for degrees Celsius
  raw >>= 3;
  return raw * 0.25;
}

void loop() {
  double tempC = readThermocouple();
  if (tempC >= 0) {
    Serial.print("Temperature: ");
    Serial.print(tempC, 2);
    Serial.println(" °C");
  }
  delay(1000);  // MAX6675 needs ~220 ms between conversions; 1 s is safe
}
```

**How it works:** The raw 16-bit SPI word must be right-shifted by 3 bits to discard the device ID and fault bits, after which each LSB represents 0.25 °C — checking bit 2 before the shift catches an open thermocouple fault that the MAX6675 signals in hardware.

The SIM800L operates at 3.7–4.2V for its power supply (not 3.3V or 5V directly), so it needs its own regulated supply, while its UART logic pins are 2.8V-tolerant but generally work with ESP32 GPIOs when the module is properly powered.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | External 3.7–4.2V supply (e.g., LiPo or dedicated regulator), NOT 3.3V/5V pin |
| GND | GND (shared with ESP32 GND) |
| TXD | GPIO16 (ESP32 RX2) |
| RXD | GPIO17 (ESP32 TX2) |
| RST | GPIO5 (optional, for hardware reset) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
|        ESP32        |          |     SIM800L       |
|                     |          |                   |
|  GPIO17 (TX2) ------|--------->| RXD               |
|  GPIO16 (RX2) <-----|----------| TXD               |
|  GPIO5        ------|--------->| RST               |
|  GND          ------|----------| GND               |
|                     |          | VCC <--- [3.7-4.2V|
+---------------------+          |          supply]  |
                                 +-------------------+
                                       |
                               [SIM Card inserted]
```
The SIM800L can draw up to 2A in bursts during transmission, so use a dedicated supply capable of at least 2A — connecting VCC to the ESP32's 3.3V or 5V pin will cause brownouts and resets.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <HardwareSerial.h>

// Use ESP32 hardware UART2 for SIM800L
HardwareSerial sim800(2); // UART2

const int SIM800_TX = 17; // ESP32 TX2 -> SIM800L RXD
const int SIM800_RX = 16; // ESP32 RX2 <- SIM800L TXD
const int SIM800_RST = 5;

const char* phoneNumber = "+1234567890"; // Replace with target number
const char* message = "Hello from ESP32 + SIM800L!";

void sendAT(const char* cmd, unsigned long timeout = 2000) {
  sim800.println(cmd);
  unsigned long start = millis();
  while (millis() - start < timeout) {
    while (sim800.available()) {
      Serial.write(sim800.read());
    }
  }
}

void resetSIM800() {
  digitalWrite(SIM800_RST, LOW);
  delay(200);
  digitalWrite(SIM800_RST, HIGH);
  delay(3000); // Wait for module to boot
}

void sendSMS(const char* number, const char* text) {
  // Set SMS to text mode
  sendAT("AT+CMGF=1");
  delay(500);

  // Specify recipient
  String cmd = String("AT+CMGS=\"") + number + "\"";
  sim800.println(cmd);
  delay(500);

  // Send message body followed by Ctrl+Z (ASCII 26) to transmit
  sim800.print(text);
  delay(100);
  sim800.write(26); // Ctrl+Z signals end of SMS content
  delay(5000); // Wait for send confirmation

  // Print any response
  while (sim800.available()) {
    Serial.write(sim800.read());
  }
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(SIM800_RST, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(SIM800_RST, HIGH);

  // Initialize SIM800L UART
  sim800.begin(9600, SERIAL_8N1, SIM800_RX, SIM800_TX);
  delay(1000);

  Serial.println("Resetting SIM800L...");
  resetSIM800();

  Serial.println("Checking module...");
  sendAT("AT");         // Basic handshake
  sendAT("AT+CPIN?");   // Check SIM card status
  sendAT("AT+CSQ");     // Check signal quality
  sendAT("AT+CREG?");   // Check network registration

  Serial.println("Sending SMS...");
  sendSMS(phoneNumber, message);
  Serial.println("SMS sent (or attempted).");
}

void loop() {
  // Echo SIM800L responses to Serial Monitor for debugging
  while (sim800.available()) {
    Serial.write(sim800.read());
  }
  while (Serial.available()) {
    sim800.write(Serial.read());
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The critical detail is sending `sim800.write(26)` (ASCII Ctrl+Z) after the message body — this is the GSM standard terminator that tells the SIM800L to actually transmit the SMS, replacing the more intuitive newline. The `AT+CMGF=1` command switches the module into text mode, which lets you pass a plain string instead of PDU-encoded binary data.

The HC-05 operates at 3.3V logic but its VCC can accept 5V; since the ESP32 is 3.3V logic, no level shifting is needed on the data lines, and we use UART2 (GPIO16/17) to keep the programming/debug UART0 free.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V (or 3.3V if module supports it) |
| GND | GND |
| TX | GPIO16 (RX2) |
| RX | GPIO17 (TX2) |
| EN/KEY | (leave floating or tie to 3.3V to enter AT mode) |
| STATE | (optional, leave unconnected) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +----------------------+          +-------------------+
        |        ESP32         |          |      HC-05        |
        |                      |          |                   |
        |   GPIO16 (RX2) <-----+----------+---- TX            |
        |   GPIO17 (TX2) ------+----------+---> RX            |
        |                      |          |                   |
        |              3.3V/5V-+----------+---- VCC           |
        |                 GND -+----------+---- GND           |
        +----------------------+          +-------------------+
```
The HC-05 RX pin is nominally 3.3V tolerant on most modules, but if yours is not, add a voltage divider (1kΩ + 2kΩ) between ESP32 TX and HC-05 RX.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// Use hardware UART2 on ESP32 to communicate with HC-05
// leaving UART0 free for USB serial debugging
#define HC05_RX_PIN 16  // ESP32 RX2 <-- HC-05 TX
#define HC05_TX_PIN 17  // ESP32 TX2 --> HC-05 RX
#define HC05_BAUD   9600 // Default HC-05 baud rate

HardwareSerial btSerial(2); // UART2

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);             // USB debug serial
  btSerial.begin(HC05_BAUD, SERIAL_8N1, HC05_RX_PIN, HC05_TX_PIN);
  Serial.println("HC-05 Bluetooth UART bridge ready.");
  Serial.println("Type in Serial Monitor to send over Bluetooth.");
}

void loop() {
  // Forward data from USB Serial Monitor --> Bluetooth
  if (Serial.available()) {
    String input = Serial.readStringUntil('\n');
    btSerial.println(input);
    Serial.print("[Sent BT]: ");
    Serial.println(input);
  }

  // Forward data received from Bluetooth --> USB Serial Monitor
  if (btSerial.available()) {
    String received = btSerial.readStringUntil('\n');
    Serial.print("[Recv BT]: ");
    Serial.println(received);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** `HardwareSerial btSerial(2)` instantiates ESP32's second hardware UART peripheral, and passing the custom pin numbers to `btSerial.begin()` is what remaps UART2 from its defaults to GPIO16/17 — this is ESP32-specific and essential for the wiring to work. The loop then acts as a transparent bridge, shuttling bytes in both directions so you can test the HC-05 link from the Arduino IDE Serial Monitor while a phone paired to the HC-05 sends and receives on the other end.

The R307 fingerprint sensor communicates over UART at 3.3V logic levels, so it can be connected directly to any free ESP32 hardware UART pins without a level shifter since the sensor's logic is 3.3V-compatible (though it is powered from 5V).

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC (Power) | 5V (VIN) |
| GND | GND |
| TX | GPIO16 (RX2) |
| RX | GPIO17 (TX2) |
| Wakeup (optional) | 3.3V or leave floating |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
 +------------------+          +-------------------+
 |      ESP32       |          |   R307 Fingerprint|
 |                  |          |      Sensor       |
 |       GPIO16(RX2)|<---------|TX                 |
 |       GPIO17(TX2)|--------->|RX                 |
 |              GND |----------|GND                |
 |              VIN |----------|VCC (5V)           |
 +------------------+          +-------------------+
```
The R307 requires 5V on its VCC pin for the optical scanner to operate, but its UART data lines operate at 3.3V logic, making them safe to connect directly to the ESP32's GPIO pins.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <Adafruit_Fingerprint.h>

// Use ESP32 hardware UART2: RX=GPIO16, TX=GPIO17
HardwareSerial mySerial(2);
Adafruit_Fingerprint finger = Adafruit_Fingerprint(&mySerial);

uint8_t enrollId = 1; // ID slot to enroll into

uint8_t getFingerprintEnroll() {
  int p = -1;
  Serial.print("Waiting for valid finger to enroll as #");
  Serial.println(enrollId);

  while (p != FINGERPRINT_OK) {
    p = finger.getImage();
    if (p == FINGERPRINT_OK) Serial.println("Image taken");
    else if (p == FINGERPRINT_NOFINGER) Serial.print(".");
    else { Serial.println("Image error"); return p; }
  }

  p = finger.image2Tz(1);
  if (p != FINGERPRINT_OK) { Serial.println("Image convert error"); return p; }

  Serial.println("Remove finger");
  delay(2000);
  while (finger.getImage() != FINGERPRINT_NOFINGER);

  Serial.println("Place same finger again");
  p = -1;
  while (p != FINGERPRINT_OK) {
    p = finger.getImage();
    if (p == FINGERPRINT_OK) Serial.println("Image taken");
    else if (p == FINGERPRINT_NOFINGER) Serial.print(".");
    else { Serial.println("Image error"); return p; }
  }

  p = finger.image2Tz(2);
  if (p != FINGERPRINT_OK) { Serial.println("Image convert error"); return p; }

  p = finger.createModel();
  if (p != FINGERPRINT_OK) { Serial.println("Fingerprints did not match"); return p; }

  p = finger.storeModel(enrollId);
  if (p == FINGERPRINT_OK) {
    Serial.print("Stored fingerprint at ID #"); Serial.println(enrollId);
  } else {
    Serial.println("Store error");
  }
  return p;
}

uint8_t getFingerprintID() {
  uint8_t p = finger.getImage();
  if (p != FINGERPRINT_OK) return p;

  p = finger.image2Tz();
  if (p != FINGERPRINT_OK) return p;

  p = finger.fingerFastSearch();
  if (p == FINGERPRINT_OK) {
    Serial.print("Match found! ID: "); Serial.print(finger.fingerID);
    Serial.print(" Confidence: "); Serial.println(finger.confidence);
  } else {
    Serial.println("No match found");
  }
  return p;
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  // Initialize UART2 at R307 default baud rate, with RX=16, TX=17
  mySerial.begin(57600, SERIAL_8N1, 16, 17);
  finger.begin(57600);

  if (finger.verifyPassword()) {
    Serial.println("R307 fingerprint sensor found!");
  } else {
    Serial.println("R307 not found. Check wiring.");
    while (1) delay(1);
  }

  Serial.println("Type 'e' to enroll, any other key to search.");
}

void loop() {
  if (Serial.available()) {
    char cmd = Serial.read();
    if (cmd == 'e') {
      getFingerprintEnroll();
    }
  } else {
    getFingerprintID();
    delay(50);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The `mySerial.begin(57600, SERIAL_8N1, 16, 17)` call explicitly assigns GPIO16 as RX and GPIO17 as TX for ESP32's hardware UART2, which is required because ESP32 doesn't default UART2 to those pins the same way Arduino boards do. The two-pass enrollment (`image2Tz(1)` then `image2Tz(2)` followed by `createModel()`) captures the finger twice and merges both images into a single template before storing it, improving match reliability.

The SG90 servo is controlled via a 50 Hz PWM signal with pulse widths between ~500 µs and ~2400 µs, and while the ESP32's `ledcWrite` peripheral handles this cleanly, the servo's power draw means it should ideally be powered from a separate 5V supply rather than the ESP32's 3.3V rail.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| Signal (Orange) | GPIO 18 |
| VCC (Red) | External 5V supply (or VBUS/VIN if USB-powered) |
| GND (Brown/Black) | GND (shared with ESP32 GND) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------+          +------------------+
|     ESP32      |          |   SG90 Servo     |
|                |          |                  |
|       GPIO 18  |--------->| Signal (Orange)  |
|                |          |                  |
|          3.3V  |          | VCC (Red) <------+--- 5V Supply
|                |          |                  |
|            GND |----+---->| GND (Brown)      |
+----------------+    |     +------------------+
                       |
                  [Common GND]
```
Make sure the external 5V supply's GND is tied to the ESP32's GND to create a common reference.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// PWM configuration
const int SERVO_PIN    = 18;
const int LEDC_CHANNEL = 0;
const int LEDC_FREQ    = 50;      // 50 Hz standard servo frequency
const int LEDC_RES     = 16;      // 16-bit resolution for fine control

// Pulse widths in microseconds
const int PULSE_MIN_US = 500;     // ~0 degrees
const int PULSE_MAX_US = 2400;    // ~180 degrees

// Convert microseconds to 16-bit duty cycle value at 50 Hz
// Period = 1,000,000 / 50 = 20,000 µs
uint32_t usToDuty(int us) {
  return (uint32_t)((us / 20000.0) * 65536);
}

void setServoAngle(int angle) {
  // Map 0-180 degrees to pulse width range
  int pulseUs = map(angle, 0, 180, PULSE_MIN_US, PULSE_MAX_US);
  ledcWrite(LEDC_CHANNEL, usToDuty(pulseUs));
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  ledcSetup(LEDC_CHANNEL, LEDC_FREQ, LEDC_RES);
  ledcAttachPin(SERVO_PIN, LEDC_CHANNEL);
  Serial.println("Servo sweep starting...");
}

void loop() {
  // Sweep from 0 to 180 degrees
  for (int angle = 0; angle <= 180; angle += 1) {
    setServoAngle(angle);
    delay(15);
  }
  delay(500);

  // Sweep back from 180 to 0 degrees
  for (int angle = 180; angle >= 0; angle -= 1) {
    setServoAngle(angle);
    delay(15);
  }
  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** Using 16-bit resolution with `ledcSetup` gives 65536 steps over the 20 ms servo period, so the `usToDuty` conversion achieves much finer pulse-width granularity than the Arduino `Servo` library's typical 8-bit approach — this prevents jitter at fine angle increments. The `map()` call then linearizes degree values to microsecond pulse widths, which are the values SG90 datasheets specify rather than raw duty percentages.

A passive buzzer requires a PWM signal to produce sound, so it's driven directly from a GPIO pin capable of PWM output — no I2C or SPI bus needed.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| + (positive) | GPIO 25 |
| - (negative) | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +------------------+
        |      ESP32       |
        |                  |
        |        GPIO 25 o-|-------> + (Passive Buzzer)
        |                  |              |
        |           GND o--|-----------> - (Passive Buzzer)
        |                  |
        +------------------+
```
Passive buzzers are typically 3.3V-tolerant and draw little current, so driving them directly from an ESP32 GPIO pin is safe without a transistor for short durations.

**Code**
```cpp
#include "Arduino.h"

const int BUZZER_PIN = 25;
const int PWM_CHANNEL = 0;
const int PWM_RESOLUTION = 8; // 8-bit resolution (0-255)

// Notes as frequencies (Hz)
#define NOTE_C4  262
#define NOTE_D4  294
#define NOTE_E4  330
#define NOTE_F4  349
#define NOTE_G4  392
#define NOTE_A4  440
#define NOTE_B4  494
#define NOTE_C5  523
#define REST     0

// Melody: frequencies and durations (ms)
int melody[] = {
  NOTE_C4, NOTE_D4, NOTE_E4, NOTE_F4,
  NOTE_G4, NOTE_A4, NOTE_B4, NOTE_C5,
  REST
};
int durations[] = {
  300, 300, 300, 300,
  300, 300, 300, 500,
  200
};
const int NOTE_COUNT = sizeof(melody) / sizeof(melody[0]);

void playTone(int frequency, int duration) {
  if (frequency == REST) {
    ledcWrite(PWM_CHANNEL, 0); // silence
  } else {
    ledcChangeFrequency(PWM_CHANNEL, frequency, PWM_RESOLUTION);
    ledcWrite(PWM_CHANNEL, 128); // 50% duty cycle for loudest passive buzzer tone
  }
  delay(duration);
  ledcWrite(PWM_CHANNEL, 0); // brief silence between notes
  delay(30);
}

void setup() {
  ledcSetup(PWM_CHANNEL, NOTE_C4, PWM_RESOLUTION);
  ledcAttachPin(BUZZER_PIN, PWM_CHANNEL);
}

void loop() {
  for (int i = 0; i < NOTE_COUNT; i++) {
    playTone(melody[i], durations[i]);
  }
  delay(1500); // pause before repeating
}
```

**How it works:** Unlike an active buzzer that buzzes at a fixed frequency, a passive buzzer needs a toggling PWM signal — `ledcChangeFrequency()` is called before each note to shift the PWM frequency to match the desired musical pitch, and a 50% duty cycle (value 128 out of 255) is used because it produces the maximum amplitude swing across the buzzer element.

A relay module with an active-low IN pin is driven directly from an ESP32 GPIO; the module's onboard optocoupler means no extra resistor is needed, but the relay coil is powered from VCC (5V preferred for reliable switching) while the control signal runs at 3.3V logic which most relay modules accept.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | VIN (5V) |
| GND | GND |
| IN | GPIO26 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------+          +--------------------+
|   ESP32        |          |  Relay Module      |
|                |          |                    |
|   VIN (5V) ----+----------+---- VCC            |
|   GND      ----+----------+---- GND            |
|   GPIO26   ----+----------+---- IN             |
|                |          |                    |
|                |          |  [NO / COM / NC]   |
|                |          |  (load terminals)  |
+----------------+          +--------------------+
```
Make sure any load wired through the relay's NO/COM terminals is within the relay's rated voltage and current; never switch mains voltage without proper isolation and enclosure.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int RELAY_PIN = 26;

// Most relay modules are active-LOW:
// sending LOW energizes the coil (relay closes / NO connects to COM)
// sending HIGH de-energizes the coil (relay opens)
const int RELAY_ON  = LOW;
const int RELAY_OFF = HIGH;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(RELAY_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, RELAY_OFF); // start with relay open (safe default)
  Serial.println("Relay control ready.");
}

void loop() {
  // Turn relay ON
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, RELAY_ON);
  Serial.println("Relay ON");
  delay(1000);

  // Turn relay OFF
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, RELAY_OFF);
  Serial.println("Relay OFF");
  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** The `RELAY_ON = LOW` / `RELAY_OFF = HIGH` constants are the key non-obvious detail — most opto-isolated relay modules are active-low, so driving the IN pin LOW energizes the coil, and the safe default in `setup()` explicitly drives the pin HIGH before the loop starts to prevent the relay from momentarily closing during ESP32 boot.

WS2812 strips use a single-wire timed digital protocol, so only one data GPIO is needed, but the strip must be powered from 5V while the ESP32 data line is driven at 3.3V — this works reliably in practice because WS2812 chips accept 3.3V logic as a valid HIGH.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| 5V / VCC | 5V (external supply or USB VBUS) |
| GND | GND (shared with ESP32 GND) |
| DIN (Data In) | GPIO 5 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +---------------------+
        |        ESP32        |
        |                     |
        |   GPIO5 -----------+---> 330Ω --+
        |                    |            |
        |     GND -----------+-------+   |
        +---------------------+      |   |
                                      |   |
              External 5V PSU         |   |
              +----------+            |   |
              | 5V  o----+------+     |   |
              | GND o----+--+   |     |   |
              +----------+  |   |     |   |
                            |   |     |   |
                        +---+---+--+--+---+---+
                        | WS2812 LED Strip     |
                        |  GND   5V    DIN     |
                        +---------------------+
```

Add a 300–500 Ω resistor in series on the data line to protect against ringing, and place a 100–1000 µF capacitor across 5V and GND near the strip to suppress power-supply spikes.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <FastLED.h>

#define LED_PIN     5
#define NUM_LEDS    30
#define BRIGHTNESS  80
#define LED_TYPE    WS2812
#define COLOR_ORDER GRB

CRGB leds[NUM_LEDS];

uint8_t hueOffset = 0;

void setup() {
  FastLED.addLeds<LED_TYPE, LED_PIN, COLOR_ORDER>(leds, NUM_LEDS);
  FastLED.setBrightness(BRIGHTNESS);
  FastLED.clear();
  FastLED.show();
}

void loop() {
  // Spread the full hue wheel across the strip, then rotate each frame
  for (int i = 0; i < NUM_LEDS; i++) {
    uint8_t hue = hueOffset + (uint8_t)((i * 256) / NUM_LEDS);
    leds[i] = CHSV(hue, 255, 255);
  }

  FastLED.show();
  hueOffset++;        // advance the rainbow one step per frame
  delay(20);          // ~50 fps animation speed
}
```

**How it works:** `FastLED.addLeds<WS2812, LED_PIN, GRB>()` handles the precise 800 kHz single-wire bit-banging protocol so you never deal with timing manually. The rainbow effect works by mapping each LED's index to a position in the HSV hue wheel (0–255) and incrementing `hueOffset` each frame, which rotates the entire gradient around the strip continuously.

A 4x4 matrix keypad uses 8 GPIO lines total — 4 row outputs driven low one at a time and 4 column inputs with internal pull-ups — so no extra components are needed beyond direct pin connections.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| R1 (Row 1) | GPIO 19 |
| R2 (Row 2) | GPIO 18 |
| R3 (Row 3) | GPIO 5 |
| R4 (Row 4) | GPIO 17 |
| C1 (Col 1) | GPIO 16 |
| C2 (Col 2) | GPIO 4 |
| C3 (Col 3) | GPIO 2 |
| C4 (Col 4) | GPIO 15 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
 +------------------+          +-------------------+
 |      ESP32       |          |  4x4 Matrix Keypad|
 |                  |          |                   |
 | GPIO19 (Row1) ---+----------+--- R1             |
 | GPIO18 (Row2) ---+----------+--- R2             |
 | GPIO5  (Row3) ---+----------+--- R3             |
 | GPIO17 (Row4) ---+----------+--- R4             |
 |                  |          |                   |
 | GPIO16 (Col1) ---+----------+--- C1             |
 | GPIO4  (Col2) ---+----------+--- C2             |
 | GPIO2  (Col3) ---+----------+--- C3             |
 | GPIO15 (Col4) ---+----------+--- C4             |
 +------------------+          +-------------------+
```

No external resistors are required because the ESP32's internal pull-up resistors are enabled on the column pins in firmware.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// Row and column pin definitions
const int ROW_PINS[4] = {19, 18, 5, 17};
const int COL_PINS[4] = {16, 4, 2, 15};

// Key map matching physical keypad layout
const char KEYS[4][4] = {
  {'1', '2', '3', 'A'},
  {'4', '5', '6', 'B'},
  {'7', '8', '9', 'C'},
  {'*', '0', '#', 'D'}
};

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  // Rows as OUTPUT, default HIGH
  for (int r = 0; r < 4; r++) {
    pinMode(ROW_PINS[r], OUTPUT);
    digitalWrite(ROW_PINS[r], HIGH);
  }

  // Columns as INPUT with internal pull-up
  for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {
    pinMode(COL_PINS[c], INPUT_PULLUP);
  }

  Serial.println("Keypad ready. Press a key:");
}

char scanKeypad() {
  for (int r = 0; r < 4; r++) {
    // Drive current row LOW
    digitalWrite(ROW_PINS[r], LOW);

    for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {
      if (digitalRead(COL_PINS[c]) == LOW) {
        // Debounce: wait for release
        delay(20);
        while (digitalRead(COL_PINS[c]) == LOW);
        delay(20);
        digitalWrite(ROW_PINS[r], HIGH);
        return KEYS[r][c];
      }
    }

    // Restore row to HIGH before scanning next
    digitalWrite(ROW_PINS[r], HIGH);
  }
  return 0; // No key pressed
}

void loop() {
  char key = scanKeypad();
  if (key != 0) {
    Serial.print("Key pressed: ");
    Serial.println(key);
  }
  delay(10);
}
```

**How it works:** The scanning loop drives each row LOW one at a time; if a key is held down, it shorts that row to the corresponding column, pulling the column pin LOW and identifying the exact row/column intersection. The `INPUT_PULLUP` mode on column pins is what keeps unpressed columns reading HIGH, making the LOW detection unambiguous without any external resistors.

Rotary encoders use two quadrature digital signals (CLK and DT) plus a separate active-low button signal, so three independent GPIO inputs are needed — all benefit from internal pull-ups to avoid floating states.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| CLK (A) | GPIO 18 |
| DT (B) | GPIO 19 |
| SW (Button) | GPIO 21 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+--------------------+          +-------------------------+
|   Rotary Encoder   |          |          ESP32          |
|                    |          |                         |
|  VCC o------------|----------| 3.3V                    |
|  GND o------------|----------| GND                     |
|  CLK o------------|----------| GPIO 18 (INPUT_PULLUP)  |
|   DT o------------|----------| GPIO 19 (INPUT_PULLUP)  |
|   SW o------------|----------| GPIO 21 (INPUT_PULLUP)  |
+--------------------+          +-------------------------+
```
Using INPUT_PULLUP on all three pins eliminates the need for external resistors, since the encoder's open-collector outputs are compatible with the ESP32's internal pull-ups.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

#define PIN_CLK 18
#define PIN_DT  19
#define PIN_SW  21

volatile int encoderCount = 0;
volatile bool buttonPressed = false;

int lastClkState;
unsigned long lastDebounceTime = 0;
const unsigned long debounceDelay = 50;

void IRAM_ATTR handleEncoder() {
  int clkState = digitalRead(PIN_CLK);
  int dtState  = digitalRead(PIN_DT);
  if (clkState != lastClkState) {
    if (dtState != clkState) {
      encoderCount++;
    } else {
      encoderCount--;
    }
    lastClkState = clkState;
  }
}

void IRAM_ATTR handleButton() {
  if ((millis() - lastDebounceTime) > debounceDelay) {
    buttonPressed = true;
    lastDebounceTime = millis();
  }
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  pinMode(PIN_CLK, INPUT_PULLUP);
  pinMode(PIN_DT,  INPUT_PULLUP);
  pinMode(PIN_SW,  INPUT_PULLUP);

  lastClkState = digitalRead(PIN_CLK);

  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(PIN_CLK), handleEncoder, CHANGE);
  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(PIN_SW),  handleButton,  FALLING);
}

void loop() {
  static int lastCount = 0;

  if (encoderCount != lastCount) {
    Serial.print("Encoder count: ");
    Serial.println(encoderCount);
    lastCount = encoderCount;
  }

  if (buttonPressed) {
    buttonPressed = false;
    Serial.println("Button pressed!");
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The CLK pin fires an interrupt on every edge (CHANGE), and comparing CLK vs DT state at that moment reveals rotation direction — if they differ, the shaft turned clockwise; if they match, counter-clockwise. The button ISR uses a millis()-based debounce check rather than a blocking delay, which is essential since blocking inside an ISR on the ESP32 can cause a watchdog reset.

A potentiometer is a passive voltage divider, so its wiper output feeds directly into one of the ESP32's ADC-capable GPIO pins — no additional interface hardware needed.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| Left terminal (VCC end) | 3.3V |
| Right terminal (GND end) | GND |
| Wiper (middle pin) | GPIO34 (ADC1_CH6) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------+          +-------------------+
|  Potentiometer |          |       ESP32        |
|                |          |                   |
| VCC terminal --+----------+-- 3.3V            |
|                |          |                   |
| Wiper (mid) ---+----------+-- GPIO34 (ADC)    |
|                |          |                   |
| GND terminal --+----------+-- GND             |
+----------------+          +-------------------+
```
Use the 3.3V rail (not 5V) to power the potentiometer, since the ESP32 ADC inputs are not 5V tolerant and max out at 3.3V.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int POT_PIN = 34;       // ADC1_CH6, input-only GPIO safe for analog reads
const int ADC_MAX  = 4095;    // ESP32 ADC is 12-bit (0–4095)
const float V_REF  = 3.3f;    // reference voltage matches supply rail

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  // GPIO34 is input-only by default; no pinMode needed for analog reads
  analogReadResolution(12);   // explicitly set 12-bit resolution
}

void loop() {
  int   rawValue  = analogRead(POT_PIN);
  float voltage   = (rawValue / (float)ADC_MAX) * V_REF;
  float percent   = (rawValue / (float)ADC_MAX) * 100.0f;

  Serial.printf("Raw: %4d  |  Voltage: %.3f V  |  Position: %.1f%%\n",
                rawValue, voltage, percent);

  delay(200);
}
```

**How it works:** GPIO34 is chosen because it is an input-only pin on the ESP32, which means it has no internal driver circuitry that could interfere with the ADC reading. Calling `analogReadResolution(12)` ensures the full 12-bit range (0–4095) is used, giving finer resolution than the default 10-bit mode on some frameworks.

An LDR forms a voltage divider with a fixed resistor so the ESP32's ADC pin sees an analog voltage that varies with light intensity.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| LDR leg 1 | 3.3V |
| LDR leg 2 | GPIO34 (ADC) and 10kΩ resistor leg 1 |
| 10kΩ resistor leg 2 | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +3.3V
          |
        [LDR]
          |
          +-----> GPIO34 (ADC input)
          |
        [10kΩ]
          |
         GND

  +------------------+
  |      ESP32       |
  |                  |
  |  3.3V o----------+--- LDR ---+
  |                  |           |
  | GPIO34 o---------+       [10kΩ]
  |                              |
  |    GND o--------------------GND
  +------------------+
```
GPIO34 is input-only and has no internal pull-up/pull-down, making it ideal as a clean ADC pin; keep wires to the divider short to reduce noise.

**Code**
```cpp
// LDR voltage divider analog read on ESP32
// LDR between 3.3V and the ADC pin; 10kΩ pull-down to GND

const int LDR_PIN = 34;          // GPIO34 – ADC1_CH6, input-only pin
const int ADC_MAX  = 4095;       // ESP32 ADC is 12-bit
const float V_REF  = 3.3f;       // ADC reference voltage

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  analogReadResolution(12);      // Explicitly set 12-bit resolution
  analogSetAttenuation(ADC_11db); // Full 0–3.3 V input range
}

void loop() {
  int   rawValue  = analogRead(LDR_PIN);
  float voltage   = (rawValue / (float)ADC_MAX) * V_REF;

  // Higher raw value → more voltage across pull-down → brighter light
  // Map to a 0-100 light level percentage
  int lightLevel = map(rawValue, 0, ADC_MAX, 0, 100);

  Serial.print("Raw ADC: ");
  Serial.print(rawValue);
  Serial.print("  Voltage: ");
  Serial.print(voltage, 2);
  Serial.print(" V  Light level: ");
  Serial.print(lightLevel);
  Serial.println("%");

  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** `analogSetAttenuation(ADC_11db)` extends the ESP32 ADC's input range to the full 0–3.3 V so the divider's output is never clipped; without this the default 0–1.1 V range would saturate in bright light. Because the LDR is on the high side of the divider, resistance drops as light increases, pulling more current through the 10 kΩ pull-down and raising the ADC voltage, so a higher raw value correctly maps to a brighter reading.

Capacitive soil moisture sensors output an analog voltage that varies with soil water content, so the signal wire connects directly to one of the ESP32's ADC-capable GPIO pins — the 12-bit ADC gives good resolution for distinguishing wet vs. dry readings.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| AOUT | GPIO34 (ADC1_CH6) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------------+          +-----------------------------+
|  Capacitive Soil Sensor |          |           ESP32             |
|                         |          |                             |
|  VCC  o-----------------+----------o 3.3V                        |
|                         |          |                             |
|  GND  o-----------------+----------o GND                         |
|                         |          |                             |
|  AOUT o-----------------+----------o GPIO34 (ADC1_CH6)           |
|                         |          |                             |
+-------------------------+          +-----------------------------+
```
Use GPIO34 or another ADC1 pin (e.g. 32, 33, 35) — avoid ADC2 pins as they conflict with Wi-Fi if enabled.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// GPIO34 is input-only and ADC1_CH6, safe for analog reads
const int SOIL_PIN = 34;

// Calibrate these values for your specific sensor and soil type:
// AIR_VALUE   = raw ADC reading when sensor is dry (in air)
// WATER_VALUE = raw ADC reading when sensor is fully submerged
const int AIR_VALUE   = 3100;  // adjust after measuring
const int WATER_VALUE =  950;  // adjust after measuring

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  // No pinMode needed for analog input on ESP32
  analogReadResolution(12);       // 12-bit: values 0–4095
  analogSetAttenuation(ADC_11db); // full 0–3.3V input range
}

void loop() {
  int rawValue = analogRead(SOIL_PIN);

  // Map raw reading to 0–100% moisture (note: sensor output is inverted)
  int moisturePercent = map(rawValue, AIR_VALUE, WATER_VALUE, 0, 100);
  moisturePercent = constrain(moisturePercent, 0, 100);

  Serial.print("Raw ADC: ");
  Serial.print(rawValue);
  Serial.print("  |  Moisture: ");
  Serial.print(moisturePercent);
  Serial.println("%");

  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** The `map()` call inverts the relationship — capacitive sensors output a *higher* voltage when dry and *lower* when wet, so AIR_VALUE maps to 0% and WATER_VALUE maps to 100%. The `analogSetAttenuation(ADC_11db)` line is critical because without it the ESP32's ADC defaults to a ~1V input range, clipping most of the sensor's output swing.

The A4988 driver only needs two logic signals from the ESP32 (STEP and DIR) to control the motor, while the motor coils are powered from a separate higher-voltage supply through the driver's VMOT pin.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| STEP | GPIO 26 |
| DIR | GPIO 27 |
| SLEEP | 3.3V (always awake) |
| RESET | 3.3V (tie to SLEEP) |
| GND (logic) | GND |
| VMOT | External motor supply (8–35V) |
| GND (motor) | External motor supply GND |
| 1A, 1B | Stepper coil A (two wires) |
| 2A, 2B | Stepper coil B (two wires) |
| VDD | 3.3V |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------------+          +---------------------+
|        ESP32         |          |    A4988 Driver     |
|                      |          |                     |
|  GPIO26 (STEP) ------+----------+-- STEP              |
|  GPIO27 (DIR)  ------+----------+-- DIR               |
|  3.3V          ------+----+-----+-- VDD               |
|                      |   |      +-- SLEEP <---+        |
|  GND           ------+---+------+-- GND(logic)|        |
|                      |          +-- RESET ----+        |
+----------------------+          |                     |
                                  |  VMOT <--- V+ (ext) |
                                  |  GND  <--- GND(ext) |
                                  |                     |
                                  |  1A ---|            |
                                  |  1B ---+-- Coil A   |
                                  |  2A ---|            |
                                  |  2B ---+-- Coil B   |
                                  +---------------------+
                                       |
                              [Bipolar Stepper Motor]
```
Place a 100µF capacitor across VMOT and GND (motor side) close to the driver to suppress voltage spikes when the motor steps.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int STEP_PIN = 26;
const int DIR_PIN  = 27;

// Number of steps per revolution depends on motor (typically 200 for 1.8°)
// A4988 MS1/MS2/MS3 pins are left floating = full-step mode here
const int STEPS_PER_REV = 200;

// Delay between step pulses in microseconds (controls speed)
// Smaller = faster. Do not go below ~1000us without load testing.
const int STEP_DELAY_US = 2000;

void stepMotor(int steps, bool clockwise) {
  digitalWrite(DIR_PIN, clockwise ? HIGH : LOW);
  // A4988 needs at least 1µs setup time on DIR before first STEP pulse
  delayMicroseconds(2);

  for (int i = 0; i < steps; i++) {
    // STEP pulse must be at least 1µs high; we use 5µs to be safe
    digitalWrite(STEP_PIN, HIGH);
    delayMicroseconds(5);
    digitalWrite(STEP_PIN, LOW);
    delayMicroseconds(STEP_DELAY_US);
  }
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(STEP_PIN, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(DIR_PIN,  OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(STEP_PIN, LOW);
  digitalWrite(DIR_PIN,  LOW);
  Serial.println("A4988 stepper test starting...");
}

void loop() {
  Serial.println("One revolution clockwise");
  stepMotor(STEPS_PER_REV, true);
  delay(1000);

  Serial.println("One revolution counter-clockwise");
  stepMotor(STEPS_PER_REV, false);
  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** The A4988 advances the motor by exactly one step (in whatever micro-step mode is selected by MS1/MS2/MS3) on each rising edge of the STEP pin, so speed is entirely controlled by the delay between pulses rather than by a library—the 2 µs DIR setup guard before the first pulse prevents the driver from misreading direction on fast transitions.

