Keep the IR receiver away from strong ambient light sources and point it toward the expected remote direction for reliable reception.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <IRremoteESP8266.h>
#include <IRrecv.h>
#include <IRutils.h>

const uint16_t IR_RECV_PIN = 15;
const uint16_t CAPTURE_BUFFER_SIZE = 1024;

IRrecv irrecv(IR_RECV_PIN, CAPTURE_BUFFER_SIZE, 15, true);
decode_results results;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  irrecv.enableIRIn(); // Start the receiver
  Serial.println("IR Receiver ready. Point remote and press a button.");
}

void loop() {
  if (irrecv.decode(&results)) {
    Serial.print("Protocol : ");
    Serial.println(typeToString(results.decode_type));
    Serial.print("Code     : 0x");
    Serial.println(results.value, HEX);
    Serial.print("Bits     : ");
    Serial.println(results.bits);
    Serial.println("---");
    irrecv.resume(); // Ready to receive the next signal
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The `IRremoteESP8266` library uses a hardware timer interrupt to precisely time the incoming pulse train on GPIO 15, automatically identifying the modulation protocol (NEC, SONY, RC5, etc.) and decoding the raw bit pattern into a human-readable hex value. Calling `irrecv.resume()` after each decode is essential — without it the receive buffer is never cleared and no further signals are captured.

