**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int PIN_VRX = 34;   // X-axis analog input
const int PIN_VRY = 35;   // Y-axis analog input
const int PIN_SW  = 32;   // Joystick button (active LOW)

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(PIN_VRX, INPUT);
  pinMode(PIN_VRY, INPUT);
  pinMode(PIN_SW,  INPUT_PULLUP);  // Button pulls to GND when pressed
}

void loop() {
  int xValue  = analogRead(PIN_VRX);  // 0 – 4095
  int yValue  = analogRead(PIN_VRY);  // 0 – 4095
  bool pressed = (digitalRead(PIN_SW) == LOW);

  // Map raw ADC values to a -100..100 range centered at rest position
  int xMapped = map(xValue, 0, 4095, -100, 100);
  int yMapped = map(yValue, 0, 4095, -100, 100);

  Serial.print("X: ");
  Serial.print(xMapped);
  Serial.print("  Y: ");
  Serial.print(yMapped);
  Serial.print("  Button: ");
  Serial.println(pressed ? "PRESSED" : "released");

  delay(100);
}
```

**How it works:** The ESP32's ADC returns 12-bit values (0–4095), so `map()` re-scales both axes to a signed –100 to 100 range, making center-stick near zero intuitive for downstream control logic. `INPUT_PULLUP` on the button pin keeps it HIGH at rest, so a LOW reading unambiguously indicates a press without any external resistor.

The I2C backpack reduces the LCD connection from 6+ GPIO lines down to just the two shared I2C bus lines (SDA/SCL), so any other I2C devices on the same bus can coexist without additional wiring.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | GPIO 21 (SDA) |
| SCL | GPIO 22 (SCL) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
 +----------------------+          +-------------------+
 | ESP32                |          | 16x2 LCD          |
 |                      |          | (I2C Backpack)    |
 |           GPIO 21 (SDA)---------(SDA               |
 |           GPIO 22 (SCL)---------(SCL               |
 |                  5V ------------(VCC               |
 |                 GND ------------(GND               |
 +----------------------+          +-------------------+
```

The I2C backpack typically operates the LCD at 5V; SDA/SCL signals from the ESP32 at 3.3V are usually accepted by the PCF8574 I/O expander on the backpack, but verify your specific module's logic-level tolerance.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <LiquidCrystal_I2C.h>

// Most I2C LCD backpacks use address 0x27; try 0x3F if display is blank
#define LCD_I2C_ADDRESS 0x27
#define LCD_COLUMNS     16
#define LCD_ROWS         2

LiquidCrystal_I2C lcd(LCD_I2C_ADDRESS, LCD_COLUMNS, LCD_ROWS);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  // Initialize I2C on ESP32 default pins: SDA=21, SCL=22
  Wire.begin(21, 22);

  lcd.init();
  lcd.backlight();

  lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
  lcd.print("Hello, ESP32!");

  lcd.setCursor(0, 1);
  lcd.print("I2C LCD Ready");
}

void loop() {
  // Scroll a counter on row 1 every second
  static uint32_t lastMillis = 0;
  static uint32_t count = 0;

  if (millis() - lastMillis >= 1000) {
    lastMillis = millis();
    count++;

    lcd.setCursor(0, 1);
    lcd.print("Count: ");
    lcd.print(count);
    // Pad with spaces to clear leftover digits
    lcd.print("         ");
  }
}
```

**How it works:** `lcd.init()` sends the initialization sequence over I2C to the PCF8574 expander on the backpack, which then toggles the LCD's parallel data lines internally — you never bit-bang the LCD directly. The trailing spaces after `lcd.print(count)` are the simplest way to erase leftover characters when the printed string shrinks, since the LCD has no auto-clear on partial writes.

Both the BME280 and MPU6050 operate at 3.3V and share the I2C bus; since they have different default addresses (0x76 and 0x68 respectively), they coexist on SDA/SCL without any address conflicts.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| BME280 VCC | 3.3V |
| BME280 GND | GND |
| BME280 SDA | GPIO 21 |
| BME280 SCL | GPIO 22 |
| MPU6050 VCC | 3.3V |
| MPU6050 GND | GND |
| MPU6050 SDA | GPIO 21 |
| MPU6050 SCL | GPIO 22 |
| MPU6050 AD0 | GND (sets address to 0x68) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +----------------------+
        |        ESP32         |
        |                     |
        |   3.3V |---+---+    |
        |        |   |   |    |
        |   GND  |---+---+    |
        |        |   |   |    |
        | GPIO21 |---+---+    |  (SDA)
        | GPIO22 |---+---+    |  (SCL)
        +------|---|---|--+---+
               |   |   |
         +-----+   |   +-------+
         |         |           |
  +------+------+  |  +--------+------+
  |   BME280    |  |  |    MPU6050    |
  |             |  |  |               |
  | VCC -> 3.3V |  |  | VCC -> 3.3V   |
  | GND -> GND  |  |  | GND -> GND    |
  | SDA -> 21   |  |  | SDA -> 21     |
  | SCL -> 22   |  |  | SCL -> 22     |
  | (addr 0x76) |  |  | AD0 -> GND    |
  +-------------+  |  | (addr 0x68)   |
                   |  +---------------+
                   |
         (shared I2C bus)
```

Both devices are 3.3V only — do not connect VCC to 5V, as this will damage them.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_BME280.h>
#include <Adafruit_MPU6050.h>
#include <Adafruit_Sensor.h>

#define SDA_PIN 21
#define SCL_PIN 22

#define BME280_I2C_ADDR 0x76

Adafruit_BME280 bme;
Adafruit_MPU6050 mpu;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Wire.begin(SDA_PIN, SCL_PIN);

  // Initialize BME280
  if (!bme.begin(BME280_I2C_ADDR, &Wire)) {
    Serial.println("BME280 not found! Check wiring.");
    while (1) delay(10);
  }
  Serial.println("BME280 initialized.");

  // Initialize MPU6050
  if (!mpu.begin(0x68, &Wire)) {
    Serial.println("MPU6050 not found! Check wiring.");
    while (1) delay(10);
  }
  mpu.setAccelerometerRange(MPU6050_RANGE_8_G);
  mpu.setGyroRange(MPU6050_RANGE_500_DEG);
  mpu.setFilterBandwidth(MPU6050_BAND_21_HZ);
  Serial.println("MPU6050 initialized.");
}

void loop() {
  // --- BME280 readings ---
  float temperature = bme.readTemperature();    // Celsius
  float humidity    = bme.readHumidity();       // %RH
  float pressure    = bme.readPressure() / 100.0F; // hPa

  Serial.println("=== BME280 ===");
  Serial.printf("  Temp:     %.2f C\n", temperature);
  Serial.printf("  Humidity: %.2f %%\n", humidity);
  Serial.printf("  Pressure: %.2f hPa\n", pressure);

  // --- MPU6050 readings ---
  sensors_event_t accel, gyro, temp_event;
  mpu.getEvent(&accel, &gyro, &temp_event);

  Serial.println("=== MPU6050 ===");
  Serial.printf("  Accel X: %.3f  Y: %.3f  Z: %.3f m/s^2\n",
    accel.acceleration.x, accel.acceleration.y, accel.acceleration.z);
  Serial.printf("  Gyro  X: %.3f  Y: %.3f  Z: %.3f rad/s\n",
    gyro.gyro.x, gyro.gyro.y, gyro.gyro.z);
  Serial.printf("  Temp: %.2f C\n", temp_event.temperature);

  Serial.println();
  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** Passing `&Wire` explicitly to both `bme.begin()` and `mpu.begin()` ensures both drivers share the single `Wire` instance initialised with the custom SDA/SCL pins, rather than defaulting to separate or incorrect buses. The `Wire.begin(SDA_PIN, SCL_PIN)` call must come before either sensor is initialised so the bus is ready when each device is probed at its address.

The ACS712 outputs an analog voltage centered at VCC/2 (nominally 2.5V at 5V supply), so the ESP32's ADC reads the offset signal — note the ESP32 ADC is 3.3V max, so power the ACS712 from 3.3V to keep the output safely within range.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| OUT | GPIO34 (ADC1_CH6) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------+          +----------------------+
|   ACS712       |          |        ESP32         |
|                |          |                      |
|  VCC  o--------+----------o 3.3V                 |
|  GND  o--------+----------o GND                  |
|  OUT  o-------------------o GPIO34 (ADC1_CH6)    |
|                |          |                      |
+----------------+          +----------------------+
```
Powering the ACS712 from 3.3V instead of 5V keeps the analog output within the ESP32's ADC input range and avoids the need for a voltage divider.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// ACS712 wiring
const int ACS712_PIN = 34;       // ADC1 channel, input-only GPIO safe for ADC

// ACS712-05B sensitivity: 185 mV/A (use 100 mV/A for 20A, 66 mV/A for 30A variant)
const float SENSITIVITY_MV_PER_A = 185.0f;

// With 3.3V supply, quiescent (zero-current) output is ~1.65V (VCC/2)
const float ZERO_CURRENT_VOLTAGE = 1.65f;  // volts

// ESP32 ADC: 12-bit (0-4095) over 0-3.3V
const float ADC_REFERENCE_V = 3.3f;
const int   ADC_RESOLUTION   = 4096;

// Number of samples to average to reduce ADC noise
const int NUM_SAMPLES = 200;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  // GPIO34 is input-only — no pinMode needed, but explicit is fine
  pinMode(ACS712_PIN, INPUT);
  Serial.println("ACS712 Current Sensor Ready");
}

float readCurrentAmps() {
  long sum = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < NUM_SAMPLES; i++) {
    sum += analogRead(ACS712_PIN);
    delayMicroseconds(100);  // small delay between samples
  }
  float avgRaw = (float)sum / NUM_SAMPLES;

  // Convert ADC count to voltage
  float voltage = (avgRaw / ADC_RESOLUTION) * ADC_REFERENCE_V;

  // Subtract quiescent offset voltage, then divide by sensitivity
  float current = (voltage - ZERO_CURRENT_VOLTAGE) / (SENSITIVITY_MV_PER_A / 1000.0f);
  return current;
}

void loop() {
  float current = readCurrentAmps();
  Serial.print("Current: ");
  Serial.print(current, 3);
  Serial.println(" A");
  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** The key step is subtracting `ZERO_CURRENT_VOLTAGE` (VCC/2 = 1.65V) before dividing by the sensitivity, because the ACS712 outputs a bipolar signal centered at half-supply rather than starting at 0V. Averaging 200 ADC samples significantly reduces the ESP32 ADC's inherent noise and gives a more stable current reading.

The rain sensor module provides both a digital threshold output and an analog voltage output; here we use the analog pin connected to one of the ESP32's ADC-capable GPIOs so we can read a continuous 0–4095 value representing rain intensity rather than just a wet/dry boolean.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| AO (Analog Out) | GPIO34 (ADC1_CH6) |
| DO (Digital Out) | Not connected (optional: GPIO26) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +--------------------+          +---------------------------+
        |   Rain Sensor      |          |          ESP32            |
        |   Module           |          |                           |
        |                    |          |                           |
        |  VCC o-------------|----------| 3.3V                      |
        |  GND o-------------|----------| GND                       |
        |   AO o-------------|----------| GPIO34 (ADC)              |
        |   DO o   (unused)  |          |                           |
        +--------------------+          +---------------------------+
```
Note: GPIO34 is input-only on the ESP32 and ADC1-capable, making it safe for analog reads without interference from Wi-Fi; avoid ADC2 pins if Wi-Fi is in use.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int RAIN_ANALOG_PIN = 34;  // ADC1_CH6, input-only GPIO
const int DRY_THRESHOLD   = 3500; // tweak based on your sensor calibration
const int WET_THRESHOLD   = 1500; // below this = heavy rain

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  // GPIO34 is input-only; no pinMode needed, but setting explicitly is harmless
  pinMode(RAIN_ANALOG_PIN, INPUT);
  Serial.println("Rain Sensor Ready");
}

void loop() {
  int rawValue = analogRead(RAIN_ANALOG_PIN); // 0 (wet) to 4095 (dry)

  Serial.print("Raw ADC value: ");
  Serial.print(rawValue);

  if (rawValue >= DRY_THRESHOLD) {
    Serial.println(" -> No rain (dry)");
  } else if (rawValue >= WET_THRESHOLD) {
    Serial.println(" -> Light rain");
  } else {
    Serial.println(" -> Heavy rain");
  }

  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** The rain sensor's conductive traces increase resistance as water bridges them, dropping the voltage on the AO pin — so a lower ADC reading (closer to 0) means more rain, which is why the thresholds are inverted from what you might expect. Using GPIO34 (ADC1) rather than any ADC2 pin avoids the known ESP32 conflict where ADC2 is disabled when the Wi-Fi radio is active.

