Servo motors draw significant current during movement; power them from a dedicated 5 V supply (≥1 A) rather than the ESP32's onboard regulator to avoid brownouts.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// GPIO pins for each servo signal
const int PAN_PIN  = 18;
const int TILT_PIN = 19;

// LEDC channels (ESP32 has 16; pick any two)
const int PAN_CHANNEL  = 0;
const int TILT_CHANNEL = 1;

// 50 Hz PWM for standard servos, 16-bit resolution
const int SERVO_FREQ       = 50;
const int SERVO_RESOLUTION = 16;  // 65535 ticks per period

// At 50 Hz, one period = 20 ms = 65535 ticks
// Typical SG90: 0° ≈ 1 ms pulse, 180° ≈ 2 ms pulse
// duty = (pulse_ms / 20 ms) * 65535
const int DUTY_MIN  = 1638;  // ~1.0 ms → 0°
const int DUTY_MAX  = 4915;  // ~2.0 ms (some SG90s go to ~2.4 ms) → 180°

// Convert angle (0–180) to LEDC duty count
int angleToDuty(int angle) {
  return map(angle, 0, 180, DUTY_MIN, DUTY_MAX);
}

void setServo(int channel, int angle) {
  angle = constrain(angle, 0, 180);
  ledcWrite(channel, angleToDuty(angle));
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  // Configure LEDC channels
  ledcSetup(PAN_CHANNEL,  SERVO_FREQ, SERVO_RESOLUTION);
  ledcSetup(TILT_CHANNEL, SERVO_FREQ, SERVO_RESOLUTION);

  // Attach channels to GPIO pins
  ledcAttachPin(PAN_PIN,  PAN_CHANNEL);
  ledcAttachPin(TILT_PIN, TILT_CHANNEL);

  // Start both servos at center position
  setServo(PAN_CHANNEL,  90);
  setServo(TILT_CHANNEL, 90);
  delay(500);
}

void loop() {
  // Sweep pan 0→180 while tilt does inverse 180→0
  for (int angle = 0; angle <= 180; angle++) {
    setServo(PAN_CHANNEL,  angle);
    setServo(TILT_CHANNEL, 180 - angle);
    delay(12);
  }

  // Reverse: pan 180→0, tilt 0→180
  for (int angle = 180; angle >= 0; angle--) {
    setServo(PAN_CHANNEL,  angle);
    setServo(TILT_CHANNEL, 180 - angle);
    delay(12);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The ESP32 lacks a traditional analogWrite, so `ledcSetup` / `ledcWrite` from the Arduino-ESP32 LEDC API generate the precise 50 Hz PWM that servos expect — the 16-bit resolution gives fine enough granularity (≈0.003 ms per tick) that the `map()` call from angle to duty count produces smooth, stutter-free motion. Using two independent LEDC channels means both servos receive their own unlinked PWM signals and can be positioned simultaneously without one blocking the other.

The L298N uses two digital direction pins plus a PWM-capable enable pin per motor channel, so any three ESP32 GPIOs work — just make sure the enable pin is on a PWM-capable output (almost all ESP32 GPIOs support this via ledcWrite).

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| IN1 | GPIO 26 |
| IN2 | GPIO 27 |
| ENA | GPIO 14 (PWM) |
| GND | GND |
| 5V (logic) | 5V (VIN) |
| OUT1 | DC Motor terminal A |
| OUT2 | DC Motor terminal B |
| 12V | External motor supply + |
| GND (power) | External motor supply − |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +---------------------+
        |        ESP32        |
        |                     |
        |  GPIO26 (IN1) ------+----------> IN1 ---+
        |  GPIO27 (IN2) ------+----------> IN2 ---+---[ L298N ]---+
        |  GPIO14 (ENA) ------+----------> ENA ---+               |
        |  GND   -------------+----------> GND    |    OUT1 ------+---[ DC Motor ]---+
        |  5V (VIN) ----------+----------> 5V     |    OUT2 ------+                  |
        +---------------------+           |       |               +------------------+
                                          |  12V <--- External PSU +
                                          |  GND <--- External PSU −
                                          +-------+
```
The L298N logic supply (5V pin) can be powered from the ESP32's 5V/VIN rail, but the motor power (12V terminal) must come from a separate supply sized for your motor — never feed motor current through the ESP32.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// L298N control pins
const int IN1_PIN = 26;
const int IN2_PIN = 27;
const int ENA_PIN = 14;

// LEDC (PWM) settings
const int PWM_CHANNEL = 0;
const int PWM_FREQ    = 1000;  // 1 kHz
const int PWM_RES     = 8;     // 8-bit: 0–255

void motorForward(uint8_t speed) {
  digitalWrite(IN1_PIN, HIGH);
  digitalWrite(IN2_PIN, LOW);
  ledcWrite(PWM_CHANNEL, speed);
}

void motorReverse(uint8_t speed) {
  digitalWrite(IN1_PIN, LOW);
  digitalWrite(IN2_PIN, HIGH);
  ledcWrite(PWM_CHANNEL, speed);
}

void motorStop() {
  digitalWrite(IN1_PIN, LOW);
  digitalWrite(IN2_PIN, LOW);
  ledcWrite(PWM_CHANNEL, 0);
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  pinMode(IN1_PIN, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(IN2_PIN, OUTPUT);

  // Attach LEDC PWM channel to ENA pin
  ledcSetup(PWM_CHANNEL, PWM_FREQ, PWM_RES);
  ledcAttachPin(ENA_PIN, PWM_CHANNEL);

  motorStop();
  Serial.println("L298N DC Motor Control Ready");
}

void loop() {
  Serial.println("Forward at 75% speed...");
  motorForward(191);   // 191/255 ≈ 75%
  delay(3000);

  Serial.println("Stopping...");
  motorStop();
  delay(1000);

  Serial.println("Reverse at 50% speed...");
  motorReverse(127);   // 127/255 ≈ 50%
  delay(3000);

  Serial.println("Stopping...");
  motorStop();
  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** The ESP32's `ledcWrite()` drives the ENA pin with a PWM duty cycle (0–255) that the L298N uses to vary motor speed, while `IN1`/`IN2` are set as a complementary pair — `HIGH/LOW` for forward, `LOW/HIGH` for reverse — so flipping their logic levels reverses the current through the motor and changes direction without any additional hardware.

Each HC-SR04 needs its own dedicated TRIG and ECHO GPIO pair since the sensors operate independently with no shared bus, and the ECHO pin outputs 5V logic which is marginally safe on most ESP32 boards but a voltage divider is strongly recommended for long-term reliability.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| Sensor A VCC | 5V |
| Sensor A GND | GND |
| Sensor A TRIG | GPIO 5 |
| Sensor A ECHO | GPIO 18 (via 1kΩ + 2kΩ voltage divider to GND) |
| Sensor B VCC | 5V |
| Sensor B GND | GND |
| Sensor B TRIG | GPIO 19 |
| Sensor B ECHO | GPIO 21 (via 1kΩ + 2kΩ voltage divider to GND) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +---------------------------+
        |          ESP32            |
        |                           |
        |  GPIO5  |-----------------|----> TRIG  [HC-SR04 Sensor A]
        |  GPIO18 |<---[1kΩ]-------+---- ECHO   [HC-SR04 Sensor A]
        |         |        |        |       VCC ---> 5V
        |         |       [2kΩ]     |       GND ---> GND
        |         |        |        |
        |         |       GND       |
        |                           |
        |  GPIO19 |-----------------|----> TRIG  [HC-SR04 Sensor B]
        |  GPIO21 |<---[1kΩ]-------+---- ECHO   [HC-SR04 Sensor B]
        |         |        |        |       VCC ---> 5V
        |         |       [2kΩ]     |       GND ---> GND
        |         |        |        |
        |   5V    |       GND      5V
        |   GND   |                 |
        +---------+-----------------+
```
The HC-SR04 ECHO pin outputs 5V logic; use a resistor voltage divider (1kΩ series + 2kΩ to GND) on each ECHO line to bring the signal down to ~3.3V before it reaches the ESP32 GPIO.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// Sensor A pins
const int TRIG_A = 5;
const int ECHO_A = 18;

// Sensor B pins
const int TRIG_B = 19;
const int ECHO_B = 21;

// Speed of sound in cm/us
const float SOUND_SPEED_CM_US = 0.0343f;

// Timeout: ~38ms pulse = ~650cm max range, add margin
const unsigned long ECHO_TIMEOUT_US = 25000UL;

float measureDistance(int trigPin, int echoPin) {
  // Ensure trigger is low before pulse
  digitalWrite(trigPin, LOW);
  delayMicroseconds(2);

  // Send 10us HIGH pulse to trigger
  digitalWrite(trigPin, HIGH);
  delayMicroseconds(10);
  digitalWrite(trigPin, LOW);

  // Measure echo pulse duration
  unsigned long duration = pulseIn(echoPin, HIGH, ECHO_TIMEOUT_US);

  if (duration == 0) {
    return -1.0f; // timeout / no object detected
  }

  // Distance = (duration * speed) / 2  (divide by 2 for round trip)
  return (duration * SOUND_SPEED_CM_US) / 2.0f;
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  pinMode(TRIG_A, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(ECHO_A, INPUT);

  pinMode(TRIG_B, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(ECHO_B, INPUT);

  Serial.println("Dual HC-SR04 Ultrasonic Sensor Ready");
}

void loop() {
  float distA = measureDistance(TRIG_A, ECHO_A);
  // Small delay between sensors to prevent cross-talk
  delay(30);
  float distB = measureDistance(TRIG_B, ECHO_B);

  Serial.print("Sensor A: ");
  if (distA < 0) {
    Serial.print("Out of range");
  } else {
    Serial.print(distA, 1);
    Serial.print(" cm");
  }

  Serial.print("  |  Sensor B: ");
  if (distB < 0) {
    Serial.print("Out of range");
  } else {
    Serial.print(distB, 1);
    Serial.print(" cm");
  }

  Serial.println();
  delay(200);
}
```

**How it works:** The `pulseIn()` call with the `ECHO_TIMEOUT_US` argument is critical — without a timeout, the ESP32 would block indefinitely if no echo is received (object out of range or absent), returning 0 which the code maps to -1 to signal an invalid reading. The 30ms delay between firing each sensor prevents acoustic cross-talk, where the echo from Sensor A could accidentally trigger Sensor B's ECHO pin.

The e-paper display uses SPI plus three extra control lines (DC, RST, BUSY) that are unique to e-ink panels and must be handled carefully to avoid driving the display while it is refreshing.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SCK | GPIO 18 (SPI SCK) |
| MOSI | GPIO 23 (SPI MOSI) |
| CS | GPIO 5 |
| DC | GPIO 17 |
| RST | GPIO 16 |
| BUSY | GPIO 4 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+---------------------+          +----------------------+
|        ESP32        |          |  2.9" e-Paper (SPI)  |
|                     |          |                      |
|          3.3V |-----+----------| VCC                  |
|            GND|-----+----------| GND                  |
|    GPIO18 SCK |------------>---| SCK                  |
|   GPIO23 MOSI |------------>---| MOSI                 |
|         GPIO5 |------------>---| CS                   |
|        GPIO17 |------------>---| DC                   |
|        GPIO16 |------------>---| RST                  |
|         GPIO4 |---<------------| BUSY                 |
+---------------------+          +----------------------+
```
The e-paper controller runs on 3.3 V only — never connect VCC to 5 V or you will permanently damage the display.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <SPI.h>
#include <GxEPD2_BW.h>
#include <Fonts/FreeMonoBold9pt7b.h>

// Pin definitions
#define EPD_CS   5
#define EPD_DC   17
#define EPD_RST  16
#define EPD_BUSY 4

// GxEPD2 display object for a common 2.9" 296x128 panel (GDEH029A1 / similar)
// Adjust the driver class to match your exact panel if different
GxEPD2_BW<GxEPD2_290_BS, GxEPD2_290_BS::HEIGHT> display(
    GxEPD2_290_BS(EPD_CS, EPD_DC, EPD_RST, EPD_BUSY));

void setup() {
    Serial.begin(115200);

    // Start SPI on default ESP32 VSPI pins (SCK=18, MOSI=23)
    SPI.begin(18, -1, 23, EPD_CS); // SCK, MISO(unused), MOSI, SS

    display.init(115200);
    display.setRotation(1);           // landscape orientation
    display.setFont(&FreeMonoBold9pt7b);
    display.setTextColor(GxEPD_BLACK);

    // Draw a full-screen update with text
    display.setFullWindow();
    display.firstPage();
    do {
        display.fillScreen(GxEPD_WHITE);
        display.setCursor(10, 40);
        display.print("Hello, ESP32!");
        display.setCursor(10, 70);
        display.print("e-Paper SPI Demo");
    } while (display.nextPage());

    Serial.println("Display update complete.");
}

void loop() {
    // e-paper retains image without power; nothing to do here
    delay(10000);
}
```

**How it works:** The `firstPage()`/`nextPage()` loop is the GxEPD2 library's paged-drawing pattern — it internally waits on the BUSY pin between each page so the ESP32 never pushes new SPI data while the high-voltage refresh waveform is still running inside the panel. Passing `-1` for MISO in `SPI.begin()` tells the ESP32 SPI driver that the MISO line is unused, which is correct because e-paper displays are purely write-only devices.

Each color channel of the RGB LED gets its own PWM-capable GPIO with a current-limiting resistor in series, and the common cathode ties to ground.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| R (Red anode) | GPIO 25 (via 100Ω resistor) |
| G (Green anode) | GPIO 26 (via 100Ω resistor) |
| B (Blue anode) | GPIO 27 (via 100Ω resistor) |
| Cathode (common) | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------+          100Ω        +------------------+
|            GPIO25|---/\/\/\------------|R                 |
|            GPIO26|---/\/\/\------------|G   RGB LED       |
|   ESP32    GPIO27|---/\/\/\------------|B  (common cathode)|
|               GND|---------------------|Cathode           |
+----------------+                       +------------------+
```

Use resistors of at least 47Ω–150Ω per channel to limit current; 100Ω is a safe choice for 3.3V GPIO outputs to avoid exceeding the LED's ~20mA rating.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// PWM channel assignments (ESP32 has 16 LEDC channels)
const int RED_PIN   = 25;
const int GREEN_PIN = 26;
const int BLUE_PIN  = 27;

const int RED_CH   = 0;
const int GREEN_CH = 1;
const int BLUE_CH  = 2;

const int PWM_FREQ       = 5000;  // 5 kHz
const int PWM_RESOLUTION = 8;     // 8-bit: 0–255

void setColor(uint8_t r, uint8_t g, uint8_t b) {
  ledcWrite(RED_CH,   r);
  ledcWrite(GREEN_CH, g);
  ledcWrite(BLUE_CH,  b);
}

void setup() {
  // Attach each GPIO to a dedicated LEDC channel
  ledcSetup(RED_CH,   PWM_FREQ, PWM_RESOLUTION);
  ledcSetup(GREEN_CH, PWM_FREQ, PWM_RESOLUTION);
  ledcSetup(BLUE_CH,  PWM_FREQ, PWM_RESOLUTION);

  ledcAttachPin(RED_PIN,   RED_CH);
  ledcAttachPin(GREEN_PIN, GREEN_CH);
  ledcAttachPin(BLUE_PIN,  BLUE_CH);
}

void loop() {
  // Cycle through a few mixed colors
  setColor(255, 0,   0);    delay(1000); // Red
  setColor(0,   255, 0);    delay(1000); // Green
  setColor(0,   0,   255);  delay(1000); // Blue
  setColor(255, 255, 0);    delay(1000); // Yellow
  setColor(0,   255, 255);  delay(1000); // Cyan
  setColor(255, 0,   255);  delay(1000); // Magenta
  setColor(255, 255, 255);  delay(1000); // White

  // Smooth red fade
  for (int val = 0; val <= 255; val++) {
    setColor(val, 0, 0);
    delay(8);
  }
  for (int val = 255; val >= 0; val--) {
    setColor(val, 0, 0);
    delay(8);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The ESP32's LEDC peripheral requires each GPIO to be explicitly bound to a numbered channel via `ledcSetup()` and `ledcAttachPin()` before `ledcWrite()` will produce PWM — unlike AVR Arduino where `analogWrite()` works directly on a pin. Mixing 8-bit duty cycle values across the three channels lets you produce any of 16 million colors by adjusting relative brightness.

The MPU6050 communicates over I2C, so it connects directly to the M5Stack Core2's Port A Grove connector (GPIO32/GPIO33), keeping it off the internal bus used by the AXP192 and touch controller.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to M5Stack Core2 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V (Grove Port A) |
| GND | GND (Grove Port A) |
| SDA | GPIO32 (Port A SDA) |
| SCL | GPIO33 (Port A SCL) |
| AD0 | GND (sets I2C address to 0x68) |
| INT | Not connected (optional) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+--------------------+          +-------------------+
|   M5Stack Core2    |          |     MPU6050       |
|                    |          |                   |
|  Port A (Grove)    |          |                   |
|  GPIO32 (SDA) -----+----------+--- SDA            |
|  GPIO33 (SCL) -----+----------+--- SCL            |
|  3.3V         -----+----------+--- VCC            |
|  GND          -----+----------+--- GND            |
|                    |          |--- AD0 --- GND    |
|                    |          |--- INT (NC)       |
+--------------------+          +-------------------+
```

The MPU6050 is a 3.3V I2C device; do not supply 5V to its VCC pin, and tying AD0 to GND fixes the I2C address at 0x68 (pulling it high would select 0x69 instead).

**Code**
```cpp
#include <M5Core2.h>
#include <Wire.h>

// MPU6050 I2C address with AD0 tied to GND
#define MPU6050_ADDR     0x68

// Register addresses
#define REG_PWR_MGMT_1   0x6B
#define REG_ACCEL_XOUT_H 0x3B
#define REG_GYRO_XOUT_H  0x43

// Scale factors for default full-scale ranges
// Accel: ±2g  -> 16384 LSB/g
// Gyro:  ±250 deg/s -> 131 LSB/(deg/s)
const float ACCEL_SCALE = 16384.0f;
const float GYRO_SCALE  = 131.0f;

void writeRegister(uint8_t reg, uint8_t value) {
  Wire.beginTransmission(MPU6050_ADDR);
  Wire.write(reg);
  Wire.write(value);
  Wire.endTransmission();
}

bool readBytes(uint8_t startReg, uint8_t* buf, uint8_t len) {
  Wire.beginTransmission(MPU6050_ADDR);
  Wire.write(startReg);
  if (Wire.endTransmission(false) != 0) return false;
  Wire.requestFrom((uint8_t)MPU6050_ADDR, len);
  for (uint8_t i = 0; i < len && Wire.available(); i++) {
    buf[i] = Wire.read();
  }
  return true;
}

int16_t combineBytes(uint8_t high, uint8_t low) {
  return (int16_t)((high << 8) | low);
}

void setup() {
  // Initialize M5Stack Core2 (starts Serial, display, etc.)
  M5.begin();

  // Start I2C on Port A pins (GPIO32=SDA, GPIO33=SCL)
  Wire.begin(32, 33);

  // Wake MPU6050 from sleep (PWR_MGMT_1 = 0x00 clears sleep bit)
  writeRegister(REG_PWR_MGMT_1, 0x00);
  delay(100); // Allow sensor to stabilize

  M5.Lcd.setTextSize(2);
  M5.Lcd.setTextColor(WHITE, BLACK);
  M5.Lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
  M5.Lcd.println("MPU6050 Ready");

  Serial.println("MPU6050 initialized.");
}

void loop() {
  M5.update();

  uint8_t accelBuf[6];
  uint8_t gyroBuf[6];

  if (!readBytes(REG_ACCEL_XOUT_H, accelBuf, 6)) {
    Serial.println("Accel read failed!");
    delay(500);
    return;
  }
  if (!readBytes(REG_GYRO_XOUT_H, gyroBuf, 6)) {
    Serial.println("Gyro read failed!");
    delay(500);
    return;
  }

  float ax = combineBytes(accelBuf[0], accelBuf[1]) / ACCEL_SCALE;
  float ay = combineBytes(accelBuf[2], accelBuf[3]) / ACCEL_SCALE;
  float az = combineBytes(accelBuf[4], accelBuf[5]) / ACCEL_SCALE;

  float gx = combineBytes(gyroBuf[0], gyroBuf[1]) / GYRO_SCALE;
  float gy = combineBytes(gyroBuf[2], gyroBuf[3]) / GYRO_SCALE;
  float gz = combineBytes(gyroBuf[4], gyroBuf[5]) / GYRO_SCALE;

  // Print to Serial
  Serial.printf("Accel (g):  X=%.3f  Y=%.3f  Z=%.3f\n", ax, ay, az);
  Serial.printf("Gyro (d/s): X=%.3f  Y=%.3f  Z=%.3f\n", gx, gy, gz);

  // Display on M5Stack screen
  M5.Lcd.setCursor(0, 40);
  M5.Lcd.printf("Ax:%.2f Ay:%.2f Az:%.2f  ", ax, ay, az);
  M5.Lcd.setCursor(0, 70);
  M5.Lcd.printf("Gx:%.1f Gy:%.1f Gz:%.1f  ", gx, gy, gz);

  delay(200);
}
```

**How it works:** Writing `0x00` to the PWR_MGMT_1 register is essential because the MPU6050 powers up in sleep mode by default and will return all zeros until that bit is cleared. Raw 16-bit values from consecutive high/low register pairs are combined with a bitshift and divided by the datasheet-specified scale factors to convert them into human-readable g and degrees-per-second units.

Port B on the M5Stack Core2 exposes GPIO26 as a PWM-capable output, making it a natural choice for driving an SG90 servo's signal line directly without needing additional hardware.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to M5Stack Core2 |
|---|---|
| SG90 VCC (Red) | 5V (Grove Port B) |
| SG90 GND (Brown) | GND (Grove Port B) |
| SG90 Signal (Orange) | GPIO26 (Port B pin2) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +-------------+
|   M5Stack Core2   |          |  SG90 Servo |
|                   |          |             |
|  Port B           |          |             |
|  5V    -----------+----------+-- VCC (Red) |
|  GND   -----------+----------+-- GND (Brn) |
|  GPIO26-----------+----------+-- SIG (Org) |
+-------------------+          +-------------+
```

The SG90 can technically run on 3.3V but performs more reliably and with greater torque at 5V; the M5Stack Core2 Grove port supplies 5V power while GPIO26 outputs a 3.3V PWM signal which is sufficient to drive the SG90 signal line.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <M5Core2.h>

// SG90 servo signal connected to Port B GPIO26
const int SERVO_PIN = 26;

// SG90 PWM parameters: 50Hz, 20ms period
// Pulse width: ~500us (0 deg) to ~2400us (180 deg)
const int PWM_CHANNEL  = 0;
const int PWM_FREQ     = 50;      // 50 Hz
const int PWM_RESOLUTION = 16;    // 16-bit resolution

// For 16-bit at 50Hz: period = 1/50 = 20ms
// duty = (pulse_us / 20000us) * 65535
const int SERVO_MIN_DUTY = (int)(0.5 / 20.0 * 65535);   // ~500us -> ~1638
const int SERVO_MAX_DUTY = (int)(2.4 / 20.0 * 65535);   // ~2400us -> ~7864

// Map an angle (0-180) to a 16-bit PWM duty value
int angleToDuty(int angle) {
  return map(angle, 0, 180, SERVO_MIN_DUTY, SERVO_MAX_DUTY);
}

void setup() {
  M5.begin();
  M5.Lcd.setTextSize(2);
  M5.Lcd.setCursor(20, 100);
  M5.Lcd.println("SG90 Servo Demo");

  // Configure LEDC PWM channel for servo
  ledcSetup(PWM_CHANNEL, PWM_FREQ, PWM_RESOLUTION);
  ledcAttachPin(SERVO_PIN, PWM_CHANNEL);

  // Move to center position on startup
  ledcWrite(PWM_CHANNEL, angleToDuty(90));
  delay(1000);
}

void loop() {
  // Sweep from 0 to 180 degrees
  for (int angle = 0; angle <= 180; angle += 5) {
    ledcWrite(PWM_CHANNEL, angleToDuty(angle));
    M5.Lcd.fillRect(20, 140, 280, 30, BLACK);
    M5.Lcd.setCursor(20, 140);
    M5.Lcd.printf("Angle: %3d deg", angle);
    delay(50);
  }

  delay(500);

  // Sweep back from 180 to 0 degrees
  for (int angle = 180; angle >= 0; angle -= 5) {
    ledcWrite(PWM_CHANNEL, angleToDuty(angle));
    M5.Lcd.fillRect(20, 140, 280, 30, BLACK);
    M5.Lcd.setCursor(20, 140);
    M5.Lcd.printf("Angle: %3d deg", angle);
    delay(50);
  }

  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** The ESP32's `ledcSetup`/`ledcAttachPin`/`ledcWrite` API generates hardware PWM via the LEDC peripheral, which is far more timing-accurate than software PWM and frees the CPU entirely — the 16-bit resolution at 50 Hz gives fine-grained duty cycle control so that `map()` cleanly translates 0–180° angles into the ~500 µs to ~2400 µs pulse widths the SG90 expects.

Port B on the M5Stack Core2 exposes GPIO36 and GPIO26 as general-purpose digital pins; since GPIO36 is input-only, the DS18B20 data line is routed to GPIO26, which supports both read and the OneWire protocol's bidirectional signaling.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to M5Stack Core2 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V (Grove Port B) |
| GND | GND (Grove Port B) |
| DATA | GPIO26 (Port B pin2) |
| DATA | 4.7kΩ pull-up resistor to 3.3V |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
|   M5Stack Core2     |          |     DS18B20       |
|                     |          |                   |
|  Port B (Grove)     |          |                   |
|  3.3V  o-----------+----------o VCC               |
|  GND   o-----------+----------o GND               |
|  GPIO26 o----------+----+-----o DATA              |
|                     |   |                          |
+---------------------+   |     +-------------------+
                          |4.7kΩ
                          +------o 3.3V
```

The DS18B20 is rated for 3.3V operation, so no level shifting is needed; ensure the 4.7kΩ pull-up resistor is connected between the DATA line and 3.3V, not 5V.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <M5Core2.h>
#include <OneWire.h>
#include <DallasTemperature.h>

#define ONE_WIRE_BUS 26  // Port B pin2 = GPIO26

OneWire oneWire(ONE_WIRE_BUS);
DallasTemperature sensors(&oneWire);

void setup() {
  M5.begin();
  M5.Lcd.setTextSize(2);
  M5.Lcd.setTextColor(WHITE, BLACK);
  M5.Lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
  M5.Lcd.println("DS18B20 Temp");

  sensors.begin();

  if (sensors.getDeviceCount() == 0) {
    M5.Lcd.setCursor(0, 40);
    M5.Lcd.println("No sensor found!");
    while (true) { delay(1000); }
  }

  Serial.begin(115200);
  Serial.println("DS18B20 ready");
}

void loop() {
  M5.update();

  sensors.requestTemperatures();
  float tempC = sensors.getTempCByIndex(0);
  float tempF = tempC * 9.0f / 5.0f + 32.0f;

  if (tempC == DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C) {
    M5.Lcd.setCursor(0, 40);
    M5.Lcd.println("Read error!     ");
    Serial.println("Sensor disconnected or read error");
  } else {
    M5.Lcd.setCursor(0, 40);
    M5.Lcd.printf("%.2f C   \n", tempC);
    M5.Lcd.printf("%.2f F   \n", tempF);
    Serial.printf("Temperature: %.2f C / %.2f F\n", tempC, tempF);
  }

  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** `sensors.requestTemperatures()` triggers a blocking conversion on all DS18B20 devices on the bus before `getTempCByIndex(0)` fetches the result — this ordering is mandatory because the sensor only updates its scratchpad register after a conversion command is issued. Checking against `DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C` catches the sentinel value (-127.0) that the library returns when the sensor is missing or the CRC fails, preventing garbage readings from appearing on screen.

Port B's two GPIO pins (GPIO36 and GPIO26) are used for TRIG and ECHO, but GPIO36 is input-only on the ESP32, so TRIG is assigned to GPIO26 (output) and ECHO to GPIO36 (input).

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to M5Stack Core2 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V (Port B) |
| GND | GND (Port B) |
| TRIG | GPIO26 (Port B pin2) |
| ECHO | GPIO36 (Port B pin1) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
|    M5Stack Core2    |          |     HC-SR04       |
|                     |          |                   |
|  Port B             |          |                   |
|  5V  ---------------+----------+ VCC               |
|  GND ---------------+----------+ GND               |
|  GPIO26 (pin2) -----+----------+ TRIG              |
|  GPIO36 (pin1) -----+----------+ ECHO              |
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
```
Note: GPIO36 is a receive-only (input-only) pin on the ESP32 and cannot be used for output — always assign TRIG to GPIO26.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <M5Core2.h>

#define TRIG_PIN 26
#define ECHO_PIN 36

void setup() {
  M5.begin();
  M5.Lcd.setTextSize(2);
  M5.Lcd.setTextColor(WHITE, BLACK);

  pinMode(TRIG_PIN, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(ECHO_PIN, INPUT);

  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, LOW);
  Serial.begin(115200);
}

float measureDistanceCm() {
  // Send 10us trigger pulse
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, LOW);
  delayMicroseconds(2);
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, HIGH);
  delayMicroseconds(10);
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, LOW);

  // Measure echo pulse width (timeout after 30ms ~ 5m)
  long duration = pulseIn(ECHO_PIN, HIGH, 30000);

  if (duration == 0) return -1.0; // No echo received

  // Convert to cm: sound travels at ~343 m/s
  // distance = duration(us) * 0.0343 / 2
  return (duration * 0.0343f) / 2.0f;
}

void loop() {
  M5.update();

  float distance = measureDistanceCm();

  M5.Lcd.setCursor(20, 100);
  if (distance < 0) {
    M5.Lcd.println("Out of range  ");
    Serial.println("Out of range");
  } else {
    M5.Lcd.printf("Dist: %.1f cm   ", distance);
    Serial.printf("Distance: %.1f cm\n", distance);
  }

  delay(200);
}
```

**How it works:** `pulseIn(ECHO_PIN, HIGH, 30000)` blocks until the ECHO pin goes HIGH then times how long it stays HIGH in microseconds — that round-trip time divided by 2 and multiplied by the speed of sound (0.0343 cm/µs) gives the one-way distance. The timeout of 30,000 µs caps the wait at ~5 m, preventing the loop from hanging if no echo returns.

The DHT11 uses a single-wire digital protocol, so only one data pin is needed alongside power and ground, with a pull-up resistor to keep the line stable between transmissions.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Arduino Uno |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V pin |
| GND | GND pin |
| DATA | D2 (with 10kΩ pull-up resistor to 5V) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        Arduino Uno
       +------------------+
       |               5V |---+-----> DHT11 VCC
       |                  |   |
       |                  |  [10kΩ]
       |                  |   |
       |              D2  |---+-----> DHT11 DATA
       |                  |
       |             GND  |---------> DHT11 GND
       +------------------+
```
The 10kΩ pull-up resistor between VCC and the DATA line is required to ensure proper signal levels during idle and start conditions.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <DHT.h>

#define DHTPIN 2
#define DHTTYPE DHT11

DHT dht(DHTPIN, DHTTYPE);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  dht.begin();
  Serial.println("DHT11 sensor initialized.");
}

void loop() {
  // DHT11 needs at least 1 second between readings
  delay(2000);

  float humidity = dht.readHumidity();
  float temperature = dht.readTemperature(); // Celsius by default

  // Check if reads failed
  if (isnan(humidity) || isnan(temperature)) {
    Serial.println("ERROR: Failed to read from DHT11 sensor!");
    return;
  }

  Serial.print("Humidity: ");
  Serial.print(humidity);
  Serial.print(" %  |  Temperature: ");
  Serial.print(temperature);
  Serial.println(" °C");
}
```

**How it works:** The `isnan()` check is critical because the DHT library returns `NaN` (not a number) when a read fails due to a timing issue or missing pull-up resistor, so catching it early prevents garbage values from being printed. The 2-second `delay()` respects the DHT11's maximum sampling rate of 1 Hz — polling it faster will cause read failures.

The DS18B20 uses the OneWire single-wire protocol, so only one data pin is needed alongside a 4.7kΩ pull-up resistor to keep the bus high at idle.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Arduino Uno |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V pin |
| GND | GND |
| DATA | D2 (with 4.7kΩ resistor to 5V) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------+          +-------------------+
|   Arduino Uno  |          |     DS18B20       |
|                |          |                   |
|   5V ----------+----+-----+ VCC               |
|                |    |     |                   |
|                |   4.7kΩ  |                   |
|                |    |     |                   |
|   D2 ----------+----+-----+ DATA              |
|                |          |                   |
|   GND ---------+----------+ GND               |
+----------------+          +-------------------+
```

The 4.7kΩ pull-up resistor between DATA and 5V is mandatory; without it the OneWire bus will not function correctly.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <OneWire.h>
#include <DallasTemperature.h>

#define ONE_WIRE_BUS 2  // DS18B20 DATA pin connected to D2

OneWire oneWire(ONE_WIRE_BUS);
DallasTemperature sensors(&oneWire);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  sensors.begin();
  Serial.println("DS18B20 Temperature Sensor Ready");
}

void loop() {
  sensors.requestTemperatures();  // Send command to all sensors to convert temperature

  float tempC = sensors.getTempCByIndex(0);  // Read first sensor on the bus
  float tempF = sensors.toFahrenheit(tempC);

  if (tempC == DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C) {
    Serial.println("Error: sensor not found or disconnected!");
  } else {
    Serial.print("Temperature: ");
    Serial.print(tempC);
    Serial.print(" C  /  ");
    Serial.print(tempF);
    Serial.println(" F");
  }

  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** `sensors.requestTemperatures()` broadcasts a global conversion command over the OneWire bus, triggering the DS18B20 to perform its internal ADC measurement before `getTempCByIndex(0)` retrieves the result — these must always be called in sequence because the sensor does not continuously output data. Checking against `DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C` guards against a floating bus reading that would otherwise silently return -127°C.

The HC-SR04 uses two dedicated digital pins — one to trigger the ultrasonic burst and one to receive the echo pulse — so any two free GPIO pins work fine here.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Arduino Uno |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V pin |
| GND | GND |
| TRIG | D9 |
| ECHO | D10 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+------------------+          +-------------------+
|   Arduino Uno    |          |     HC-SR04        |
|                  |          |                    |
|               5V |----------| VCC                |
|              GND |----------| GND                |
|               D9 |----------| TRIG               |
|              D10 |----------| ECHO               |
+------------------+          +-------------------+
```

The HC-SR04 runs on 5V and its ECHO pin outputs 5V logic, which is safe for the Arduino Uno's 5V-tolerant digital inputs — no level shifting required.

**Code**
```cpp
const int TRIG_PIN = 9;
const int ECHO_PIN = 10;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(TRIG_PIN, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(ECHO_PIN, INPUT);
}

void loop() {
  // Ensure trigger is low before sending pulse
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, LOW);
  delayMicroseconds(2);

  // Send a 10-microsecond HIGH pulse to trigger measurement
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, HIGH);
  delayMicroseconds(10);
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, LOW);

  // Measure the duration of the ECHO pulse in microseconds
  long duration = pulseIn(ECHO_PIN, HIGH, 30000); // 30ms timeout

  if (duration == 0) {
    Serial.println("Out of range or no echo received");
  } else {
    // Speed of sound: 343 m/s = 0.0343 cm/us
    // Divide by 2 because sound travels to object AND back
    float distanceCm = (duration * 0.0343) / 2.0;
    Serial.print("Distance: ");
    Serial.print(distanceCm, 1);
    Serial.println(" cm");
  }

  delay(200);
}
```

**How it works:** `pulseIn(ECHO_PIN, HIGH, 30000)` blocks until the ECHO pin goes HIGH then measures exactly how long it stays HIGH in microseconds, with a 30 ms timeout to avoid hanging if no object reflects the pulse. Dividing the round-trip travel time by 2 before applying the speed-of-sound conversion gives the one-way distance to the target.

The SG90 servo is controlled via a single PWM signal line, so only one data wire is needed alongside power and ground — use a PWM-capable pin to ensure hardware-assisted pulse generation.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Arduino Uno |
|---|---|
| VCC (Red) | 5V pin |
| GND (Brown/Black) | GND |
| Signal (Orange/Yellow) | D9 (PWM) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+------------------+          +-------------------+
|   Arduino Uno    |          |    SG90 Servo     |
|                  |          |                   |
|              5V  |--------->| VCC  (Red)        |
|             GND  |--------->| GND  (Brown)      |
|              D9  |--------->| Signal (Orange)   |
+------------------+          +-------------------+
```
The SG90 can draw up to ~500mA under load — for multiple servos or heavy loads, power VCC from an external 5V supply sharing GND with the Arduino rather than the onboard 5V pin.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Servo.h>

Servo myServo;

const int SERVO_PIN = 9;

void setup() {
  myServo.attach(SERVO_PIN);
}

void loop() {
  // Sweep from 0 to 180 degrees
  for (int angle = 0; angle <= 180; angle++) {
    myServo.write(angle);
    delay(15);
  }

  // Sweep back from 180 to 0 degrees
  for (int angle = 180; angle >= 0; angle--) {
    myServo.write(angle);
    delay(15);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** `myServo.attach(SERVO_PIN)` configures the Servo library to generate 50Hz PWM pulses on D9, where `myServo.write(angle)` translates a 0–180 degree value into the corresponding pulse width (roughly 544–2400 microseconds) that the SG90 uses to set its shaft position. The 15ms delay between each degree step gives the servo mechanical time to reach each position without stalling.

A passive buzzer requires a PWM-capable pin because it needs an oscillating signal at a specific frequency to produce sound, unlike an active buzzer which only needs power.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Arduino Uno |
|---|---|
| + (positive) | D9 |
| - (negative) | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+------------------+          +------------------+
|   Arduino Uno    |          |  Passive Buzzer  |
|                  |          |                  |
|               D9 |----------| +                |
|              GND |----------| -                |
+------------------+          +------------------+
```

Passive buzzers are safe at 5V logic levels; no current-limiting resistor is strictly required, but adding a 100Ω resistor in series with the positive leg can protect the pin and reduce volume if needed.

**Code**
```cpp
#define BUZZER_PIN 9

// Note frequencies in Hz
#define NOTE_C4  262
#define NOTE_D4  294
#define NOTE_E4  330
#define NOTE_F4  349
#define NOTE_G4  392
#define NOTE_A4  440
#define NOTE_B4  494
#define NOTE_C5  523

// Melody: notes and durations (in ms)
int melody[] = {
  NOTE_C4, NOTE_E4, NOTE_G4, NOTE_C5,
  NOTE_C5, NOTE_G4, NOTE_E4, NOTE_C4
};

int noteDurations[] = {
  300, 300, 300, 600,
  300, 300, 300, 600
};

void setup() {
  pinMode(BUZZER_PIN, OUTPUT);

  // Play the melody once on startup
  int numNotes = sizeof(melody) / sizeof(melody[0]);
  for (int i = 0; i < numNotes; i++) {
    tone(BUZZER_PIN, melody[i], noteDurations[i]);
    // Add a short gap between notes to make them distinct
    delay(noteDurations[i] + 50);
    noTone(BUZZER_PIN);
  }
}

void loop() {
  // Replay melody every 3 seconds
  delay(3000);

  int numNotes = sizeof(melody) / sizeof(melody[0]);
  for (int i = 0; i < numNotes; i++) {
    tone(BUZZER_PIN, melody[i], noteDurations[i]);
    delay(noteDurations[i] + 50);
    noTone(BUZZER_PIN);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The `tone()` function handles all the PWM timing automatically — it configures Timer2 on the ATmega328P to generate a square wave at the specified frequency on the pin, so the buzzer's diaphragm vibrates at that pitch. The 50ms gap added after each note duration (before `noTone()`) creates a brief silence that separates successive notes so they don't blur together, which is especially important when two adjacent notes share the same pitch.

A relay module with an active-low IN pin is driven directly from a 5V Arduino digital output, so no level shifting is needed — just pick any free GPIO and keep the relay's VCC tied to the Uno's 5V rail.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Arduino Uno |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V pin |
| GND | GND |
| IN | D7 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+------------------+          +-------------------+
|   Arduino Uno    |          |  Relay Module     |
|                  |          |                   |
|              5V  |--------->| VCC               |
|             GND  |--------->| GND               |
|              D7  |--------->| IN                |
|                  |          |                   |
|                  |          | NO  (Normally Open)|
|                  |          | COM (Common)      |
|                  |          | NC  (Normally Closed)|
+------------------+          +-------------------+
```
Most relay modules are active-low (IN = LOW energizes the coil), but some are active-high — check your module's silkscreen or datasheet and invert the logic constants in the code if needed.

**Code**
```cpp
#define RELAY_PIN 7

// Most relay modules are ACTIVE LOW:
// LOW  = relay energized (NO contact closed)
// HIGH = relay de-energized (NO contact open)
#define RELAY_ON  LOW
#define RELAY_OFF HIGH

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  // Set pin as output and make sure relay starts OFF
  pinMode(RELAY_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, RELAY_OFF);
  Serial.println("Relay control ready.");
}

void loop() {
  // Turn relay ON for 2 seconds
  Serial.println("Relay ON");
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, RELAY_ON);
  delay(2000);

  // Turn relay OFF for 2 seconds
  Serial.println("Relay OFF");
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, RELAY_OFF);
  delay(2000);
}
```

**How it works:** The `RELAY_ON`/`RELAY_OFF` preprocessor constants abstract the active-low logic so swapping to an active-high module only requires changing two `#define` lines rather than hunting through every `digitalWrite` call. Initializing the pin HIGH in `setup()` before the first `loop()` prevents a brief unintended relay trigger that would otherwise occur while the pin floats during boot.

A PIR motion sensor outputs a simple HIGH/LOW digital signal, so it connects directly to any GPIO pin on the Arduino Uno without needing a special protocol library.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Arduino Uno |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V pin |
| GND | GND |
| OUT (signal) | D2 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------+          +-------------------+
|  Arduino Uno   |          |   PIR Sensor      |
|                |          |                   |
|           5V  |----------| VCC               |
|          GND  |----------| GND               |
|           D2  |----------| OUT (signal)      |
|          D13  |--- (onboard LED, built-in)   |
+----------------+          +-------------------+
```
Most HC-SR501 PIR modules include an onboard 3.3V regulator and are designed to run at 5V supply, but their OUT pin signal is safe to read directly with a 5V Arduino GPIO.

**Code**
```cpp
const int PIR_PIN = 2;        // PIR signal output connected to D2
const int LED_PIN = 13;       // Onboard LED

void setup() {
  pinMode(PIR_PIN, INPUT);    // PIR OUT drives the line; no pull-up needed
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
  Serial.begin(9600);

  // Allow PIR sensor to stabilize (typically 30-60 seconds on first power-up)
  Serial.println("Waiting for PIR sensor to stabilize...");
  delay(2000);                // Shortened delay for demo; use 30000 in production
  Serial.println("PIR sensor ready.");
}

void loop() {
  int motionState = digitalRead(PIR_PIN);

  if (motionState == HIGH) {
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH);
    Serial.println("Motion detected!");
  } else {
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  }

  delay(100); // Small debounce delay to avoid flooding Serial output
}
```

**How it works:** The PIR sensor internally latches its OUT pin HIGH for a configurable hold time (set by the onboard potentiometer) after detecting motion, so `digitalRead` simply catches that latched HIGH level without needing edge-interrupt logic. The 2-second stabilization delay in `setup()` is important because PIR sensors need time to settle and calibrate to ambient IR levels before giving reliable readings — in a real deployment this should be closer to 30 seconds.

An LDR forms a voltage divider with a fixed resistor so the analog voltage on the Arduino's ADC pin rises and falls with ambient light intensity.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Arduino Uno |
|---|---|
| LDR leg 1 | 5V pin |
| LDR leg 2 | A0 and one leg of 10kΩ resistor |
| 10kΩ resistor other leg | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
  +------------------+          +-------------------+
  |   Arduino Uno    |          |   LDR + Resistor  |
  |                  |          |                   |
  |   5V  o----------+----------o LDR leg 1         |
  |                  |          |                   |
  |   A0  o----------+----------o LDR leg 2 / Divider mid-point
  |                  |          |                   |
  |  GND  o----------+----------o 10kΩ to GND       |
  +------------------+          +-------------------+
```
The 10kΩ pull-down resistor is essential — without it the ADC pin floats when the LDR resistance is very high.

**Code**
```cpp
const int LDR_PIN = A0;       // Analog input connected to voltage divider mid-point
const int DARK_THRESHOLD = 300; // ADC counts below this = dark (tune to your environment)

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(LDR_PIN, INPUT);    // A0 as analog input (default, but explicit for clarity)
}

void loop() {
  int rawValue = analogRead(LDR_PIN); // 0-1023, higher = more light (LDR resistance drops)

  // Map raw ADC reading to a 0-100 light percentage
  int lightPercent = map(rawValue, 0, 1023, 0, 100);

  Serial.print("Raw ADC: ");
  Serial.print(rawValue);
  Serial.print("  Light level: ");
  Serial.print(lightPercent);
  Serial.print("%");

  if (rawValue < DARK_THRESHOLD) {
    Serial.println("  [DARK]");
  } else {
    Serial.println("  [BRIGHT]");
  }

  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** `analogRead()` returns a value from 0–1023 representing the voltage at the divider mid-point; as light increases the LDR's resistance drops, pulling that node closer to 5 V and producing a higher reading. The `map()` call rescales the raw 10-bit ADC value to a human-readable 0–100 percentage without any floating-point math, keeping things efficient on the Uno's limited hardware.

The IR receiver module outputs a single digital signal line, so only one data pin is needed alongside power — making it straightforward to connect without any bus protocol or level-shifting concerns.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Arduino Uno |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V pin |
| GND | GND |
| DATA (OUT) | D2 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
|   Arduino Uno     |          |   IR Receiver     |
|                   |          |   Module          |
|               5V  |--------->| VCC               |
|              GND  |--------->| GND               |
|               D2  |<---------| DATA/OUT          |
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
```
D2 is used because it supports hardware interrupts (INT0), which the IRremote library relies on for reliable pulse timing.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <IRremote.hpp>

const int IR_RECEIVE_PIN = 2;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  IrReceiver.begin(IR_RECEIVE_PIN, ENABLE_LED_FEEDBACK);
  Serial.println("IR Receiver ready. Point remote and press a button.");
}

void loop() {
  if (IrReceiver.decode()) {
    Serial.print("Protocol : ");
    Serial.println(IrReceiver.decodedIRData.protocol);
    Serial.print("Address  : 0x");
    Serial.println(IrReceiver.decodedIRData.address, HEX);
    Serial.print("Command  : 0x");
    Serial.println(IrReceiver.decodedIRData.command, HEX);
    Serial.print("Raw value: 0x");
    Serial.println(IrReceiver.decodedIRData.decodedRawData, HEX);
    Serial.println("-----------------------------");
    IrReceiver.resume(); // Ready to receive the next signal
  }
}
```

**How it works:** `IrReceiver.begin()` attaches an interrupt to D2 so the library captures every pulse edge in the background without blocking `loop()`. After each successful decode, `IrReceiver.resume()` is essential — without it the receiver stays locked and ignores all subsequent signals.

