**Code**
```cpp
#include <SPI.h>
#include <MFRC522.h>

#define SS_PIN  10
#define RST_PIN  9

MFRC522 rfid(SS_PIN, RST_PIN);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  SPI.begin();
  rfid.PCD_Init();
  Serial.println("MFRC522 RFID Reader ready. Scan a tag...");
}

void loop() {
  // Check for a new card present and readable
  if (!rfid.PICC_IsNewCardPresent() || !rfid.PICC_ReadCardSerial()) {
    return;
  }

  Serial.print("Tag UID: ");
  for (byte i = 0; i < rfid.uid.size; i++) {
    if (rfid.uid.uidByte[i] < 0x10) {
      Serial.print("0"); // leading zero for single hex digits
    }
    Serial.print(rfid.uid.uidByte[i], HEX);
    if (i < rfid.uid.size - 1) Serial.print(":");
  }
  Serial.println();

  // Halt the card and stop encrypted communication
  rfid.PICC_HaltA();
  rfid.PCD_StopCrypto1();
}
```

**How it works:** The two-step gate `PICC_IsNewCardPresent() && PICC_ReadCardSerial()` is essential — the first call detects a card's RF field response while the second actually loads the UID into `rfid.uid`; skipping either causes the read to fail silently. `PCD_StopCrypto1()` must be called after each read to reset the module's crypto state, otherwise subsequent scans of different cards will be ignored.

The SSD1306 OLED uses I2C, so it shares the Uno's dedicated I2C bus on A4/A5, keeping digital pins free for other peripherals.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Arduino Uno |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V pin |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | A4 |
| SCL | A5 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+------------------+          +-------------------+
|   Arduino Uno    |          |   SSD1306 OLED    |
|                  |          |                   |
|               5V |----------| VCC               |
|              GND |----------| GND               |
|        A4 (SDA)  |----------| SDA               |
|        A5 (SCL)  |----------| SCL               |
+------------------+          +-------------------+
```

Most SSD1306 breakout boards include onboard pull-up resistors for SDA/SCL; if yours does not, add 4.7kΩ resistors from each line to 5V.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>
#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>

#define SCREEN_WIDTH  128
#define SCREEN_HEIGHT  64
#define OLED_RESET     -1   // no reset pin used
#define OLED_I2C_ADDR 0x3C

Adafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, OLED_RESET);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);

  if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, OLED_I2C_ADDR)) {
    Serial.println(F("SSD1306 init failed — check wiring!"));
    while (true); // halt
  }

  display.clearDisplay();

  // Large centered title
  display.setTextSize(2);
  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);
  display.setCursor(10, 10);
  display.println(F("Hello!"));

  // Smaller subtitle
  display.setTextSize(1);
  display.setCursor(20, 40);
  display.println(F("Arduino + OLED"));

  display.display(); // push buffer to screen
}

void loop() {
  // Static display — nothing to do here
}
```

**How it works:** `display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, OLED_I2C_ADDR)` both initialises the I2C connection and tells the driver to use the display's internal charge pump to generate its own 3.3 V rail, which is why the module can run from the Uno's 5 V supply without extra circuitry. All drawing calls write into an in-RAM framebuffer and nothing appears on screen until `display.display()` flushes the entire 1 KB buffer over I2C in one go.

The joystick's X and Y axes output ratiometric analog voltages (0–5V), so they connect directly to the Uno's analog inputs without any level shifting, while the push-button output is read as a digital input with the internal pull-up enabled.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Arduino Uno |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V pin |
| GND | GND |
| VRx (X-axis) | A0 |
| VRy (Y-axis) | A1 |
| SW (button) | D2 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +----------------------+
|  Analog Joystick  |          |     Arduino Uno      |
|                   |          |                      |
|  VCC  o-----------+----------+- 5V                  |
|  GND  o-----------+----------+- GND                 |
|  VRx  o-----------+----------+- A0                  |
|  VRy  o-----------+----------+- A1                  |
|  SW   o-----------+----------+- D2                  |
|                   |          |                      |
+-------------------+          +----------------------+
```
The SW pin relies on the Uno's internal pull-up resistor, so no external resistor is needed — the button reads LOW when pressed and HIGH when released.

**Code**
```cpp
const int PIN_VRX = A0;   // X-axis analog input
const int PIN_VRY = A1;   // Y-axis analog input
const int PIN_SW  = 2;    // Joystick button (active LOW)

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  // Enable internal pull-up so SW reads HIGH at rest, LOW when pressed
  pinMode(PIN_SW, INPUT_PULLUP);
}

void loop() {
  int xValue  = analogRead(PIN_VRX);  // 0–1023, center ~512
  int yValue  = analogRead(PIN_VRY);  // 0–1023, center ~512
  bool pressed = (digitalRead(PIN_SW) == LOW); // button pulls to GND when pressed

  Serial.print("X: ");
  Serial.print(xValue);
  Serial.print("  Y: ");
  Serial.print(yValue);
  Serial.print("  Button: ");
  Serial.println(pressed ? "PRESSED" : "released");

  delay(100);
}
```

**How it works:** Using `INPUT_PULLUP` on the SW pin internally connects it to 5V through ~50kΩ, so no external resistor is needed and the logic is inverted — `LOW` means the button is pressed because pressing it shorts the pin to GND. The analog axes return values centered near 512 (mid-supply), drifting toward 0 or 1023 as the stick is pushed in either direction along each axis.

A 4x4 matrix keypad uses 8 digital GPIO pins — 4 rows driven as outputs and 4 columns read as inputs — so no special protocol or extra hardware is needed beyond direct connections.

