  }

  display.clearDisplay();

  display.setTextSize(2);
  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);
  display.setCursor(0, 0);
  display.println(F("Hello!"));

  display.setTextSize(1);
  display.setCursor(0, 24);
  display.println(F("ESP8266 + SSD1306"));
  display.println(F("I2C OLED Demo"));

  display.display();  // Push buffer to screen
}

void loop() {
  // Static display -- nothing to update
}
```

**How it works:** Passing `&Wire` into the `Adafruit_SSD1306` constructor and calling `Wire.begin(D2, D1)` explicitly sets the I2C bus to NodeMCU's silkscreen D-number pins rather than relying on defaults, ensuring portability. All draw calls write into an in-RAM framebuffer, and `display.display()` is what actually transfers that buffer over I2C to the physical screen — nothing appears until that call is made.

A single-channel relay module takes a 5V supply but its control signal is driven from a GPIO pin, with most modules including an onboard transistor that makes them 3.3V-signal compatible — so D1 is a safe choice for the control line.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| VCC | VIN (5V from USB) |
| GND | GND |
| IN (signal) | D1 (GPIO5) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
|   ESP8266 NodeMCU |          |   Relay Module    |
|                   |          |                   |
|        VIN (5V) --+----------+-- VCC             |
|            GND --+----------+-- GND             |
|             D1 --+----------+-- IN (signal)     |
|                   |          |                   |
|                   |          | COM  NO  NC       |
|                   |          | (load terminals)  |
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
```
Most relay modules use active-LOW logic (LOW on IN energizes the relay), but some are active-HIGH — check your module's silkscreen or datasheet and flip the digitalWrite values if the relay behaves inverted.

**Code**
```cpp
#define RELAY_PIN D1  // GPIO5 on NodeMCU

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(RELAY_PIN, OUTPUT);
  // Start with relay OFF (HIGH = off for active-LOW modules)
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, HIGH);
  Serial.println("Relay controller ready");
}

void loop() {
  // Turn relay ON
  Serial.println("Relay ON");
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, LOW);   // LOW energizes active-LOW relay
  delay(2000);

  // Turn relay OFF
  Serial.println("Relay OFF");
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, HIGH);  // HIGH de-energizes relay
  delay(2000);
}
```

**How it works:** The relay module's onboard transistor inverts and amplifies the control signal, so writing LOW to D1 sources enough base current to saturate the transistor and energize the relay coil — this is why `digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, HIGH)` in `setup()` is the safe initialization state, ensuring the relay is off immediately at power-on before any loop logic runs.

The SG90 servo uses a standard 50Hz PWM signal, and on the ESP8266 the `Servo` library leverages the hardware timer to generate the required 1–2ms pulse widths — avoid D3/D4/D8 boot-strapping pins and D0 (no PWM) when choosing the signal pin.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| Signal (Orange) | D2 (GPIO4) |
| VCC (Red) | VIN (5V from USB) |
| GND (Brown/Black) | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
|   ESP8266 NodeMCU   |          |    SG90 Servo     |
|                     |          |                   |
|  VIN (5V) ----------|----------| VCC  (Red)        |
|  GND      ----------|----------| GND  (Brown)      |
|  D2       ----------|----------| Signal (Orange)   |
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
```
Power the servo from VIN (5V USB rail) rather than 3V3, since the SG90 stalls or behaves erratically below ~4.5V under load.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <Servo.h>

Servo myServo;

const int SERVO_PIN = D2;   // GPIO4 — good PWM-capable pin, not a strapping pin
const int SWEEP_DELAY = 15; // ms between each 1-degree step

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  myServo.attach(SERVO_PIN, 544, 2400); // attach with explicit min/max pulse widths (µs)
  Serial.println("SG90 sweep demo starting...");
}

void loop() {
  // Sweep from 0° to 180°
  for (int angle = 0; angle <= 180; angle++) {
    myServo.write(angle);
    delay(SWEEP_DELAY);
  }

  // Sweep back from 180° to 0°
  for (int angle = 180; angle >= 0; angle--) {
    myServo.write(angle);
    delay(SWEEP_DELAY);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** `myServo.attach(SERVO_PIN, 544, 2400)` explicitly sets the minimum (544 µs) and maximum (2400 µs) pulse widths to match the SG90's actual range, preventing mechanical binding at the extremes that can occur with the library's narrower defaults. The 15 ms step delay keeps angular velocity slow enough that the servo's small plastic gears aren't stressed by sudden large position jumps.

WS2812 strips use a single-wire timed data protocol, so only one GPIO is needed for data — choose a pin away from boot-strapping pins to avoid startup conflicts.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| 5V (VCC) | VIN (5V from USB) |
| GND | GND |
| DIN (Data In) | D2 (GPIO4) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+------------------+          +-------------------+
|  ESP8266 NodeMCU |          |  WS2812 LED Strip  |
|                  |          |                   |
|  VIN (5V)  >----|----------| 5V (VCC)          |
|  GND       >----|----------| GND               |
|  D2        >----|--[330Ω]--| DIN (Data In)     |
+------------------+          +-------------------+
```
A 300–500Ω series resistor on the data line helps protect against signal reflection and overvoltage spikes that can damage the first LED in the strip.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Adafruit_NeoPixel.h>

#define LED_PIN    D2
#define LED_COUNT  12   // adjust to match your strip length

Adafruit_NeoPixel strip(LED_COUNT, LED_PIN, NEO_GRB + NEO_KHZ800);

void setup() {
  strip.begin();
  strip.show(); // Initialize all pixels to off
  strip.setBrightness(80); // 0-255; keep reasonable to limit current draw
}

void loop() {
  colorWipe(strip.Color(255, 0, 0), 50); // Red
  colorWipe(strip.Color(0, 255, 0), 50); // Green
  colorWipe(strip.Color(0, 0, 255), 50); // Blue
  rainbowCycle(5);
}

void colorWipe(uint32_t color, int wait) {
  for (int i = 0; i < strip.numPixels(); i++) {
    strip.setPixelColor(i, color);
    strip.show();
    delay(wait);
  }
}

void rainbowCycle(int wait) {
  for (long firstPixelHue = 0; firstPixelHue < 5 * 65536; firstPixelHue += 256) {
    for (int i = 0; i < strip.numPixels(); i++) {
      int pixelHue = firstPixelHue + (i * 65536L / strip.numPixels());
      strip.setPixelColor(i, strip.gamma32(strip.ColorHSV(pixelHue)));
    }
    strip.show();
    delay(wait);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** `NEO_KHZ800` tells the library to use the 800 kHz timing protocol required by WS2812B LEDs, and changes are only pushed to the strip when `strip.show()` is called — batching all pixel updates before that call is what prevents partial/glitchy animation frames.

The HC-SR04 uses two digital GPIO lines — one to trigger a pulse and one to receive the echo — so any two free non-strapping GPIOs work well here.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| VCC | VIN (5V from USB) |
| GND | GND |
| TRIG | D5 (GPIO14) |
| ECHO | D6 (GPIO12) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
  +----------------------+          +-------------------+
  |   ESP8266 NodeMCU    |          |     HC-SR04        |
  |                      |          |                    |
  |  VIN (5V) ----------+----------+-- VCC              |
  |  GND      ----------+----------+-- GND              |
  |  D5 (GPIO14) -------+----------+-- TRIG             |
  |  D6 (GPIO12) -------+----------+-- ECHO             |
  +----------------------+          +-------------------+
```

The HC-SR04 runs on 5V and its ECHO pin outputs 5V logic — on most NodeMCU boards the GPIO pins are somewhat tolerant of this, but adding a voltage divider (e.g. 1kΩ + 2kΩ) on the ECHO line to bring it to ~3.3V is recommended for long-term safety.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int TRIG_PIN = D5;  // GPIO14
const int ECHO_PIN = D6;  // GPIO12

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(TRIG_PIN, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(ECHO_PIN, INPUT);
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, LOW);
  delay(500); // let sensor settle
  Serial.println("HC-SR04 Ultrasonic Distance Sensor");
}

float readDistanceCm() {
  // Ensure trigger is low before sending pulse
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, LOW);
  delayMicroseconds(2);

  // Send a 10-microsecond HIGH pulse to trigger measurement
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, HIGH);
  delayMicroseconds(10);
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, LOW);

  // Measure the duration of the echo pulse
  // pulseIn waits for the pin to go HIGH, then times how long it stays HIGH
  // Timeout set to 30000 µs (~5 meters max range)
  long duration = pulseIn(ECHO_PIN, HIGH, 30000UL);

  if (duration == 0) {
    return -1.0; // timeout or no object detected
  }

  // Convert travel time to distance:
  // Sound travels ~343 m/s = 0.0343 cm/µs
  // Distance = (duration / 2) * 0.0343
  float distanceCm = (duration / 2.0) * 0.0343;
  return distanceCm;
}

void loop() {
  float distance = readDistanceCm();

  if (distance < 0) {
    Serial.println("Out of range or no echo received.");
  } else {
    Serial.print("Distance: ");
    Serial.print(distance, 1);
    Serial.println(" cm");
  }

  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** `pulseIn(ECHO_PIN, HIGH, 30000UL)` is the key call — it blocks until the echo pin goes high, then measures exactly how many microseconds it stays high (which corresponds to the round-trip sound travel time), with a 30 ms timeout to avoid hanging when no object is in range. Dividing the duration by 2 before multiplying by the speed of sound (0.0343 cm/µs) converts the round-trip time into a one-way distance.

The PIR sensor outputs a simple 3.3V-compatible HIGH/LOW digital signal, so it connects directly to a GPIO pin without any level-shifting or pull-up resistors.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3V3 |
| GND | GND |
| OUT (DATA) | D5 (GPIO14) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
 +------------------+          +-------------------+
 | ESP8266 NodeMCU  |          |    PIR Sensor     |
 |                  |          |                   |
 |             3V3  |----------| VCC               |
 |              GND |----------| GND               |
 |        D5/GPIO14 |----------| OUT               |
 +------------------+          +-------------------+
```

Most HC-SR501 PIR modules run happily on 3.3V and output a 3.3V-level signal, but confirm your specific module's datasheet — some variants prefer 5V on VCC (in which case use VIN/5V instead of 3V3, though the OUT signal should still be 3.3V-safe).

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int PIR_PIN = D5; // GPIO14
bool lastState = LOW;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(PIR_PIN, INPUT);
  // Allow PIR sensor to stabilize (typically 30-60 seconds in a real deployment)
  Serial.println("PIR sensor warming up... please wait 2 seconds.");
  delay(2000);
  Serial.println("Ready. Monitoring for motion.");
}

void loop() {
  bool motionState = digitalRead(PIR_PIN);

  if (motionState == HIGH && lastState == LOW) {
    Serial.println("[ALERT] Motion detected!");
  } else if (motionState == LOW && lastState == HIGH) {
    Serial.println("[INFO] Motion ended.");
  }

  lastState = motionState;
  delay(100); // debounce / polling interval
}
```

**How it works:** The edge-detection logic (comparing `motionState` to `lastState`) ensures the alert prints only once when motion starts and once when it ends, rather than flooding the Serial monitor every 100 ms while the PIR output remains HIGH. The 2-second warm-up delay in `setup()` is a shortened stand-in for the PIR's internal calibration period — in a real deployment you should allow the full 30–60 seconds the sensor needs after power-on before trusting its output.

The capacitive soil moisture sensor outputs an analog voltage (typically 1.2V–2.8V) that feeds directly into the NodeMCU's A0 pin, which has an onboard voltage divider allowing readings up to 3.3V.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| AOUT | A0 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +-------------------------+
| Capacitive Soil   |          |   ESP8266 NodeMCU       |
| Moisture Sensor   |          |                         |
|                   |          |                         |
|  VCC o------------|----------| 3V3                     |
|  GND o------------|----------| GND                     |
|  AOUT o-----------|----------| A0                      |
|                   |          |                         |
+-------------------+          +-------------------------+
```
Capacitive sensors are 3.3V-safe and preferred over resistive types since they don't corrode; ensure your specific module is rated for 3.3V (most are labeled 3.3V–5V).

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// Calibration values -- adjust these by testing sensor in dry air and submerged in water
const int DRY_VALUE   = 820;  // Raw ADC value in dry air
const int WET_VALUE   = 380;  // Raw ADC value in water

const int SAMPLE_COUNT = 10;  // Number of samples to average for stability

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  delay(100);
  Serial.println("Capacitive Soil Moisture Sensor Ready");
}

int readAveragedADC() {
  long sum = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < SAMPLE_COUNT; i++) {
    sum += analogRead(A0);
    delay(10);
  }
  return (int)(sum / SAMPLE_COUNT);
}

void loop() {
  int rawValue = readAveragedADC();

  // Map raw ADC reading to 0-100% moisture (constrain handles out-of-range values)
  int moisturePercent = constrain(
    map(rawValue, DRY_VALUE, WET_VALUE, 0, 100),
    0, 100
  );

  Serial.print("Raw ADC: ");
  Serial.print(rawValue);
  Serial.print(" | Moisture: ");
  Serial.print(moisturePercent);
  Serial.println("%");

  if (moisturePercent < 30) {
    Serial.println("  --> Soil is DRY, consider watering.");
  } else if (moisturePercent < 70) {
    Serial.println("  --> Soil moisture is OPTIMAL.");
  } else {
    Serial.println("  --> Soil is WET.");
  }

  delay(2000);
}
```

**How it works:** The `map()` call inverts the relationship between raw ADC value and moisture percentage because capacitive sensors output a *lower* voltage when wetter (more capacitance lowers the oscillator frequency, reducing the output voltage). Averaging 10 samples before mapping reduces noise from the ESP8266's ADC, which can be jittery on single reads.

The MPU6050 uses I2C and runs on 3.3V, so it connects directly to the NodeMCU's default I2C bus on D2 (SDA) and D1 (SCL) without any level shifting.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | D2 (GPIO4) |
| SCL | D1 (GPIO5) |
| AD0 | GND (sets I2C address to 0x68) |
| INT | (not connected for basic polling) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        ESP8266 NodeMCU                  MPU6050
       +----------------+               +--------+
       |           3.3V |-------------->| VCC    |
       |            GND |-------------->| GND    |
       |    D2 (GPIO4)  |<-- SDA ------>| SDA    |
       |    D1 (GPIO5)  |--- SCL ------>| SCL    |
       |                |         GND ->| AD0    |
       +----------------+               +--------+
```

The AD0 pin tied to GND selects I2C address 0x68; pulling it HIGH would select 0x69, allowing two MPU6050s on the same bus.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>

const uint8_t MPU_ADDR = 0x68;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  delay(500);

  Wire.begin(D2, D1); // SDA, SCL

  // Wake the MPU6050 (it starts in sleep mode)
  Wire.beginTransmission(MPU_ADDR);
  Wire.write(0x6B); // PWR_MGMT_1 register
  Wire.write(0x00); // Clear sleep bit, use internal 8 MHz oscillator
  Wire.endTransmission(true);

  // Optional: set accelerometer range to ±2g (default, for reference)
  Wire.beginTransmission(MPU_ADDR);
  Wire.write(0x1C); // ACCEL_CONFIG register
  Wire.write(0x00); // ±2g full scale
  Wire.endTransmission(true);

  // Optional: set gyro range to ±250°/s (default, for reference)
  Wire.beginTransmission(MPU_ADDR);
  Wire.write(0x1B); // GYRO_CONFIG register
  Wire.write(0x00); // ±250°/s full scale
  Wire.endTransmission(true);

  Serial.println("MPU6050 initialized.");
}

void loop() {
  // Point to first data register (ACCEL_XOUT_H = 0x3B)
  Wire.beginTransmission(MPU_ADDR);
  Wire.write(0x3B);
  Wire.endTransmission(false); // false = repeated start, keep bus active

  Wire.requestFrom(MPU_ADDR, (uint8_t)14, (uint8_t)true); // 14 bytes: accel + temp + gyro

  int16_t ax = (Wire.read() << 8) | Wire.read();
  int16_t ay = (Wire.read() << 8) | Wire.read();
  int16_t az = (Wire.read() << 8) | Wire.read();
  int16_t temp_raw = (Wire.read() << 8) | Wire.read();
  int16_t gx = (Wire.read() << 8) | Wire.read();
  int16_t gy = (Wire.read() << 8) | Wire.read();
  int16_t gz = (Wire.read() << 8) | Wire.read();

  // Convert to physical units
  // Accel: LSB sensitivity = 16384 LSB/g at ±2g
  float ax_g = ax / 16384.0f;
  float ay_g = ay / 16384.0f;
  float az_g = az / 16384.0f;

  // Gyro: LSB sensitivity = 131 LSB/(°/s) at ±250°/s
  float gx_ds = gx / 131.0f;
  float gy_ds = gy / 131.0f;
  float gz_ds = gz / 131.0f;

  // Temperature: formula from datasheet
  float temp_c = temp_raw / 340.0f + 36.53f;

  Serial.print("Accel (g)  X: "); Serial.print(ax_g, 3);
  Serial.print("  Y: ");          Serial.print(ay_g, 3);
  Serial.print("  Z: ");          Serial.println(az_g, 3);

  Serial.print("Gyro (°/s) X: "); Serial.print(gx_ds, 2);
  Serial.print("  Y: ");          Serial.print(gy_ds, 2);
  Serial.print("  Z: ");          Serial.println(gz_ds, 2);

  Serial.print("Temperature: ");  Serial.print(temp_c, 1);
  Serial.println(" °C");
  Serial.println("---");

  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** Writing `0x00` to register `0x6B` (PWR_MGMT_1) is mandatory before any data can be read, because the MPU6050 powers up in sleep mode by default. Using `Wire.endTransmission(false)` followed by `Wire.requestFrom()` issues an I2C repeated-start condition, which lets the master read a burst of 14 consecutive registers starting at `0x3B` in a single transaction rather than re-addressing the device for each byte.

The DS18B20 uses the OneWire single-wire protocol and requires a 4.7kΩ pull-up resistor on its DATA line to ensure reliable communication.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| DATA | D2 (GPIO4) via 4.7kΩ resistor to 3.3V |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+------------------+          +-------------------+
|   ESP8266        |          |    DS18B20        |
|   NodeMCU        |          |                   |
|                  |          |                   |
|   3V3 o----------+----+-----o VCC               |
|                  |    |     |                   |
|                  |   4.7kΩ  |                   |
|                  |    |     |                   |
|    D2 o----------+----+-----o DATA              |
|                  |          |                   |
|   GND o----------+----------o GND               |
+------------------+          +-------------------+
```

The DS18B20 is 3.3V compatible so it can be powered directly from NodeMCU's 3V3 pin without any level shifting.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <OneWire.h>
#include <DallasTemperature.h>

#define ONE_WIRE_BUS D2

OneWire oneWire(ONE_WIRE_BUS);
DallasTemperature sensors(&oneWire);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  sensors.begin();
  Serial.println("DS18B20 Temperature Sensor Ready");
}

void loop() {
  sensors.requestTemperatures();

  float tempC = sensors.getTempCByIndex(0);
  float tempF = sensors.toFahrenheit(tempC);

  if (tempC == DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C) {
    Serial.println("Error: Could not read temperature (device disconnected?)");
  } else {
    Serial.print("Temperature: ");
    Serial.print(tempC);
    Serial.print(" °C  /  ");
    Serial.print(tempF);
    Serial.println(" °F");
  }

  delay(2000);
}
```

**How it works:** `sensors.requestTemperatures()` triggers all DS18B20 devices on the bus to perform a conversion (which takes up to 750ms at default 12-bit resolution), and then `getTempCByIndex(0)` retrieves the result from the first sensor found — checking against `DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C` catches wiring faults or a missing pull-up resistor that would otherwise silently return garbage values.

An LDR is a passive resistive sensor, so it must be wired as a voltage divider with a fixed resistor to produce an analog voltage readable on the NodeMCU's single analog input A0.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| LDR leg 1 | 3V3 (3.3V) |
| LDR leg 2 | A0 AND one leg of 10kΩ resistor |
| 10kΩ resistor other leg | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
|   ESP8266 NodeMCU |          |        LDR        |
|                   |          |  (light-dependent  |
|              3V3  |----------| resistor, leg 1)  |
|                   |          |        leg 2      |
|               A0  |----------+-------------------+
|                   |          |
|               GND |---+    [10kΩ]
|                   |   |      |
+-------------------+   +------+
                     (voltage divider midpoint -> A0)
```

NodeMCU boards include an on-board voltage divider that scales the A0 input range to 0–3.3V (rather than the bare ESP8266's 0–1.0V), so 3.3V from the board's 3V3 pin feeds the divider safely.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int LDR_PIN = A0;       // Only analog input on NodeMCU
const int NUM_SAMPLES = 10;   // Average multiple readings to reduce noise

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  delay(500);
  Serial.println("LDR Light Sensor Ready");
}

void loop() {
  // Take multiple samples and average them
  long sum = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < NUM_SAMPLES; i++) {
    sum += analogRead(LDR_PIN);
    delay(5);
  }
  int rawValue = sum / NUM_SAMPLES;

  // Convert raw ADC value (0-1023) to voltage (0-3.3V)
  float voltage = rawValue * (3.3f / 1023.0f);

  // Estimate LDR resistance: R_ldr = 10000 * V_ldr / (3.3 - V_ldr)
  // V_ldr = voltage across LDR = 3.3V - voltage at A0
  float v_ldr = 3.3f - voltage;
  float ldrResistance = (v_ldr > 0.01f) ? (10000.0f * v_ldr / voltage) : 999999.0f;

  // Map raw ADC to a simple 0-100% brightness estimate
  // Higher rawValue means more light (LDR resistance drops, voltage at A0 rises)
  int brightnessPercent = map(rawValue, 0, 1023, 0, 100);

  Serial.print("Raw ADC: ");
  Serial.print(rawValue);
  Serial.print(" | Voltage at A0: ");
  Serial.print(voltage, 3);
  Serial.print(" V | LDR Resistance: ");
  Serial.print(ldrResistance, 0);
  Serial.print(" Ohm | Brightness: ");
  Serial.print(brightnessPercent);
  Serial.println("%");

  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** The LDR and the 10kΩ fixed resistor form a voltage divider — as light increases the LDR's resistance drops, raising the voltage at the midpoint (A0), so a higher ADC reading means brighter light. The `ldrResistance` calculation inverts the divider formula to recover the actual LDR resistance, which is useful for calibrating against a datasheet lux curve.

A passive buzzer requires a PWM-capable pin to generate tones, so D5 is chosen here as a safe general-purpose output that avoids the boot-strapping pins.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| + (positive) | D5 (GPIO14) |
| - (negative) | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+------------------+          +------------------+
|  ESP8266 NodeMCU |          |  Passive Buzzer  |
|                  |          |                  |
|               D5 |----------| + (positive)     |
|              GND |----------| - (negative)     |
+------------------+          +------------------+
```

Passive buzzers (unlike active buzzers) contain no internal oscillator, so they must be driven with a PWM or toggled signal — simply applying DC voltage will produce no sound.

**Code**
```cpp
#define BUZZER_PIN D5

// Note frequencies in Hz
#define NOTE_C4  262
#define NOTE_D4  294
#define NOTE_E4  330
#define NOTE_F4  349
#define NOTE_G4  392
#define NOTE_A4  440
#define NOTE_B4  494
#define NOTE_C5  523
#define REST     0

// Simple melody: C major scale up and back down
int melody[] = {
  NOTE_C4, NOTE_D4, NOTE_E4, NOTE_F4,
  NOTE_G4, NOTE_A4, NOTE_B4, NOTE_C5,
  NOTE_B4, NOTE_A4, NOTE_G4, NOTE_F4,
  NOTE_E4, NOTE_D4, NOTE_C4, REST
};

// Duration in milliseconds for each note
int noteDurations[] = {
  300, 300, 300, 300,
  300, 300, 300, 600,
  300, 300, 300, 300,
  300, 300, 600, 400
};

int totalNotes = sizeof(melody) / sizeof(melody[0]);

void setup() {
  pinMode(BUZZER_PIN, OUTPUT);
}

void playMelody() {
  for (int i = 0; i < totalNotes; i++) {
    if (melody[i] == REST) {
      noTone(BUZZER_PIN);
    } else {
      tone(BUZZER_PIN, melody[i], noteDurations[i]);
    }
    // Small gap between notes for articulation
    delay(noteDurations[i] + 50);
    noTone(BUZZER_PIN);
  }
}

void loop() {
  playMelody();
  delay(2000); // Pause 2 seconds before repeating
}
```

**How it works:** The `tone()` function drives the pin with a square wave at the specified frequency using ESP8266's hardware timer, which is what causes the passive buzzer's diaphragm to vibrate and produce sound — without this oscillating signal, a passive buzzer stays silent. The 50 ms gap added after each note duration via `delay(noteDurations[i] + 50)` followed by `noTone()` creates brief silence between notes so they sound distinct rather than blurring together.

