| VCC (3.3V) | 3V3 |
| GND | GND |
| SCK | D5 (GPIO14) |
| MISO | D6 (GPIO12) |
| MOSI | D7 (GPIO13) |
| SDA (CS) | D8 (GPIO15) |
| RST | D2 (GPIO4) |
| IRQ | Not connected |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
|   ESP8266 NodeMCU |          |   MFRC522 RFID    |
|                   |          |                   |
|  3V3 o------------+----------+o VCC              |
|  GND o------------+----------+o GND              |
|                   |          |                   |
|   D5 (SCK)  o-----+----------+o SCK              |
|   D6 (MISO) o-----+----------+o MISO             |
|   D7 (MOSI) o-----+----------+o MOSI             |
|   D8 (CS)   o-----+----------+o SDA (CS)         |
|   D2 (RST)  o-----+----------+o RST              |
|                   |          |                   |
|                   |          |  IRQ  (NC)        |
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
```

The MFRC522 is strictly 3.3V — never connect VCC to the 5V/VIN pin or you risk permanently damaging the module.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <SPI.h>
#include <MFRC522.h>

// D8 = CS, D2 = RST (using NodeMCU silkscreen labels)
#define SS_PIN  D8
#define RST_PIN D2

MFRC522 rfid(SS_PIN, RST_PIN);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  while (!Serial); // wait for Serial on some boards

  SPI.begin();          // initialises SCK=D5, MISO=D6, MOSI=D7
  rfid.PCD_Init();      // initialise MFRC522

  Serial.println("MFRC522 RFID Reader ready.");
  Serial.println("Scan a card or tag...");
}

void loop() {
  // Look for a new card; return early if none present
  if (!rfid.PICC_IsNewCardPresent()) {
    return;
  }

  // Try to read the card's serial (UID)
  if (!rfid.PICC_ReadCardSerial()) {
    return;
  }

  // Print the UID byte by byte
  Serial.print("Card UID: ");
  for (byte i = 0; i < rfid.uid.size; i++) {
    if (rfid.uid.uidByte[i] < 0x10) {
      Serial.print("0"); // leading zero for single-digit hex
    }
    Serial.print(rfid.uid.uidByte[i], HEX);
    if (i < rfid.uid.size - 1) {
      Serial.print(":");
    }
  }
  Serial.println();

  // Print the card type
  MFRC522::PICC_Type piccType = rfid.PICC_GetType(rfid.uid.sak);
  Serial.print("Card type: ");
  Serial.println(rfid.PICC_GetTypeName(piccType));

  // Halt the card and stop encryption so the reader is ready for the next scan
  rfid.PICC_HaltA();
  rfid.PCD_StopCrypto1();
}
```

**How it works:** `rfid.PICC_IsNewCardPresent()` and `rfid.PICC_ReadCardSerial()` are called together as a two-step anti-collision sequence — skipping either one causes the reader to miss cards or block indefinitely. Calling `rfid.PICC_HaltA()` followed by `rfid.PCD_StopCrypto1()` at the end of each loop iteration resets the reader's internal state so it can detect the same card again on the next pass rather than ignoring it as already processed.

The microSD module uses the NodeMCU's hardware SPI bus (D5/D6/D7 for SCK/MISO/MOSI), with D8 as chip select — note that D8 is a boot-strapping pin so it must be kept LOW during boot, which is actually fine here since SPI CS idles LOW only when selected (the SD library manages this correctly).

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SCK | D5 (GPIO14) |
| MISO | D6 (GPIO12) |
| MOSI | D7 (GPIO13) |
| CS | D8 (GPIO15) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------------+          +-------------------+
|    ESP8266 NodeMCU      |          |  MicroSD Module   |
|                         |          |                   |
|  3.3V |-----------------+----------| VCC               |
|  GND  |-----------------+----------| GND               |
|  D5   |-----------------+----------| SCK               |
|  D6   |-----------------+----------| MISO              |
|  D7   |-----------------+----------| MOSI              |
|  D8   |-----------------+----------| CS                |
+-------------------------+          +-------------------+
```

Most microSD breakout modules include an onboard 3.3V regulator and level shifters, but verify yours — bare SD cards are 3.3V only and are NOT 5V tolerant on the data lines.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <SPI.h>
#include <SD.h>

const int SD_CS_PIN = D8;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  delay(500);
  Serial.println("Initializing SD card...");

  if (!SD.begin(SD_CS_PIN)) {
    Serial.println("SD card initialization failed! Check wiring and card.");
    while (true) { delay(1000); }
  }
  Serial.println("SD card initialized.");

  // Write a file
  File dataFile = SD.open("log.txt", FILE_WRITE);
  if (dataFile) {
    dataFile.println("Hello from ESP8266 NodeMCU!");
    dataFile.println("MicroSD SPI write test successful.");
    dataFile.close();
    Serial.println("File written successfully.");
  } else {
    Serial.println("Error opening log.txt for writing.");
  }

  // Read the file back
  dataFile = SD.open("log.txt");
  if (dataFile) {
    Serial.println("--- Contents of log.txt ---");
    while (dataFile.available()) {
      Serial.write(dataFile.read());
    }
    dataFile.close();
  } else {
    Serial.println("Error opening log.txt for reading.");
  }
}

void loop() {
  // Nothing to do here
}
```

**How it works:** `SD.begin(SD_CS_PIN)` initializes the SPI bus and negotiates with the card using the standard Arduino SD library, which handles the SPI clock-speed ramp-up sequence the SD protocol requires during card initialization. Opening the file with `FILE_WRITE` appends if the file exists and creates it if it does not, so repeated resets will accumulate log entries rather than overwriting them.

A pull-up resistor approach is used here so the button reads HIGH at rest and LOW when pressed, avoiding floating-pin noise without needing an external resistor since the ESP8266's internal pull-up is enabled in code.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| One leg of button | D2 (GPIO4) |
| Other leg of button | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------------+
| ESP8266 NodeMCU |
| |
| D2 (GPIO4) o-----------+
| | |
| GND o-------+ [Button]
| | |
+-------------------------+ +-+
```

No external resistor is needed because we enable the internal pull-up on D2; the pin is pulled to 3.3V by default and the button shorts it to GND when pressed.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int BUTTON_PIN = D2;
const unsigned long DEBOUNCE_DELAY_MS = 50;

int lastRawState = HIGH;
int stableButtonState = HIGH;
unsigned long lastDebounceTime = 0;

void setup() {
 Serial.begin(115200);
 pinMode(BUTTON_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);
 Serial.println("Button debounce demo ready.");
}

void loop() {
 int rawState = digitalRead(BUTTON_PIN);

 // If the raw reading changed, reset the debounce timer
 if (rawState != lastRawState) {
 lastDebounceTime = millis();
 }

 // Only accept the new state if it has been stable for DEBOUNCE_DELAY_MS
 if ((millis() - lastDebounceTime) >= DEBOUNCE_DELAY_MS) {
 if (rawState != stableButtonState) {
 stableButtonState = rawState;

 if (stableButtonState == LOW) {
 Serial.println("Button PRESSED");
 } else {
 Serial.println("Button RELEASED");
 }
 }
 }

 lastRawState = rawState;
}
```

**How it works:** The debounce logic only updates `stableButtonState` after the raw pin reading has remained unchanged for at least 50 ms, filtering out the rapid bouncing contacts that occur mechanically during a press. `INPUT_PULLUP` is the key hardware choice — it eliminates the need for an external resistor by internally pulling D2 high, so the logic is inverted (LOW = pressed).

A rotary encoder outputs two quadrature digital signals (CLT/DT) that require interrupt-capable pins for reliable step detection, plus a momentary push-button on its SW pin — all three need internal or external pull-ups since the encoder pulls lines low when activated.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| VCC (if present) | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| CLK (A) | D5 (GPIO14) |
| DT (B) | D6 (GPIO12) |
| SW (button) | D7 (GPIO13) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------------+          +-------------------+
|      ESP8266 NodeMCU    |          |   Rotary Encoder  |
|                         |          |                   |
|                    3.3V |----------| VCC               |
|                     GND |----------| GND               |
|              D5 (GPIO14)|----------| CLK (A)           |
|              D6 (GPIO12)|----------| DT  (B)           |
|              D7 (GPIO13)|----------| SW  (button)      |
+-------------------------+          +-------------------+
```
The ESP8266's internal pull-ups are enabled in code, so no external resistors are required for this module; however, if you see erratic readings, a 10kΩ pull-up from each signal line to 3.3V can improve noise immunity.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

#define CLK_PIN D5
#define DT_PIN  D6
#define SW_PIN  D7

volatile int encoderCount = 0;
volatile bool buttonPressed = false;

uint8_t lastCLK;

void IRAM_ATTR handleEncoder() {
  uint8_t currentCLK = digitalRead(CLK_PIN);
  if (currentCLK != lastCLK) {
    // If DT differs from CLK, rotation is clockwise
    if (digitalRead(DT_PIN) != currentCLK) {
      encoderCount++;
    } else {
      encoderCount--;
    }
    lastCLK = currentCLK;
  }
}

void IRAM_ATTR handleButton() {
  buttonPressed = true;
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  pinMode(CLK_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);
  pinMode(DT_PIN,  INPUT_PULLUP);
  pinMode(SW_PIN,  INPUT_PULLUP);

  lastCLK = digitalRead(CLK_PIN);

  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(CLK_PIN), handleEncoder, CHANGE);
  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(SW_PIN),  handleButton,  FALLING);

  Serial.println("Rotary Encoder Ready");
}

void loop() {
  static int lastCount = 0;

  if (encoderCount != lastCount) {
    Serial.print("Encoder count: ");
    Serial.println(encoderCount);
    lastCount = encoderCount;
  }

  if (buttonPressed) {
    buttonPressed = false;
    Serial.println("Button pressed! Count reset.");
    encoderCount = 0;
    lastCount = 0;
    delay(200); // simple debounce
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The `IRAM_ATTR` attribute places both ISRs in RAM rather than flash so they execute safely during interrupt context on the ESP8266 — omitting it can cause crashes. By comparing DT to CLK at the moment a CLK edge fires, the code determines rotation direction without needing a timer or polling loop, making it reliable even at moderate rotation speeds.

The HX711 uses a simple two-wire digital protocol (clock + data) that works on any free GPIO pins, so we avoid boot-strapping pins and pick stable GPIOs on D1 and D2 (leaving the I2C bus free for other peripherals if needed).

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| HX711 VCC | 3V3 |
| HX711 GND | GND |
| HX711 DT (Data) | D2 (GPIO4) |
| HX711 SCK (Clock) | D1 (GPIO5) |
| Load Cell E+ (Excitation+) | HX711 E+ |
| Load Cell E- (Excitation-) | HX711 E- |
| Load Cell A+ (Signal+) | HX711 A+ |
| Load Cell A- (Signal-) | HX711 A- |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------------+         +------------------+
|     ESP8266 NodeMCU     |         |      HX711       |
|                         |         |                  |
|                    3V3 |-------->| VCC              |
|                    GND |-------->| GND              |
|              D2 (GPIO4)|-------->| DT  (Data)       |
|              D1 (GPIO5)|-------->| SCK (Clock)      |
|                         |         |                  |
|                         |         | E+  ---+         |
|                         |         | E-  ---|--+      |
|                         |         | A+  ---|--|--+   |
|                         |         | A-  ---|--)--|+  |
+-------------------------+         +----|----|--|-|---+
                                         |    |  | |
                                    +----+----+--+-+---+
                                    |     Load Cell     |
                                    | (Wheatstone Bridge|
                                    |   Strain Gauge)   |
                                    +-------------------+
```

The HX711 operates at 3.3V without issue, so no level shifting is required when powered from the NodeMCU's 3V3 pin.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <HX711.h>

// HX711 pin definitions (use NodeMCU D-label silkscreen names)
const int LOADCELL_DT_PIN  = D2;  // Data pin
const int LOADCELL_SCK_PIN = D1;  // Clock pin

// Calibration factor: adjust this value until scale reads correctly.
// Positive or negative depending on load cell orientation.
// Steps: 1) Comment out scale.set_scale() line, read raw value with a known weight,
//        2) calibrationFactor = rawReading / knownWeightInGrams
float calibrationFactor = 420.0;

// Tare offset: weight reading with nothing on the scale (set at startup)
HX711 scale;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  delay(500);
  Serial.println("HX711 Load Cell Demo");

  scale.begin(LOADCELL_DT_PIN, LOADCELL_SCK_PIN);

  Serial.println("Taring scale -- remove all weight and wait...");
  delay(2000);

  scale.set_scale(calibrationFactor); // Apply calibration factor
  scale.tare();                        // Reset scale to zero

  Serial.println("Scale ready. Place weight on load cell.");
}

void loop() {
  if (scale.is_ready()) {
    float weight = scale.get_units(10); // Average of 10 readings for stability
    Serial.print("Weight: ");
    Serial.print(weight, 2);
    Serial.println(" g");
  } else {
    Serial.println("HX711 not ready -- check wiring.");
  }

  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** `scale.set_scale(calibrationFactor)` converts the raw 24-bit ADC counts from the HX711 into meaningful weight units — you must measure a known weight to find your specific load cell's factor, as it varies between cells. Calling `scale.get_units(10)` instead of `get_units(1)` averages 10 internal readings before returning, which significantly reduces noise inherent in strain-gauge measurements at the cost of a small time delay.

The IR receiver module outputs a single digital signal line, so only one data GPIO is needed alongside power — D5 is chosen here as a safe, interrupt-capable pin away from boot-strapping concerns.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| DATA (OUT) | D5 (GPIO14) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+------------------+          +-------------------+
|  ESP8266 NodeMCU |          |   IR Receiver     |
|                  |          |   Module (e.g.    |
|             3.3V |----------| VCC    VS1838B)   |
|              GND |----------| GND               |
|               D5 |----------| DATA / OUT        |
+------------------+          +-------------------+
```
The IR receiver module operates at 3.3V safely; avoid feeding it 5V directly from a NodeMCU that is USB-powered unless the specific module is rated for it.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <IRrecv.h>
#include <IRremoteESP8266.h>
#include <IRutils.h>

const uint16_t IR_RECV_PIN = D5; // GPIO14 -- interrupt-capable, safe for I/O
const uint16_t CAPTURE_BUFFER_SIZE = 1024;

IRrecv irrecv(IR_RECV_PIN, CAPTURE_BUFFER_SIZE, 15, true);
decode_results results;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  while (!Serial) { delay(10); }
  Serial.println("IR Receiver ready. Point remote and press a button.");
  irrecv.enableIRIn(); // Start the receiver interrupt
}

void loop() {
  if (irrecv.decode(&results)) {
    // Print protocol name, raw hex value, and bit length
    Serial.print("Protocol : ");
    Serial.println(typeToString(results.decode_type));
    Serial.print("Code     : 0x");
    Serial.println(results.value, HEX);
    Serial.print("Bits     : ");
    Serial.println(results.bits);
    Serial.println("---");
    irrecv.resume(); // Ready to receive the next signal
  }
}
```

**How it works:** `irrecv.enableIRIn()` sets up a timer-based interrupt that samples the DATA pin at 50 µs intervals to capture the pulse train without blocking `loop()`. Calling `irrecv.resume()` after each successful decode is essential — it clears the internal buffer so the library can capture the next incoming IR burst rather than repeatedly decoding the same result.

The flame sensor's digital output pin is connected to a standard GPIO so the ESP8266 can read a clean HIGH/LOW signal without needing the analog input.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| DO (Digital Out) | D2 (GPIO4) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+----------------------+          +-------------------+
|   ESP8266 NodeMCU    |          |  Flame Sensor Mod  |
|                      |          |                   |
|               3.3V  |----------| VCC               |
|                GND  |----------| GND               |
|          D2 (GPIO4) |----------| DO                |
+----------------------+          +-------------------+
```

The flame sensor module typically operates at 3.3V–5V; powering it from the NodeMCU's 3.3V pin keeps the DO logic level safe for the ESP8266's GPIO inputs.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int FLAME_PIN = D2;   // Digital output from flame sensor
const int LED_PIN   = D4;   // Onboard LED (active LOW on NodeMCU)

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(FLAME_PIN, INPUT);   // Read digital signal from sensor
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH); // LED off initially (inverted logic)
  Serial.println("Flame detector ready.");
}

void loop() {
  int flameDetected = digitalRead(FLAME_PIN);

  // Most flame sensor modules output LOW when flame is detected
  if (flameDetected == LOW) {
    Serial.println("WARNING: Flame detected!");
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);  // Turn onboard LED ON (active LOW)
  } else {
    Serial.println("No flame.");
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH); // Turn onboard LED OFF
  }

  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** Most flame sensor modules assert their DO pin LOW when a flame is detected (the onboard comparator pulls the output low), so the sketch treats a LOW reading as an alert condition. The onboard LED uses inverted logic on the NodeMCU (LOW = on), which conveniently mirrors this active-LOW convention for a visible indication.

The MQ-2's analog output (AOUT) connects to the NodeMCU's single analog pin A0, which on most NodeMCU boards includes a built-in voltage divider allowing up to 3.3V input — keep in mind the MQ-2's AOUT typically swings 0–5V, so an external voltage divider is needed to scale it down safely.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| VCC | VIN (5V via USB) |
| GND | GND |
| AOUT | A0 (via voltage divider: 10kΩ + 20kΩ to scale 5V → 3.3V) |
| DOUT | Not connected (optional: any free GPIO for threshold trigger) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
  +---------------------+         +-----------------------------+
  |      MQ-2 Sensor    |         |     ESP8266 NodeMCU         |
  |                     |         |                             |
  |  VCC  o-------------|---------|--- VIN (5V)                 |
  |  GND  o-------------|---------|--- GND                      |
  |                     |         |                             |
  |  AOUT o----+        |         |                             |
  |            |        |         |                             |
  |           [10kΩ]    |         |                             |
  |            |        |         |                             |
  |            +--------|---------|--- A0                       |
  |            |        |         |                             |
  |           [20kΩ]    |         |                             |
  |            |        |         |                             |
  |           GND       |         |                             |
  |                     |         |                             |
  |  DOUT o  (NC)       |         |                             |
  +---------------------+         +-----------------------------+
```
The 10kΩ/20kΩ resistor divider scales the MQ-2's 0–5V analog output down to 0–3.3V to protect the NodeMCU's A0 pin; omitting this risks damaging the ESP8266.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int GAS_SENSOR_PIN = A0;
const int SAMPLE_COUNT = 10;       // number of samples to average
const int WARM_UP_TIME_MS = 20000; // MQ-2 needs ~20s preheat for stable readings
const int GAS_THRESHOLD = 400;     // raw ADC threshold (0-1023) to trigger alert

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Serial.println("MQ-2 Gas Sensor - ESP8266 NodeMCU");
  Serial.print("Warming up sensor for ");
  Serial.print(WARM_UP_TIME_MS / 1000);
  Serial.println(" seconds...");
  delay(WARM_UP_TIME_MS);
  Serial.println("Sensor ready.");
}

int readAveragedADC(int pin, int samples) {
  long sum = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < samples; i++) {
    sum += analogRead(pin);
    delay(5);
  }
  return (int)(sum / samples);
}

void loop() {
  int rawValue = readAveragedADC(GAS_SENSOR_PIN, SAMPLE_COUNT);

  // Convert raw ADC (0-1023) to approximate voltage at A0 (0-3.3V)
  float voltage = rawValue * (3.3f / 1023.0f);

  // Back-calculate the actual sensor output voltage before the divider
  // Divider ratio: Vout = Vsensor * 20k/(10k+20k) = Vsensor * 0.6667
  float sensorVoltage = voltage / 0.6667f;

  Serial.print("Raw ADC: ");
  Serial.print(rawValue);
  Serial.print("  |  A0 Voltage: ");
  Serial.print(voltage, 3);
  Serial.print(" V  |  Sensor Voltage: ");
  Serial.print(sensorVoltage, 3);
  Serial.print(" V");

  if (rawValue > GAS_THRESHOLD) {
    Serial.println("  --> *** GAS/SMOKE DETECTED! ***");
  } else {
    Serial.println("  --> Air quality OK");
  }

  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** The `readAveragedADC()` function takes multiple samples and averages them to reduce the MQ-2's inherent analog noise, giving more stable readings. The divider ratio correction (`voltage / 0.6667`) mathematically reverses the voltage scaling so you can reason about the actual sensor output voltage rather than the divided-down value at the pin.

Each color channel of the RGB LED gets its own current-limiting resistor and is driven by a separate PWM-capable GPIO, keeping all three channels within the ESP8266's 3.3V logic and safe current limits.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| R (Red anode) | 220Ω resistor → D5 (GPIO14) |
| G (Green anode) | 220Ω resistor → D6 (GPIO12) |
| B (Blue anode) | 220Ω resistor → D7 (GPIO13) |
| Cathode (common) | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
|   ESP8266 NodeMCU   |          |  Common Cathode   |
|                     |          |     RGB LED       |
|  D5 (GPIO14) -------+--[220Ω]--+-- R (Red)         |
|                     |          |                   |
|  D6 (GPIO12) -------+--[220Ω]--+-- G (Green)       |
|                     |          |                   |
|  D7 (GPIO13) -------+--[220Ω]--+-- B (Blue)        |
|                     |          |                   |
|  GND ---------------+----------+-- Cathode (-)     |
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
```
Use at least 220Ω resistors on each channel to limit current to a safe ~10–15 mA per leg at 3.3V; without them you risk damaging both the LED and the GPIO pin.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// PWM-capable pins for each color channel
const int PIN_RED   = D5;  // GPIO14
const int PIN_GREEN = D6;  // GPIO12
const int PIN_BLUE  = D7;  // GPIO13

// Helper: set RGB color using 0-255 values per channel
void setColor(int red, int green, int blue) {
  analogWrite(PIN_RED,   red);
  analogWrite(PIN_GREEN, green);
  analogWrite(PIN_BLUE,  blue);
}

void setup() {
  pinMode(PIN_RED,   OUTPUT);
  pinMode(PIN_GREEN, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(PIN_BLUE,  OUTPUT);

  // ESP8266 analogWrite PWM range is 0-1023 by default;
  // use analogWriteRange to remap to familiar 0-255
  analogWriteRange(255);

  // Set PWM frequency to 1 kHz to avoid visible flicker
  analogWriteFreq(1000);
}

void loop() {
  // Red
  setColor(255, 0, 0);
  delay(1000);

  // Green
  setColor(0, 255, 0);
  delay(1000);

  // Blue
  setColor(0, 0, 255);
  delay(1000);

  // Yellow (Red + Green)
  setColor(255, 200, 0);
  delay(1000);

  // Cyan (Green + Blue)
  setColor(0, 255, 200);
  delay(1000);

  // Magenta (Red + Blue)
  setColor(200, 0, 255);
  delay(1000);

  // White (all channels)
  setColor(255, 255, 255);
  delay(1000);

  // Fade red in and out
  for (int brightness = 0; brightness <= 255; brightness++) {
    setColor(brightness, 0, 0);
    delay(5);
  }
  for (int brightness = 255; brightness >= 0; brightness--) {
    setColor(brightness, 0, 0);
    delay(5);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The call to `analogWriteRange(255)` is essential — by default the ESP8266 Arduino core uses a 10-bit PWM range (0–1023), so this remaps it to the familiar 8-bit 0–255 scale, making color values directly comparable to standard RGB notation. Because all three channels share a common cathode (ground), each anode is driven high with a PWM duty cycle that independently controls that channel's brightness, and blending all three produces mixed colors.

