|  GND ------------+--------->| GND            |
|                   |         | COM -- Door 1  |
|                   |         | NO  -- Motor 1 |
|                   |         +----------------+
|                   |
|                   |         +----------------+
|                   |         |    Relay 2     |
|  D2 (GPIO4) ------+-------->| IN  (Door 2)  |
|                   |         | VCC <-- 3V3    |
|  3V3 ------------+--------->| VCC            |
|  GND ------------+--------->| GND            |
|                   |         | COM -- Door 2  |
|                   |         | NO  -- Motor 2 |
+-------------------+         +----------------+
```

Most relay modules are active-LOW (the relay energizes when IN is pulled LOW), so the code accounts for this; also ensure your relay module is rated for the garage door motor voltage on the switched side.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// Relay control pins (active-LOW relay modules)
#define RELAY1_PIN D1  // GPIO5 -- controls Door 1
#define RELAY2_PIN D2  // GPIO4 -- controls Door 2

// Duration (ms) to hold the relay closed to simulate a button press
#define TRIGGER_PULSE_MS 500

void triggerDoor(uint8_t relayPin) {
  digitalWrite(relayPin, LOW);   // energize relay (active-LOW)
  delay(TRIGGER_PULSE_MS);
  digitalWrite(relayPin, HIGH);  // release relay
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  pinMode(RELAY1_PIN, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(RELAY2_PIN, OUTPUT);

  // Start with both relays de-energized (HIGH = off for active-LOW modules)
  digitalWrite(RELAY1_PIN, HIGH);
  digitalWrite(RELAY2_PIN, HIGH);

  Serial.println("Garage Door Controller Ready.");
  Serial.println("Send '1' to toggle Door 1, '2' to toggle Door 2.");
}

void loop() {
  if (Serial.available() > 0) {
    char cmd = Serial.read();

    if (cmd == '1') {
      Serial.println("Triggering Door 1...");
      triggerDoor(RELAY1_PIN);
      Serial.println("Door 1 triggered.");
    } else if (cmd == '2') {
      Serial.println("Triggering Door 2...");
      triggerDoor(RELAY2_PIN);
      Serial.println("Door 2 triggered.");
    }
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The `triggerDoor()` function briefly pulls the relay IN pin LOW for 500 ms then releases it back HIGH, mimicking a momentary wall-button press that most garage door openers expect. Both relays are explicitly initialized HIGH in `setup()` to prevent them from spuriously firing during the ESP8266's boot sequence, which can briefly float output pins.

The BME680 runs on 3.3V and uses the ESP8266's default I2C bus (D2/D1), so no level shifting is needed — connect SDA and SCL directly.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | D2 (GPIO4) |
| SCL | D1 (GPIO5) |
| SDO (ADDR) | GND (sets I2C address to 0x76) |
| CS | 3.3V (forces I2C mode) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+--------------------+          +-------------------+
|   ESP8266 NodeMCU  |          |      BME680        |
|                    |          |                    |
|               3.3V |----------| VCC                |
|                GND |----------| GND                |
|                GND |----------| SDO (ADDR=0x76)    |
|               3.3V |----------| CS  (I2C mode)     |
|          D2 (SDA)  |----------| SDA                |
|          D1 (SCL)  |----------| SCL                |
+--------------------+          +-------------------+
```
Tying CS high forces the BME680 into I2C mode; leaving SDO low sets the 7-bit I2C address to 0x76 (pull SDO high for 0x77 if you need two sensors on the same bus).

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_Sensor.h>
#include <Adafruit_BME680.h>

// BME680 I2C address with SDO tied to GND
#define BME680_I2C_ADDR 0x76

// Sea-level pressure in hPa (adjust for your location/elevation)
#define SEALEVEL_HPA 1013.25

Adafruit_BME680 bme;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  delay(500);
  Serial.println("BME680 I2C Example");

  // Wire.begin() uses D2 (SDA) and D1 (SCL) by default on NodeMCU
  Wire.begin();

  if (!bme.begin(BME680_I2C_ADDR)) {
    Serial.println("Could not find BME680 sensor! Check wiring and I2C address.");
    while (1) { delay(100); }
  }

  // Configure oversampling, IIR filter, and gas heater settings
  bme.setTemperatureOversampling(BME680_OS_8X);
  bme.setHumidityOversampling(BME680_OS_2X);
  bme.setPressureOversampling(BME680_OS_4X);
  bme.setIIRFilterSize(BME680_FILTER_SIZE_3);
  // Heat plate to 320°C for 150 ms to burn off contaminants for VOC reading
  bme.setGasHeater(320, 150);

  Serial.println("BME680 initialised successfully.");
}

void loop() {
  // performReading() blocks until a complete measurement cycle finishes
  if (!bme.performReading()) {
    Serial.println("Failed to perform BME680 reading.");
    delay(2000);
    return;
  }

  Serial.print("Temperature : ");
  Serial.print(bme.temperature, 2);
  Serial.println(" °C");

  Serial.print("Humidity    : ");
  Serial.print(bme.humidity, 2);
  Serial.println(" %");

  Serial.print("Pressure    : ");
  Serial.print(bme.pressure / 100.0, 2);
  Serial.println(" hPa");

  Serial.print("Altitude    : ");
  Serial.print(bme.readAltitude(SEALEVEL_HPA), 1);
  Serial.println(" m");

  Serial.print("Gas Resist. : ");
  Serial.print(bme.gas_resistance / 1000.0, 2);
  Serial.println(" kΩ");

  Serial.println("-----------------------------");
  delay(3000);
}
```

**How it works:** `bme.performReading()` triggers a forced-mode measurement that includes the gas heater warm-up time (150 ms here) and blocks until all five sensors have sampled, so there is no need to poll a "data ready" flag manually. The gas resistance value in kΩ rises with cleaner air — it is a raw VOC indicator rather than a calibrated ppm figure, which is why the Adafruit library exposes it directly without further conversion.

The TCS34725 uses I2C and runs on 3.3V, making it directly compatible with the NodeMCU's default I2C bus on D2/D1 without any level shifting.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | D2 (GPIO4) |
| SCL | D1 (GPIO5) |
| LED | 3.3V (or a GPIO pin to control the onboard white LED) |
| INT | Not connected (optional interrupt, unused here) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
  +----------------------+         +-------------------+
  |   ESP8266 NodeMCU    |         |    TCS34725       |
  |                      |         |   Color Sensor    |
  |  3.3V |--------------|---------|VCC                |
  |    GND|--------------|---------|GND                |
  | D2/SDA|--------------|---------|SDA                |
  | D1/SCL|--------------|---------|SCL                |
  |  3.3V |--------------|---------|LED  (always on)   |
  |       |              |         |INT  (NC)          |
  +----------------------+         +-------------------+
```

The TCS34725 is strictly a 3.3V device — do not connect VCC to the NodeMCU's VIN/5V rail or you risk damaging the sensor.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_TCS34725.h>

// Integration time and gain can be tuned for your lighting conditions
Adafruit_TCS34725 tcs = Adafruit_TCS34725(
    TCS34725_INTEGRATIONTIME_50MS,
    TCS34725_GAIN_4X
);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  delay(500);

  // NodeMCU I2C: SDA=D2 (GPIO4), SCL=D1 (GPIO5)
  Wire.begin(D2, D1);

  if (!tcs.begin()) {
    Serial.println("TCS34725 not found. Check wiring!");
    while (1) { delay(10); }
  }

  Serial.println("TCS34725 initialized.");
}

void loop() {
  uint16_t r, g, b, c;
  tcs.getRawData(&r, &g, &b, &c);

  // Compute color temperature and lux from raw readings
  uint16_t colorTemp = tcs.calculateColorTemperature_dn40(r, g, b, c);
  uint16_t lux       = tcs.calculateLux(r, g, b);

  Serial.print("R: "); Serial.print(r);
  Serial.print("  G: "); Serial.print(g);
  Serial.print("  B: "); Serial.print(b);
  Serial.print("  Clear: "); Serial.print(c);
  Serial.print("  ColorTemp: "); Serial.print(colorTemp); Serial.print(" K");
  Serial.print("  Lux: "); Serial.println(lux);

  // Delay accounts for integration time + some margin
  delay(200);
}
```

**How it works:** Passing `D2` and `D1` explicitly to `Wire.begin()` is essential on the NodeMCU because the ESP8266 Arduino core supports remapping I2C to any GPIO pair, and omitting them risks defaulting to the wrong pins. The `calculateColorTemperature_dn40()` method uses the Adafruit DN40 algorithm, which is more accurate than the simpler formula because it compensates for IR contamination in the raw RGBC channels.

The PCF8574 uses I2C, so it shares the NodeMCU's default SDA/SCL lines (D2/D1), and its 3-bit address pins (A0–A2) let you set the 7-bit I2C address — tying all three to GND gives address 0x20.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP8266 NodeMCU |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | D2 (GPIO4) |
| SCL | D1 (GPIO5) |
| A0 | GND (address bit 0 = 0) |
| A1 | GND (address bit 1 = 0) |
| A2 | GND (address bit 2 = 0) |
| P0–P7 | (free GPIO expander outputs, connect LEDs or devices) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
 +------------------+          +-------------------+
 | ESP8266 NodeMCU  |          |     PCF8574       |
 |                  |          |                   |
 |   3.3V  |--------+----------| VCC               |
 |   GND   |--------+----------| GND               |
 |         |        |          | A0 ]---> GND      |
 |   D2    |--------+----------| SDA      A1 ]---> GND |
 |   D1    |--------+----------| SCL      A2 ]---> GND |
 |                  |          |                   |
 |                  |          | P0 ]--> [LED/dev] |
 |                  |          | P1 ]--> [LED/dev] |
 |                  |          | P2 ]--> [LED/dev] |
 |                  |          | P3 ]--> [LED/dev] |
 |                  |          | P4 ]--> [LED/dev] |
 |                  |          | P5 ]--> [LED/dev] |
 |                  |          | P6 ]--> [LED/dev] |
 |                  |          | P7 ]--> [LED/dev] |
 +------------------+          +-------------------+
```
Add 4.7kΩ pull-up resistors from SDA and SCL to 3.3V if your PCF8574 module does not already include them.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>

#define PCF8574_ADDR 0x20  // A0=A1=A2=GND -> 0x20

// Write a full byte to all 8 pins of the PCF8574 at once.
// A bit value of 1 = HIGH, 0 = LOW on the corresponding P0-P7 pin.
void pcf8574Write(uint8_t value) {
  Wire.beginTransmission(PCF8574_ADDR);
  Wire.write(value);
  Wire.endTransmission();
}

// Read the current state of all 8 pins.
// To read a pin, it must first be set HIGH (quasi-bidirectional).
uint8_t pcf8574Read() {
  Wire.requestFrom(PCF8574_ADDR, 1);
  if (Wire.available()) {
    return Wire.read();
  }
  return 0xFF;  // default all high on read failure
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Wire.begin(D2, D1);  // SDA=D2, SCL=D1

  // Check if PCF8574 is reachable
  Wire.beginTransmission(PCF8574_ADDR);
  uint8_t error = Wire.endTransmission();
  if (error == 0) {
    Serial.println("PCF8574 found at 0x20");
  } else {
    Serial.print("PCF8574 NOT found, I2C error: ");
    Serial.println(error);
  }

  // Start with all pins LOW
  pcf8574Write(0x00);
}

void loop() {
  // Sweep: turn on each pin one at a time (left to right)
  for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
    pcf8574Write(1 << i);  // set only bit i HIGH
    Serial.print("Pin P");
    Serial.print(i);
    Serial.println(" HIGH");
    delay(300);
  }

  // All pins HIGH
  pcf8574Write(0xFF);
  Serial.println("All pins HIGH");
  delay(500);

  // All pins LOW
  pcf8574Write(0x00);
  Serial.println("All pins LOW");
  delay(500);

  // Demonstrate reading: set all pins HIGH first (required for input mode),
  // then read back the port state.
  pcf8574Write(0xFF);  // configure all as inputs (quasi-bidirectional)
  delay(10);
  uint8_t portState = pcf8574Read();
  Serial.print("PCF8574 port state: 0b");
  Serial.println(portState, BIN);
  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** The PCF8574 exposes all 8 GPIO pins through a single I2C byte — writing `1 << i` sets exactly one bit, toggling only that pin HIGH while the rest stay LOW, making bit-shifting the natural way to address individual pins. To use a pin as an input, you must first write it HIGH (the chip uses open-drain outputs with weak internal pull-ups), then call `Wire.requestFrom()` to read the port register back.

The MPU6050 uses I2C and runs on 3.3V logic, which matches the Teensy 4.0's native voltage, so no level shifting is needed on the data lines.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | Pin 18 (SDA) |
| SCL | Pin 19 (SCL) |
| AD0 | GND (sets I2C address to 0x68) |
| INT | (optional) any free GPIO, e.g. Pin 2 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+--------------------+          +-------------------+
|    Teensy 4.0      |          |     MPU6050        |
|                    |          |                    |
|  3.3V  o-----------+----------o VCC                |
|  GND   o-----------+----------o GND                |
|                    |    +-----o AD0 (addr sel)     |
|                    |    |     |                    |
|  Pin18 (SDA) o-----+----+-----o SDA                |
|  Pin19 (SCL) o-----+----------o SCL                |
|  Pin2  (INT) o-----+----------o INT  (optional)    |
+--------------------+          +-------------------+
                  GND rail
                  AD0 tied to GND --> address 0x68
```

The MPU6050 requires a 3.3V supply; do not connect VCC to the Teensy's 5V/VIN pin as this may damage the sensor.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>

// MPU6050 I2C address when AD0 pin is pulled LOW
const uint8_t MPU6050_ADDR = 0x68;

// Register addresses
const uint8_t PWR_MGMT_1   = 0x6B;
const uint8_t ACCEL_XOUT_H = 0x3B;
const uint8_t GYRO_XOUT_H  = 0x43;

// Scale factors (default full-scale ranges)
// Accel: ±2g  --> 16384 LSB/g
// Gyro:  ±250°/s --> 131 LSB/(°/s)
const float ACCEL_SCALE = 16384.0f;
const float GYRO_SCALE  = 131.0f;

void writeRegister(uint8_t reg, uint8_t value) {
  Wire.beginTransmission(MPU6050_ADDR);
  Wire.write(reg);
  Wire.write(value);
  Wire.endTransmission();
}

bool readBytes(uint8_t startReg, uint8_t count, uint8_t* buf) {
  Wire.beginTransmission(MPU6050_ADDR);
  Wire.write(startReg);
  if (Wire.endTransmission(false) != 0) return false;
  Wire.requestFrom((uint8_t)MPU6050_ADDR, count);
  for (uint8_t i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    if (!Wire.available()) return false;
    buf[i] = Wire.read();
  }
  return true;
}

int16_t combineBytes(uint8_t high, uint8_t low) {
  return (int16_t)((high << 8) | low);
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  while (!Serial && millis() < 3000);  // wait for Serial Monitor (up to 3s)

  Wire.begin();          // SDA=pin18, SCL=pin19 on Teensy 4.0
  Wire.setClock(400000); // Fast mode 400 kHz

  // Wake the MPU6050 -- it starts in sleep mode (PWR_MGMT_1 = 0x40)
  writeRegister(PWR_MGMT_1, 0x00); // clear sleep bit, use internal 8MHz oscillator
  delay(100); // allow sensor to stabilize

  Serial.println("MPU6050 initialized.");
  Serial.println("ax(g)\tay(g)\taz(g)\tgx(dps)\tgy(dps)\tgz(dps)");
}

void loop() {
  uint8_t buf[14];

  // Read 14 bytes starting from ACCEL_XOUT_H:
  // Accel X,Y,Z (6 bytes) + Temp (2 bytes) + Gyro X,Y,Z (6 bytes)
  if (!readBytes(ACCEL_XOUT_H, 14, buf)) {
    Serial.println("I2C read error!");
    delay(500);
    return;
  }

  int16_t rawAx = combineBytes(buf[0],  buf[1]);
  int16_t rawAy = combineBytes(buf[2],  buf[3]);
  int16_t rawAz = combineBytes(buf[4],  buf[5]);
  // buf[6] and buf[7] are raw temperature -- skipped here
  int16_t rawGx = combineBytes(buf[8],  buf[9]);
  int16_t rawGy = combineBytes(buf[10], buf[11]);
  int16_t rawGz = combineBytes(buf[12], buf[13]);

  float ax = rawAx / ACCEL_SCALE;
  float ay = rawAy / ACCEL_SCALE;
  float az = rawAz / ACCEL_SCALE;
  float gx = rawGx / GYRO_SCALE;
  float gy = rawGy / GYRO_SCALE;
  float gz = rawGz / GYRO_SCALE;

  Serial.print(ax, 3); Serial.print("\t");
  Serial.print(ay, 3); Serial.print("\t");
  Serial.print(az, 3); Serial.print("\t");
  Serial.print(gx, 2); Serial.print("\t");
  Serial.print(gy, 2); Serial.print("\t");
  Serial.println(gz, 2);

  delay(100);
}
```

**How it works:** Writing `0x00` to the `PWR_MGMT_1` register is essential because the MPU6050 boots in sleep mode and will return all zeros until explicitly woken up. All six motion axes are fetched in a single 14-byte burst read (starting at `ACCEL_XOUT_H`) rather than six separate transactions, which is both faster and ensures the accel and gyro samples are time-coherent.

The BME280 uses I2C at 3.3V logic, which matches the Teensy 4.0's native voltage, so no level shifting is needed — just connect it directly to the default I2C bus on pins 18 and 19.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | Pin 18 (SDA) |
| SCL | Pin 19 (SCL) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
|    Teensy 4.0     |          |      BME280        |
|                   |          |                    |
|          3.3V ----+----------+---- VCC            |
|            GND ---+----------+---- GND            |
|     SDA (Pin 18)--+----------+---- SDA            |
|     SCL (Pin 19)--+----------+---- SCL            |
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
```
Most BME280 breakout boards include onboard pull-up resistors on SDA and SCL; if using a bare module, add 4.7kΩ pull-ups from each data line to 3.3V.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_Sensor.h>
#include <Adafruit_BME280.h>

// BME280 default I2C address is 0x76 (some modules use 0x77)
#define BME280_I2C_ADDR 0x76

Adafruit_BME280 bme;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  while (!Serial) { delay(10); } // wait for Serial Monitor

  Wire.begin(); // SDA=18, SCL=19 by default on Teensy 4.0

  if (!bme.begin(BME280_I2C_ADDR)) {
    Serial.println("ERROR: BME280 not found. Check wiring and I2C address.");
    while (1) { delay(10); }
  }

  // Use "weather station" sampling mode for low-power, accurate readings
  bme.setSampling(
    Adafruit_BME280::MODE_FORCED,
    Adafruit_BME280::SAMPLING_X1,  // temperature
    Adafruit_BME280::SAMPLING_X1,  // pressure
    Adafruit_BME280::SAMPLING_X1,  // humidity
    Adafruit_BME280::FILTER_OFF
  );

  Serial.println("BME280 ready.");
}

void loop() {
  // In FORCED mode, trigger a single measurement then read results
  bme.takeForcedMeasurement();

  float tempC    = bme.readTemperature();        // degrees Celsius
  float humidity = bme.readHumidity();           // percent RH
  float pressure = bme.readPressure() / 100.0F; // hPa

  Serial.print("Temp: ");
  Serial.print(tempC, 1);
  Serial.print(" C  |  Humidity: ");
  Serial.print(humidity, 1);
  Serial.print(" %  |  Pressure: ");
  Serial.print(pressure, 2);
  Serial.println(" hPa");

  delay(2000);
}
```

**How it works:** `MODE_FORCED` puts the BME280 to sleep between measurements and requires an explicit `takeForcedMeasurement()` call to wake it and sample — this avoids the sensor self-heating that can skew temperature readings in continuous mode. Dividing the raw pressure by 100.0F converts Pascals to the more human-readable hectopascals (hPa / millibar) unit.

The Teensy 4.0 carrier/audio board exposes its built-in microSD slot via the dedicated SPI-connected pads internally, so no external wiring is needed — the SD uses the same SPI bus (pins 11/12/13) with chip-select on pin 10 by default on the standard Teensy 4.0 footprint.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| SD SCK (internal) | pin 13 (SPI SCK) |
| SD MISO (internal) | pin 12 (SPI MISO) |
| SD MOSI (internal) | pin 11 (SPI MOSI) |
| SD CS (internal) | pin 10 (SPI CS) |
| SD VCC (internal) | 3.3V |
| SD GND (internal) | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------------+         +----------------------+
|       Teensy 4.0        |         |  Built-in microSD    |
|                         |         |      Slot            |
|  pin 13 (SCK)  ---------|-------->| SCK                  |
|  pin 12 (MISO) ---------|---------| MISO                 |
|  pin 11 (MOSI) ---------|-------->| MOSI                 |
|  pin 10 (CS)   ---------|-------->| CS                   |
|  3.3V          ---------|-------->| VCC                  |
|  GND           ---------|-------->| GND                  |
|                         |         |                      |
+-------------------------+         +----------------------+
  (on standard Teensy 4.0 board, SD slot is internally wired
   to these SPI pins — no external jumpers required)
```

The microSD slot operates at 3.3V only — never connect an external SD module that runs at 5V logic directly to Teensy SPI pins without level shifting, though the built-in slot is already matched to 3.3V.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <SD.h>
#include <SPI.h>

const int SD_CS_PIN = 10; // Default CS pin for built-in SD slot on Teensy 4.0

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  while (!Serial && millis() < 3000) {
    // Wait for Serial monitor, timeout after 3s so it works headless
  }

  Serial.println("Initializing SD card...");

  if (!SD.begin(SD_CS_PIN)) {
    Serial.println("SD initialization failed! Check card is inserted.");
    while (true) {
      delay(1000); // Halt
    }
  }
  Serial.println("SD initialization successful.");

  // --- Write a file ---
  File outFile = SD.open("test.txt", FILE_WRITE);
  if (outFile) {
    outFile.println("Hello from Teensy 4.0!");
    outFile.printf("Millis at write: %lu\n", millis());
    outFile.close();
    Serial.println("File written successfully.");
  } else {
    Serial.println("Error opening test.txt for writing.");
  }

  // --- Read the file back ---
  File inFile = SD.open("test.txt", FILE_READ);
  if (inFile) {
    Serial.println("--- Contents of test.txt ---");
    while (inFile.available()) {
      Serial.write(inFile.read());
    }
    inFile.close();
    Serial.println("--- End of file ---");
  } else {
    Serial.println("Error opening test.txt for reading.");
  }

  // --- List root directory ---
  Serial.println("\nFiles on SD card:");
  File root = SD.open("/");
  while (true) {
    File entry = root.openNextFile();
    if (!entry) break;
    Serial.print("  ");
    Serial.print(entry.name());
    Serial.print("  ");
    Serial.print(entry.size());
    Serial.println(" bytes");
    entry.close();
  }
  root.close();
}

void loop() {
  // Nothing to do repeatedly in this demo
  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** `SD.begin(SD_CS_PIN)` initializes the SPI bus and card simultaneously — passing the correct CS pin is the only required configuration since the Teensy SD library auto-selects the hardware SPI peripheral tied to pins 11/12/13. Opening with `FILE_WRITE` appends if the file exists, so the `close()` call is critical to flush the FAT directory entry and avoid corruption.

The SG90 servo is controlled via a standard 50Hz PWM signal, so any PWM-capable Teensy 4.0 digital pin works — here pin 9 is used since it reliably supports PWM and keeps the signal wire away from the SPI/I2C clusters.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| VCC (Red) | 5V (VIN) |
| GND (Brown/Black) | GND |
| Signal (Orange/Yellow) | Pin 9 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------------+          +-------------------+
|       Teensy 4.0        |          |    SG90 Servo     |
|                         |          |                   |
|  VIN (5V) |-------------+----------| VCC  (Red)        |
|       GND |-------------+----------| GND  (Brown)      |
|     Pin 9 |-------------+----------| Signal (Orange)   |
+-------------------------+          +-------------------+
```

The SG90 draws up to ~200mA under load — powering from VIN (5V USB rail) is fine for a single servo in bench use, but for multiple servos or high-load scenarios consider a dedicated 5V supply with a shared GND.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Servo.h>

Servo sg90;

const int SERVO_PIN = 9;
const int MIN_ANGLE  = 0;
const int MAX_ANGLE  = 180;
const int STEP_DELAY = 15; // ms between each 1-degree step

void setup() {
  sg90.attach(SERVO_PIN, 500, 2400); // 500us = 0 deg, 2400us = 180 deg (SG90 calibrated)
  sg90.write(90); // start at center
  delay(500);
}

void loop() {
  // Sweep 0 -> 180
  for (int angle = MIN_ANGLE; angle <= MAX_ANGLE; angle++) {
    sg90.write(angle);
    delay(STEP_DELAY);
  }

  // Sweep 180 -> 0
  for (int angle = MAX_ANGLE; angle >= MIN_ANGLE; angle--) {
    sg90.write(angle);
    delay(STEP_DELAY);
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The `sg90.attach(SERVO_PIN, 500, 2400)` call is the key non-obvious line — it overrides the Arduino Servo library's default pulse-width range (544–2400 µs) with the SG90's actual calibrated range of 500–2400 µs, which ensures the servo reaches its true 0° and 180° endpoints without binding. The `STEP_DELAY` of 15 ms gives the servo mechanical time to move each degree, producing a smooth, jitter-free sweep.

The A4988 driver takes STEP and DIR digital signals from the Teensy, while the motor coils are powered by a separate 8–35V supply — keep the logic and motor power grounds tied together at one common GND point.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| A4988 VDD | 3.3V |
| A4988 GND (logic) | GND |
| A4988 VMOT | External motor supply 8–35V positive |
| A4988 GND (motor) | External motor supply GND (also tied to Teensy GND) |
| A4988 STEP | Pin 2 |
| A4988 DIR | Pin 3 |
| A4988 ENABLE | Pin 4 |
| A4988 MS1 | GND (full-step mode) |
| A4988 MS2 | GND (full-step mode) |
| A4988 MS3 | GND (full-step mode) |
| A4988 RST | A4988 SLP (tied together) |
| A4988 1A, 1B | Stepper coil A leads |
| A4988 2A, 2B | Stepper coil B leads |
| 100µF capacitor | Across VMOT and motor GND (near driver) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
|      Teensy 4.0     |          |   A4988 Driver    |
|                     |          |                   |
|  3.3V ─────────────────────── VDD                 |
|  GND  ─────────────────────── GND (logic)         |
|  Pin2 (STEP) ───────────────── STEP               |
|  Pin3 (DIR)  ───────────────── DIR                |
|  Pin4 (ENABLE) ─────────────── ENABLE             |
|                     |          |                   |
+---------------------+          |  RST ─── SLP      |
                                 |  MS1/MS2/MS3 ─ GND|
                                 |                   |
        +12V ────────────────── VMOT                 |
        GND  ────────────────── GND (motor) ─── Teensy GND
                                 |  [100µF cap across |
                                 |   VMOT & motor GND]|
                                 |                   |
                                 |  1A ───┐  Coil A  |
                                 |  1B ───┘  leads   |
                                 |  2A ───┐  Coil B  |
                                 |  2B ───┘  leads   |
                                 +-------------------+
                                          │
                                 +--------+--------+
                                 | Bipolar Stepper  |
                                 |     Motor        |
                                 +------------------+
```

A 100µF (or larger) electrolytic capacitor across VMOT and motor GND is essential to suppress voltage spikes that can destroy the A4988 when the motor coils switch; also set the A4988 current-limit potentiometer to match your motor's rated current before running.

**Code**
```cpp
// A4988 Bipolar Stepper Motor control on Teensy 4.0
// Full-step mode (MS1/MS2/MS3 all tied LOW)
// Adjust STEPS_PER_REV and STEP_DELAY_US to match your motor

const int STEP_PIN   = 2;
const int DIR_PIN    = 3;
const int ENABLE_PIN = 4;

// Many 1.8-degree/step motors have 200 steps per revolution
const int STEPS_PER_REV = 200;

// Minimum pulse width for A4988 is 1us; use higher value for slower/safer stepping
const unsigned int STEP_DELAY_US = 2000; // microseconds between step pulses (controls speed)

void setup() {
  pinMode(STEP_PIN,   OUTPUT);
  pinMode(DIR_PIN,    OUTPUT);
  pinMode(ENABLE_PIN, OUTPUT);

  // ENABLE is active LOW on A4988 — pull LOW to energize coils
  digitalWrite(ENABLE_PIN, LOW);

  // Start with a known direction
  digitalWrite(DIR_PIN, HIGH);

  Serial.begin(115200);
  Serial.println("A4988 Stepper ready.");
}

void stepMotor(int steps, bool direction) {
  digitalWrite(DIR_PIN, direction ? HIGH : LOW);
  // Small settling time after changing direction
  delayMicroseconds(5);

  for (int i = 0; i < steps; i++) {
    // A4988 steps on the rising edge of STEP pin
    digitalWrite(STEP_PIN, HIGH);
    delayMicroseconds(10);          // Minimum HIGH pulse width (1us min, 10us safe)
    digitalWrite(STEP_PIN, LOW);
    delayMicroseconds(STEP_DELAY_US); // Controls motor speed
  }
}

void loop() {
  // Rotate one full revolution clockwise
  Serial.println("CW one revolution");
  stepMotor(STEPS_PER_REV, true);
  delay(500);

  // Rotate one full revolution counter-clockwise
  Serial.println("CCW one revolution");
  stepMotor(STEPS_PER_REV, false);
  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** The A4988 advances the motor by exactly one step on each rising edge of the STEP pin, so the `digitalWrite(STEP_PIN, HIGH)` followed by a brief pulse and then `LOW` is the core motion primitive — adjusting `STEP_DELAY_US` between pulses directly controls rotational speed without any special library. The ENABLE pin is held LOW permanently here to keep the coils energized and holding torque; you can toggle it HIGH to de-energize the motor and reduce heat when idle.

The Teensy 4.0 has two DAC-capable pins (A21/pin 40 and A22/pin 41 on the underside pads, but the most accessible analog output is via `analogWriteResolution` on PWM pins) — since the Teensy 4.0 lacks a true voltage-output DAC on standard through-hole pins, we use a high-resolution PWM pin with a simple RC low-pass filter to synthesize a sine-wave-like tone into the piezo buzzer.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| Buzzer + (positive) | Pin 14 (via 100Ω series resistor) |
| Buzzer - (negative) | GND |
| RC filter: 100Ω resistor | Between Pin 14 and buzzer + |
| RC filter: 0.1µF capacitor | Between buzzer + and GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+------------------+          100Ω           +------------------+
|   Teensy 4.0     |        resistor          |  Piezo Buzzer    |
|                  |                          |                  |
|  Pin 14 (PWM) ---+---[100Ω]---+--- (+) BUZ+|                  |
|                  |            |             |                  |
|                  |           === 0.1µF      |                  |
|                  |            |             |                  |
|  GND ------------+-----------GND---- (-) BUZ|                  |
+------------------+                          +------------------+
```
The 100Ω + 0.1µF RC filter smooths the PWM signal before it reaches the piezo, reducing harsh switching noise and producing a cleaner audible tone.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int BUZZER_PIN = 14;   // PWM-capable pin used as pseudo-DAC output
const int PWM_RESOLUTION = 12; // 12-bit resolution = 0..4095
const int PWM_FREQ = 150000;   // High PWM carrier frequency (150 kHz) -- well above audible range

// Tone parameters
const float TONE_FREQ = 440.0f;  // A4 note in Hz
const int   TONE_DURATION_MS = 500;
const int   SILENCE_MS = 300;

// Sine-wave approximation table (12-bit, 64 samples)
const int SINE_TABLE_SIZE = 64;
uint16_t sineTable[SINE_TABLE_SIZE];

IntervalTimer toneTimer;
volatile int sineIndex = 0;
volatile bool playing = false;

void buildSineTable() {
  for (int i = 0; i < SINE_TABLE_SIZE; i++) {
    // Map sine (-1..1) to 12-bit range centered at mid-scale (2048)
    sineTable[i] = (uint16_t)(2047.5f + 2047.5f * sinf(2.0f * PI * i / SINE_TABLE_SIZE));
  }
}

void IRAM_ATTR sineISR() {
  if (playing) {
    analogWrite(BUZZER_PIN, sineTable[sineIndex]);
    sineIndex = (sineIndex + 1) % SINE_TABLE_SIZE;
  } else {
    analogWrite(BUZZER_PIN, 2048); // DC mid-point silence
  }
}

void setup() {
  analogWriteResolution(PWM_RESOLUTION);
  analogWriteFrequency(BUZZER_PIN, PWM_FREQ);
  pinMode(BUZZER_PIN, OUTPUT);
  analogWrite(BUZZER_PIN, 2048); // Start at midpoint

  buildSineTable();

  // ISR fires at TONE_FREQ * SINE_TABLE_SIZE to step through the sine table
  float isrRate = TONE_FREQ * SINE_TABLE_SIZE; // 440 * 64 = 28160 Hz
  toneTimer.begin(sineISR, 1000000.0f / isrRate); // period in microseconds
}

void loop() {
  // Play tone for TONE_DURATION_MS, then silence for SILENCE_MS
  playing = true;
  delay(TONE_DURATION_MS);

  playing = false;
  delay(SILENCE_MS);
}
```

**How it works:** The `IntervalTimer` fires an ISR at a rate of `TONE_FREQ × SINE_TABLE_SIZE` (28,160 Hz for A4), stepping through a precomputed 12-bit sine lookup table and writing each value via `analogWrite` — this advances the sine wave at exactly 440 Hz regardless of loop timing. Setting the PWM carrier frequency to 150 kHz pushes it far above human hearing so the RC filter only needs to suppress the carrier, leaving the 440 Hz audio tone intact.

A rotary encoder outputs two quadrature digital signals (CLK and DT) that require interrupt-capable pins for reliable edge detection, plus a momentary switch (SW) for the push-button click.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| CLK (A) | Pin 2 (interrupt-capable) |
| DT (B) | Pin 3 (interrupt-capable) |
| SW | Pin 4 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +----------------------+
|   Rotary Encoder  |          |     Teensy 4.0       |
|                   |          |                      |
|  VCC  o-----------+----------+-o 3.3V               |
|  GND  o-----------+----------+-o GND                |
|  CLK  o-----------+----------+-o Pin 2 (INT)        |
|  DT   o-----------+----------+-o Pin 3 (INT)        |
|  SW   o-----------+----------+-o Pin 4              |
+-------------------+          +----------------------+
```
The Teensy 4.0 has internal pull-up resistors available via INPUT_PULLUP, so no external resistors are needed for the encoder or switch lines.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int PIN_CLK = 2;
const int PIN_DT  = 3;
const int PIN_SW  = 4;

volatile long encoderPosition = 0;
volatile int lastClkState = HIGH;

bool lastButtonState = HIGH;
bool buttonPressed   = false;

void IRAM_ATTR encoderISR() {
  int clkState = digitalRead(PIN_CLK);
  int dtState  = digitalRead(PIN_DT);

  if (clkState != lastClkState) {
    // Determine direction from DT relative to CLK edge
    if (dtState != clkState) {
      encoderPosition++;
    } else {
      encoderPosition--;
    }
    lastClkState = clkState;
  }
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  pinMode(PIN_CLK, INPUT_PULLUP);
  pinMode(PIN_DT,  INPUT_PULLUP);
  pinMode(PIN_SW,  INPUT_PULLUP);

  lastClkState = digitalRead(PIN_CLK);

  // Trigger on any change of CLK for full quadrature resolution
  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(PIN_CLK), encoderISR, CHANGE);
  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(PIN_DT),  encoderISR, CHANGE);

  Serial.println("Rotary Encoder Ready");
}

void loop() {
  // Print position whenever it changes
  static long lastPosition = 0;
  long currentPosition = encoderPosition; // atomic read (32-bit aligned on CM7)
  if (currentPosition != lastPosition) {
    Serial.print("Position: ");
    Serial.println(currentPosition);
    lastPosition = currentPosition;
  }

  // Debounce button with simple state-change detection
  bool currentButtonState = digitalRead(PIN_SW);
  if (currentButtonState == LOW && lastButtonState == HIGH) {
    delay(20); // debounce
    if (digitalRead(PIN_SW) == LOW) {
      Serial.println("Button pressed! Position reset to 0.");
      encoderPosition = 0;
    }
  }
  lastButtonState = currentButtonState;
}
```

**How it works:** Attaching interrupts to both CLK and DT on CHANGE events gives full quadrature (4x) resolution — by comparing the state of DT against CLK at every edge, the ISR correctly determines direction. The encoder position variable is declared `volatile` so the compiler never caches it in a register, ensuring the main loop always reads the ISR-updated value.

The SSD1306 OLED uses the Teensy 4.0's default I2C bus (pins 18/19), so no extra configuration is needed beyond calling Wire.begin().

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | Pin 18 (SDA) |
| SCL | Pin 19 (SCL) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
|     Teensy 4.0      |          |   SSD1306 OLED    |
|                     |          |                   |
|  3.3V |-------------+----------| VCC               |
|   GND |-------------+----------| GND               |
| Pin18 |------- SDA -+----------| SDA               |
| Pin19 |------- SCL -+----------| SCL               |
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
```

The SSD1306 runs on 3.3V; do not connect VCC to 5V on modules that lack an onboard regulator, as this may damage the display.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>
#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>

#define SCREEN_WIDTH  128
#define SCREEN_HEIGHT  64
#define OLED_RESET     -1   // no reset pin used
#define OLED_I2C_ADDR 0x3C

Adafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, OLED_RESET);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  // Wire.begin() is called internally by the library, but calling it
  // explicitly here makes the bus initialisation order clear.
  Wire.begin();

  if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, OLED_I2C_ADDR)) {
    Serial.println("SSD1306 allocation failed -- check wiring!");
    while (true) { ; }  // halt
  }

  display.clearDisplay();

  // Draw a title line in large text
  display.setTextSize(2);
  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);
  display.setCursor(0, 0);
  display.println("Teensy 4.0");

  // Draw a subtitle in normal text
  display.setTextSize(1);
  display.setCursor(0, 20);
  display.println("SSD1306 I2C Demo");
  display.setCursor(0, 32);
  display.println("Hello, World!");

  display.display();  // push buffer to the hardware
}

void loop() {
  // Static display -- nothing to update each frame
}
```

**How it works:** `display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, OLED_I2C_ADDR)` initialises the panel using its internal charge-pump to generate the ~7 V needed by the OLED pixels, so no external boost supply is required. All drawing calls write to an in-RAM framebuffer and only `display.display()` actually transfers that buffer to the screen over I2C, which is why it must be called after every batch of drawing operations.

The MFRC522 uses standard SPI with an additional RST pin, and since the Teensy 4.0 operates at 3.3V logic (which the MFRC522 also requires), no level shifting is needed.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| VCC (3.3V) | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SCK | Pin 13 (SPI SCK) |
| MISO | Pin 12 (SPI MISO) |
| MOSI | Pin 11 (SPI MOSI) |
| SDA (SS/CS) | Pin 10 (SPI CS) |
| RST | Pin 9 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
|    Teensy 4.0     |          |     MFRC522        |
|                   |          |                   |
|  3.3V  -----------|----------|-  VCC             |
|  GND   -----------|----------|-  GND             |
|  Pin13 -----------|----------|-  SCK             |
|  Pin12 -----------|----------|-  MISO            |
|  Pin11 -----------|----------|-  MOSI            |
|  Pin10 -----------|----------|-  SDA (CS)        |
|  Pin 9 -----------|----------|-  RST             |
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
```

Power the MFRC522 strictly from 3.3V — applying 5V will permanently damage the module.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <SPI.h>
#include <MFRC522.h>

#define SS_PIN  10
#define RST_PIN 9

MFRC522 rfid(SS_PIN, RST_PIN);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  while (!Serial);
  SPI.begin();
  rfid.PCD_Init();
  Serial.println("MFRC522 RFID Reader ready.");
  Serial.println("Hold a card near the reader...");
}

void loop() {
  // Check for a new card present and readable
  if (!rfid.PICC_IsNewCardPresent() || !rfid.PICC_ReadCardSerial()) {
    return;
  }

  Serial.print("Card UID: ");
  for (byte i = 0; i < rfid.uid.size; i++) {
    if (rfid.uid.uidByte[i] < 0x10) {
      Serial.print("0"); // leading zero for single hex digits
    }
    Serial.print(rfid.uid.uidByte[i], HEX);
    if (i < rfid.uid.size - 1) Serial.print(":");
  }
  Serial.println();

  // Print card type
  MFRC522::PICC_Type piccType = rfid.PICC_GetType(rfid.uid.sak);
  Serial.print("Card Type: ");
  Serial.println(rfid.PICC_GetTypeName(piccType));

  // Halt the card and stop encryption to allow re-reading
  rfid.PICC_HaltA();
  rfid.PCD_StopCrypto1();

  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** The two-step check `PICC_IsNewCardPresent()` followed by `PICC_ReadCardSerial()` is required by the MFRC522 library — the first wakes and detects a card in the field, while the second performs the anti-collision loop and loads the UID into `rfid.uid`. Calling `PICC_HaltA()` and `PCD_StopCrypto1()` at the end puts the card back into an idle state so the reader can detect it again on the next loop iteration.

A single GPIO output pin drives the relay module's control input directly, since most relay modules accept a 3.3V logic signal and handle their own coil flyback protection internally.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V (VIN) |
| GND | GND |
| IN (signal) | Pin 2 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
|    Teensy 4.0     |          |  Relay Module     |
|                   |          |                   |
|           VIN(5V) |----------| VCC               |
|               GND |----------| GND               |
|            Pin 2  |----------| IN                |
|                   |          |                   |
|                   |          |  [NO / COM / NC]  |
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
```
Note: the relay module's onboard optocoupler and flyback diode protect the Teensy; always connect the load (e.g., lamp, motor) to the relay's COM and NO/NC terminals, never directly to the Teensy.

**Code**
```cpp
const int RELAY_PIN = 2;

void setup() {
  pinMode(RELAY_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, LOW); // ensure relay starts de-energized
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Serial.println("Relay control ready");
}

void loop() {
  // Turn relay ON
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, HIGH);
  Serial.println("Relay ON");
  delay(2000);

  // Turn relay OFF
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, LOW);
  Serial.println("Relay OFF");
  delay(2000);
}
```

**How it works:** The `digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, LOW)` call in `setup()` is intentional — it ensures the relay starts in a known de-energized state before the loop runs, preventing unintended load activation at power-up. Most relay modules are active-HIGH (coil energizes when IN is HIGH), but some are active-LOW — if your relay behavior is inverted, simply swap HIGH and LOW in the sketch.

The DS18B20 uses the OneWire single-wire protocol, so only one data pin is needed alongside a 4.7kΩ pull-up resistor to keep the bus high at idle.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| DATA | Pin 2 (with 4.7kΩ resistor to 3.3V) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        Teensy 4.0
       ┌───────────┐
  3.3V─┤ 3.3V      ├─────────────────────┐
       │           │                     │
   GND─┤ GND       │                 4.7kΩ
       │           │                     │
  Pin2─┤ GPIO 2    ├─────────────────────┴───── DS18B20
       └───────────┘                           ┌────────┐
                                               │  VCC   ├── 3.3V
                                               │  GND   ├── GND
                                               │  DATA  ├── Pin 2
                                               └────────┘
```
The 4.7kΩ pull-up resistor is mandatory — without it the OneWire bus will not function correctly and readings will fail.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <OneWire.h>
#include <DallasTemperature.h>

#define ONE_WIRE_BUS 2  // DS18B20 data pin connected to Teensy pin 2

OneWire oneWire(ONE_WIRE_BUS);
DallasTemperature sensors(&oneWire);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  while (!Serial) { ; }  // Wait for Serial Monitor on Teensy
  sensors.begin();
  Serial.println("DS18B20 Temperature Sensor Ready");
  Serial.print("Devices found on bus: ");
  Serial.println(sensors.getDeviceCount());
}

void loop() {
  sensors.requestTemperatures();  // Send global conversion request to all sensors

  float tempC = sensors.getTempCByIndex(0);  // Get result from first sensor
  float tempF = sensors.toFahrenheit(tempC);

  if (tempC == DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C) {
    Serial.println("ERROR: Could not read temperature — check wiring!");
  } else {
    Serial.print("Temperature: ");
    Serial.print(tempC, 2);
    Serial.print(" °C  /  ");
    Serial.print(tempF, 2);
    Serial.println(" °F");
  }

  delay(1000);
}
```

**How it works:** `sensors.requestTemperatures()` triggers the DS18B20 to perform its internal analog-to-digital conversion (which takes up to 750ms at 12-bit resolution) before the result is ready to be fetched — calling `getTempCByIndex(0)` immediately after without this request would return stale or invalid data. Checking the return value against `DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C` is important because the library returns that sentinel value when the sensor is missing or the CRC fails, preventing silent garbage readings.

The HC-SR04 uses two dedicated digital pins — one to trigger a pulse and one to receive the echo — so any two free GPIO pins on the Teensy 4.0 work fine; the sensor runs at 5V but its ECHO output is also 5V, which is tolerable on most Teensy 4.0 digital pins.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| VCC | VIN (5V) |
| GND | GND |
| TRIG | Pin 2 |
| ECHO | Pin 3 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +-------------------------+
|     HC-SR04       |          |       Teensy 4.0        |
|                   |          |                         |
|  VCC  o-----------+----------o VIN (5V)                |
|  GND  o-----------+----------o GND                     |
|  TRIG o-----------+----------o Pin 2                   |
|  ECHO o-----------+----------o Pin 3                   |
+-------------------+          +-------------------------+
```
Note: The HC-SR04 ECHO pin outputs 5V logic; most Teensy 4.0 digital pins are 5V tolerant, but verify your specific board revision before connecting.

**Code**
```cpp
#define TRIG_PIN 2
#define ECHO_PIN 3

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(TRIG_PIN, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(ECHO_PIN, INPUT);
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, LOW);
}

float readDistanceCm() {
  // Send a 10-microsecond HIGH pulse to trigger a measurement
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, LOW);
  delayMicroseconds(2);
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, HIGH);
  delayMicroseconds(10);
  digitalWrite(TRIG_PIN, LOW);

  // Measure how long the ECHO pin stays HIGH (in microseconds)
  long duration = pulseIn(ECHO_PIN, HIGH, 30000); // 30ms timeout

  // Convert travel time to distance: sound travels ~0.0343 cm/us,
  // divide by 2 because the pulse travels to target and back
  if (duration == 0) return -1.0; // timeout / no object detected
  return (duration * 0.0343f) / 2.0f;
}

void loop() {
  float distance = readDistanceCm();
  if (distance < 0) {
    Serial.println("No object detected (timeout)");
  } else {
    Serial.print("Distance: ");
    Serial.print(distance, 1);
    Serial.println(" cm");
  }
  delay(200);
}
```

**How it works:** `pulseIn(ECHO_PIN, HIGH, 30000)` blocks until the ECHO pin goes HIGH, then times how long it stays HIGH (in microseconds), returning 0 on timeout — this raw travel time is then divided by 2 and scaled by the speed of sound (0.0343 cm/µs) to get one-way distance. The 30 ms timeout prevents the code from hanging indefinitely when no object is in range (the HC-SR04's effective range is roughly 2–400 cm).

A passive buzzer requires a PWM-capable pin to produce sound, since it needs an oscillating signal rather than a steady voltage like an active buzzer.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| + (positive) | Pin 9 (PWM) |
| - (negative) | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
|      Teensy 4.0     |          |   Passive Buzzer  |
|                     |          |                   |
|           Pin 9 PWM |----------| + (positive)      |
|                     |          |                   |
|                 GND |----------| - (negative)      |
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
```

No resistor is strictly required for a passive buzzer at 3.3V logic, but a 100Ω series resistor on the signal line is a good practice to limit current draw from the Teensy pin.

**Code**
```cpp
// Passive buzzer on Teensy 4.0 using tone()/noTone() for melody playback

const int BUZZER_PIN = 9;

// Frequency definitions (Hz)
#define NOTE_C4  262
#define NOTE_D4  294
#define NOTE_E4  330
#define NOTE_F4  349
#define NOTE_G4  392
#define NOTE_A4  440
#define NOTE_B4  494
#define NOTE_C5  523

// Simple melody: frequencies and durations (ms)
int melody[] = {
  NOTE_C4, NOTE_D4, NOTE_E4, NOTE_F4,
  NOTE_G4, NOTE_A4, NOTE_B4, NOTE_C5
};

int durations[] = {
  300, 300, 300, 300,
  300, 300, 300, 600
};

const int MELODY_LENGTH = sizeof(melody) / sizeof(melody[0]);

void setup() {
  pinMode(BUZZER_PIN, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  for (int i = 0; i < MELODY_LENGTH; i++) {
    tone(BUZZER_PIN, melody[i], durations[i]);
    // Add a small gap between notes to make them distinct
    delay(durations[i] + 50);
  }

  noTone(BUZZER_PIN);
  delay(1000); // Pause before repeating
}
```

**How it works:** `tone()` drives the pin with a PWM square wave at the specified frequency, which mechanically vibrates the buzzer's diaphragm to produce sound — this is why a passive buzzer needs an oscillating signal rather than a constant HIGH. The 50ms gap added after each note duration prevents notes from blending together into a continuous drone when played back-to-back.

A potentiometer is a simple resistive voltage divider, so its wiper output connects to any analog-capable pin on the Teensy 4.0, with the two outer terminals tied to 3.3V and GND to keep the signal within the ADC's input range.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| Left terminal (or Pin 1) | 3.3V |
| Wiper (center / Pin 2) | Pin 14 (A0) |
| Right terminal (or Pin 3) | GND |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
        +------------------+
        |   Teensy 4.0     |
        |                  |
        |  3.3V o----------+----> POT Pin 1 (left)
        |                  |
        |  A0   o----------+----> POT Pin 2 (wiper)
        |  (14)            |
        |  GND  o----------+----> POT Pin 3 (right)
        |                  |
        +------------------+

             [Potentiometer]
          Pin1   Pin2   Pin3
           |      |      |
          [====[>]======]
          (resistive track + wiper)
```
Keep the potentiometer supply tied to the Teensy's 3.3V rail (not 5V) so the wiper voltage never exceeds the ADC's 3.3V maximum input.

**Code**
```cpp
// Read a potentiometer wired to Teensy 4.0 analog pin A0 (pin 14).
// The Teensy 4.0 ADC is 12-bit by default via analogReadResolution().

const int POT_PIN = A0;   // Wiper connected to pin 14 / A0
const int ADC_BITS = 12;  // Teensy 4.0 supports up to 12-bit ADC resolution

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  while (!Serial && millis() < 3000) { /* wait for Serial Monitor */ }

  analogReadResolution(ADC_BITS);  // Set ADC to 12-bit (0–4095)
  pinMode(POT_PIN, INPUT);         // Explicitly set pin as input (optional but clear)

  Serial.println("Potentiometer reader ready.");
}

void loop() {
  int raw = analogRead(POT_PIN);                          // 0 – 4095
  float voltage = raw * (3.3f / 4095.0f);                // Convert to volts
  float percent = (raw / 4095.0f) * 100.0f;              // Knob position 0–100 %

  Serial.print("Raw: ");
  Serial.print(raw);
  Serial.print("  Voltage: ");
  Serial.print(voltage, 3);
  Serial.print(" V  Position: ");
  Serial.print(percent, 1);
  Serial.println(" %");

  delay(200);
}
```

**How it works:** Calling `analogReadResolution(12)` unlocks the Teensy 4.0's full 12-bit ADC range (0–4095) instead of the Arduino-default 10-bit range, giving roughly four times finer position resolution. The voltage conversion uses 3.3 as the reference because the potentiometer supply—and therefore the wiper's maximum voltage—is tied to the 3.3V rail rather than 5V.

The joystick's X and Y axes output analog voltages (0–3.3V range works directly with Teensy 4.0's 3.3V ADC), and the push-button switch is read as a digital input with internal pull-up.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| VRX (X-axis) | Pin 14 (A0) |
| VRY (Y-axis) | Pin 15 (A1) |
| SW (button) | Pin 2 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+--------------------+          +-------------------+
|  Analog Joystick   |          |    Teensy 4.0     |
|                    |          |                   |
|  VCC  o------------|----------| 3.3V              |
|  GND  o------------|----------| GND               |
|  VRX  o------------|----------| Pin 14 (A0)       |
|  VRY  o------------|----------| Pin 15 (A1)       |
|  SW   o------------|----------| Pin 2             |
+--------------------+          +-------------------+
```
The Teensy 4.0 runs at 3.3V logic, so powering the joystick from the 3.3V pin keeps analog output voltages safely within the ADC's input range — do not use 5V here.

**Code**
```cpp
// Analog joystick on Teensy 4.0
// X axis -> A0 (pin 14), Y axis -> A1 (pin 15), Button -> pin 2

const int PIN_VRX = A0;   // pin 14
const int PIN_VRY = A1;   // pin 15
const int PIN_SW  = 2;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  while (!Serial && millis() < 3000) {}  // wait for Serial Monitor

  // Set ADC resolution to 12-bit for finer granularity (0-4095)
  analogReadResolution(12);

  // Button pin: use internal pull-up so SW reads HIGH at rest, LOW when pressed
  pinMode(PIN_SW, INPUT_PULLUP);

  Serial.println("Joystick ready.");
}

void loop() {
  int xVal      = analogRead(PIN_VRX);           // 0-4095
  int yVal      = analogRead(PIN_VRY);           // 0-4095
  bool btnPressed = (digitalRead(PIN_SW) == LOW); // LOW = pressed

  // Map raw ADC to -100..+100 percent for readability
  int xPct = map(xVal, 0, 4095, -100, 100);
  int yPct = map(yVal, 0, 4095, -100, 100);

  Serial.print("X: ");
  Serial.print(xPct);
  Serial.print("%  Y: ");
  Serial.print(yPct);
  Serial.print("%  Button: ");
  Serial.println(btnPressed ? "PRESSED" : "released");

  delay(100);
}
```

**How it works:** Calling `analogReadResolution(12)` unlocks the Teensy 4.0's full 12-bit ADC (versus the default 10-bit), giving 4096 steps for smoother joystick readings. The button pin uses `INPUT_PULLUP` so no external resistor is needed — the switch simply pulls the pin to GND when pressed, which we detect as a `LOW` reading.

A standard IR receiver module (like the VS1838B or TSOP38238) outputs a digital signal directly, so no analog pin is needed — any interrupt-capable digital GPIO works well for reliable pulse timing.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| DATA/OUT | Pin 2 |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
|    Teensy 4.0     |          |   IR Receiver     |
|                   |          |   (VS1838B etc.)  |
|              3.3V |----------| VCC               |
|               GND |----------| GND               |
|             Pin 2 |----------| DATA/OUT          |
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
```
The IR receiver module has a built-in demodulator and outputs clean 3.3V/5V-compatible digital pulses, so no pull-up resistor or level shifter is needed.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>
#include <IRremote.hpp>

const int IR_RECEIVE_PIN = 2;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  while (!Serial && millis() < 3000);
  Serial.println("IR Receiver ready");

  // Initialize the IR receiver on pin 2
  IrReceiver.begin(IR_RECEIVE_PIN, ENABLE_LED_FEEDBACK);
}

void loop() {
  if (IrReceiver.decode()) {
    // Print protocol, address, and command
    Serial.print("Protocol : ");
    Serial.println(getProtocolString(IrReceiver.decodedIRData.protocol));

    Serial.print("Address  : 0x");
    Serial.println(IrReceiver.decodedIRData.address, HEX);

    Serial.print("Command  : 0x");
    Serial.println(IrReceiver.decodedIRData.command, HEX);

    if (IrReceiver.decodedIRData.flags & IRDATA_FLAGS_IS_REPEAT) {
      Serial.println("(repeat)");
    }

    Serial.println();

    // Must call resume() to receive the next signal
    IrReceiver.resume();
  }
}
```

**How it works:** `IrReceiver.begin()` attaches an interrupt-driven timer to pin 2 that captures the demodulated pulse train in the background, so `decode()` in `loop()` just checks if a complete frame has been assembled — crucially, `IrReceiver.resume()` must be called after every successful decode to re-arm the receiver for the next transmission, otherwise it stops listening.

