**Code**
```cpp
#include <Servo.h>

Servo panServo;
Servo tiltServo;

const int PAN_PIN  = 2;
const int TILT_PIN = 3;

// Sweep limits in degrees
const int SERVO_MIN = 0;
const int SERVO_MAX = 180;
const int STEP_DELAY_MS = 15;

void sweepServo(Servo &servo, int from, int to) {
  if (from < to) {
    for (int pos = from; pos <= to; pos++) {
      servo.write(pos);
      delay(STEP_DELAY_MS);
    }
  } else {
    for (int pos = from; pos >= to; pos--) {
      servo.write(pos);
      delay(STEP_DELAY_MS);
    }
  }
}

void setup() {
  panServo.attach(PAN_PIN,  500, 2400);  // microsecond pulse range for SG90
  tiltServo.attach(TILT_PIN, 500, 2400);

  // Start both servos at center position
  panServo.write(90);
  tiltServo.write(90);
  delay(500);
}

void loop() {
  // Sweep pan left to right while tilt stays at center
  tiltServo.write(90);
  sweepServo(panServo, SERVO_MIN, SERVO_MAX);
  sweepServo(panServo, SERVO_MAX, SERVO_MIN);

  // Sweep tilt up to down while pan returns to center
  panServo.write(90);
  sweepServo(tiltServo, SERVO_MIN, SERVO_MAX);
  sweepServo(tiltServo, SERVO_MAX, SERVO_MIN);
}
```

**How it works:** The `attach(pin, 500, 2400)` call specifies the minimum and maximum pulse widths in microseconds for the SG90, overriding the library defaults — this matters because SG90 servos have a wider usable pulse range than the standard 1000–2000 µs, giving you the full 0–180° travel. The `sweepServo` helper takes a reference to whichever `Servo` object you pass in, so the same sweep logic drives both axes without code duplication.

The MCP2515 uses standard SPI with an additional interrupt line; here we use the default Teensy 4.0 SPI bus and assign CS to pin 10 and the INT line to pin 9 so it can trigger a hardware interrupt.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| SCK | Pin 13 (SPI SCK) |
| SI (MOSI) | Pin 11 (SPI MOSI) |
| SO (MISO) | Pin 12 (SPI MISO) |
| CS | Pin 10 (SPI CS) |
| INT | Pin 9 (interrupt-capable GPIO) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
|     Teensy 4.0      |          |    MCP2515 CAN    |
|                     |          |      Module       |
|  3.3V -------------+----------+-- VCC             |
|  GND  -------------+----------+-- GND             |
|  Pin 13 (SCK) -----+----------+-- SCK             |
|  Pin 11 (MOSI) ----+----------+-- SI              |
|  Pin 12 (MISO) ----+----------+-- SO              |
|  Pin 10 (CS) ------+----------+-- CS              |
|  Pin 9  (INT) -----+----------+-- INT             |
+---------------------+          +-------------------+
                                         |
                                  [CAN-H / CAN-L]
                                  (to CAN bus network)
```
The MCP2515 module runs on 3.3V; do NOT connect VCC to 5V as it may damage the module.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <SPI.h>
#include <mcp2515.h>  // Use the "MCP2515" library by autowp (install via Library Manager)

const int CS_PIN  = 10;
const int INT_PIN = 9;

MCP2515 mcp2515(CS_PIN);

struct can_frame rxFrame;
struct can_frame txFrame;

volatile bool msgAvailable = false;

void onCANInterrupt() {
  msgAvailable = true;
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  while (!Serial) {}  // wait for Serial Monitor

  SPI.begin();

  mcp2515.reset();
  // Set CAN bus speed to 500 kbps; adjust to match your network
  if (mcp2515.setBitrate(CAN_500KBPS, MCP_8MHZ) != MCP2515::ERROR_OK) {
    Serial.println("MCP2515 setBitrate failed!");
    while (1);
  }
  mcp2515.setNormalMode();  // go live on the bus

  pinMode(INT_PIN, INPUT);
  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(INT_PIN), onCANInterrupt, FALLING);

  // Prepare a test transmit frame
  txFrame.can_id  = 0x123;          // 11-bit standard ID
  txFrame.can_dlc = 4;              // 4 data bytes
  txFrame.data[0] = 0xDE;
  txFrame.data[1] = 0xAD;
  txFrame.data[2] = 0xBE;
  txFrame.data[3] = 0xEF;

  Serial.println("MCP2515 ready.");
}

void loop() {
  // --- Transmit a frame every 500 ms ---
  static unsigned long lastTx = 0;
  if (millis() - lastTx >= 500) {
    lastTx = millis();
    if (mcp2515.sendMessage(&txFrame) == MCP2515::ERROR_OK) {
      Serial.print("TX ID=0x");
      Serial.println(txFrame.can_id, HEX);
    } else {
      Serial.println("TX error");
    }
  }

  // --- Receive frames on interrupt ---
  if (msgAvailable) {
    msgAvailable = false;
    if (mcp2515.readMessage(&rxFrame) == MCP2515::ERROR_OK) {
      Serial.print("RX ID=0x");
      Serial.print(rxFrame.can_id, HEX);
      Serial.print(" DLC=");
      Serial.print(rxFrame.can_dlc);
      Serial.print(" Data:");
      for (int i = 0; i < rxFrame.can_dlc; i++) {
        Serial.print(" 0x");
        Serial.print(rxFrame.data[i], HEX);
      }
      Serial.println();
    }
  }
}
```

**How it works:** The `setBitrate(CAN_500KBPS, MCP_8MHZ)` call must match *both* the desired bus speed and the oscillator crystal actually soldered on your MCP2515 module (commonly 8 MHz or 16 MHz) — a mismatch will silently corrupt all frames. The INT pin triggers a falling-edge interrupt so `readMessage()` is only called when the MCP2515 has a frame waiting, avoiding wasteful polling over SPI.

The NEO-6M communicates over UART, so we use Teensy 4.0's Serial1 (pins 0/1) to keep the USB Serial port free for debug output.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to Teensy 4 0 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |
| TX | Pin 0 (RX1) |
| RX | Pin 1 (TX1) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
|    Teensy 4.0     |          |    NEO-6M GPS     |
|                   |          |                   |
|           3.3V ---+----------+--- VCC            |
|            GND ---+----------+--- GND            |
|   Pin 0 (RX1) ---+----------+--- TX             |
|   Pin 1 (TX1) ---+----------+--- RX             |
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
```
The NEO-6M module can accept either 3.3V or 5V supply — use 3.3V here since the Teensy's logic is 3.3V and the module's TX line would otherwise require a level shifter.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// NEO-6M connected to Serial1: RX=pin0, TX=pin1
// Default NEO-6M baud rate is 9600
#define GPS_SERIAL  Serial1
#define GPS_BAUD    9600

String nmeaBuffer = "";

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);       // USB serial for debug output
  while (!Serial && millis() < 3000); // wait up to 3s for USB

  GPS_SERIAL.begin(GPS_BAUD); // start UART to GPS module
  Serial.println("NEO-6M GPS ready. Waiting for data...");
}

void loop() {
  // Read all available bytes from GPS
  while (GPS_SERIAL.available()) {
    char c = (char)GPS_SERIAL.read();

    if (c == '\n') {
      // Complete NMEA sentence received
      nmeaBuffer.trim();
      if (nmeaBuffer.length() > 0) {
        Serial.println(nmeaBuffer);  // Print full sentence to USB serial

        // Example: detect and highlight $GPRMC sentences
        if (nmeaBuffer.startsWith("$GPRMC")) {
          Serial.println("  ^ RMC sentence (position/time data)");
        }
        // Example: detect $GPGGA sentences (fix quality, altitude, etc.)
        else if (nmeaBuffer.startsWith("$GPGGA")) {
          Serial.println("  ^ GGA sentence (fix + altitude data)");
        }
      }
      nmeaBuffer = ""; // reset buffer for next sentence
    } else if (c != '\r') {
      nmeaBuffer += c; // accumulate characters
    }
  }
}
```

**How it works:** NMEA sentences are newline-delimited ASCII strings, so we accumulate characters into a String buffer and flush it to USB Serial only when a `'\n'` is received — this ensures we always print complete, parseable sentences rather than fragmented lines. Using `Serial1` (hardware UART) instead of a SoftwareSerial workaround is critical on Teensy 4.0 because it offloads the byte timing to dedicated hardware, preventing missed characters at higher GPS output rates.

The SX1278 runs on 3.3V only, so it connects directly to the Teensy 4.0's 3.3V rail — no level shifting needed since the Teensy's SPI pins are 3.3V logic.

