| Touch pad / wire | Pin 23 (Touch input, labeled T0) |
| (Optional) 1–10 MΩ resistor to GND | Pin 23 to GND (improves sensitivity/stability) |
| Onboard LED | Pin 13 (built-in, no external wiring needed) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
+-------------------------+
| Teensy 4.0 |
| |
| Pin 23 (T0) ---[wire/pad]--- [Finger / Touch Electrode]
| |
| (optional 1-10MΩ |
| resistor to GND) |
| |
| Pin 13 (LED) [onboard LED, no wiring needed]
| |
| GND |
+-------------------------+
```
No external power supply is needed for the touch electrode itself — the Teensy's internal charge-transfer circuit handles sensing entirely on-chip.

**Code**
```cpp
// Teensy 4.0 built-in capacitive touchRead example
// Pin 23 is touch-capable (T0). Other touch pins: 22, 23, 0, 1, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19

const int TOUCH_PIN = 23;
const int LED_PIN   = 13;

// Threshold: values above this count as a touch.
// Raw touchRead() returns higher numbers when touched.
// Calibrate this to your electrode size and environment.
const int TOUCH_THRESHOLD = 2000;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
  // No pinMode() call needed for touch pins —
  // touchRead() configures the pin automatically.
}

void loop() {
  int touchValue = touchRead(TOUCH_PIN);

  Serial.print("Touch value: ");
  Serial.println(touchValue);

  if (touchValue > TOUCH_THRESHOLD) {
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH);
    Serial.println("  --> TOUCHED!");
  } else {
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  }

  delay(50); // Sample ~20 times per second
}
```

**How it works:** `touchRead()` is a Teensyduino built-in function that drives a charge-transfer measurement on the selected pin and returns a unitless integer — higher values indicate a finger is present because human capacitance slows the charge cycle. The threshold value (`2000`) must be tuned experimentally, since it depends on electrode size, wire length, and nearby grounding; print the raw values in the Serial Monitor while touching and not touching to find the right crossover point for your setup.

The BMP280 connects to Port A (GPIO32/GPIO33) on the M5Stack Core2, keeping the internal I2C bus on GPIO21/GPIO22 free for onboard peripherals like the AXP192 and touch controller.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to M5Stack Core2 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3V (Grove Port A) |
| GND | GND (Grove Port A) |
| SDA | GPIO32 (Port A SDA) |
| SCL | GPIO33 (Port A SCL) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
