The relay module must be powered from 5V (VIN on ESP32 dev board) since most relay modules require 5V coil voltage; keep the relay's switched load wiring completely separate from the ESP32 circuitry.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

const int LDR_PIN     = 34;   // ADC1 channel, input-only GPIO
const int RELAY_PIN   = 26;   // drives relay IN signal

// ADC threshold: 0-4095 (12-bit). Lower value = darker (LDR resistance high,
// voltage divider pulls GPIO34 LOW). Tune this for your environment.
const int DARK_THRESHOLD = 1500;

// Relay logic: most relay modules are ACTIVE LOW (LOW = relay ON).
// Set to true if your module is active-low, false if active-high.
const bool RELAY_ACTIVE_LOW = true;

void setRelay(bool on) {
  if (RELAY_ACTIVE_LOW) {
    digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, on ? LOW : HIGH);
  } else {
    digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, on ? HIGH : LOW);
  }
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(LDR_PIN,   INPUT);   // GPIO34 is input-only, no pull needed
  pinMode(RELAY_PIN, OUTPUT);
  setRelay(false);             // ensure light is OFF at startup
  analogReadResolution(12);    // ESP32 default: 12-bit ADC (0-4095)
  analogSetAttenuation(ADC_11db); // allows reading up to ~3.3V
}

void loop() {
  int ldrValue = analogRead(LDR_PIN);
  bool isDark  = (ldrValue < DARK_THRESHOLD);

  setRelay(isDark);  // turn light ON when dark, OFF when bright

  Serial.print("LDR ADC: ");
  Serial.print(ldrValue);
  Serial.print("  Light: ");
  Serial.println(isDark ? "ON" : "OFF");

  delay(500);
}
```

**How it works:** The LDR and 10kΩ resistor form a voltage divider — in darkness the LDR's resistance rises dramatically, pulling GPIO34 toward GND and giving a low ADC reading that triggers the relay. The `RELAY_ACTIVE_LOW` flag and `setRelay()` wrapper handle the fact that most opto-isolated relay modules turn ON when their IN pin is driven LOW, which is the opposite of what beginners expect.

Thermal receipt printers typically use 5V TTL UART logic; since the ESP32's TX pin outputs 3.3V, most common thermal printer modules (like the Adafruit or CSN-A2 series) accept 3.3V-level signals on their RX pin, but the printer's TX line may output 5V — use a voltage divider on the printer TX → ESP32 RX path to protect the ESP32.

**Wiring Table**

| Component Pin | Connects to ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 5V (external 5V supply, ~2A rated) |
| GND | GND (shared with ESP32 GND) |
| TX (printer out) | GPIO16 (via voltage divider: 1kΩ + 2kΩ to GND) |
| RX (printer in) | GPIO17 (ESP32 TX2 direct) |

**Wiring Diagram**
```
 +---------------------+         +-------------------------+
 |        ESP32        |         |   Thermal Receipt       |
 |                     |         |       Printer           |
 |    GPIO17 (TX2) ----+-------->| RX                      |
 |                     |         |                         |
 |    GPIO16 (RX2) <---+--[1kΩ]--+-- TX         VCC ------+---> 5V PSU
 |                     |         |                         |
 |                     |  [2kΩ]  |         GND  ----------+---> GND
 |             GND ----+--+      |                         |
 |                     | GND     +-------------------------+
 +---------------------+
      (voltage divider on printer TX line)
```
Use a dedicated 5V power supply capable of at least 1.5–2A for the printer — do NOT power it from the ESP32's onboard 5V pin, as thermal printing causes large current spikes.

**Code**
```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

// Use UART2 on ESP32 to avoid conflicting with USB Serial (UART0)
#define PRINTER_RX_PIN 16  // ESP32 RX2 <- printer TX (via voltage divider)
#define PRINTER_TX_PIN 17  // ESP32 TX2 -> printer RX
#define PRINTER_BAUD   9600

HardwareSerial printerSerial(2);  // UART2

void printerWrite(const char* text) {
  printerSerial.print(text);
}

void printerWriteln(const char* text) {
  printerSerial.print(text);
  printerSerial.write('\n');
}

void feedLines(uint8_t lines) {
  for (uint8_t i = 0; i < lines; i++) {
    printerSerial.write('\n');
  }
}

void printerInit() {
  // ESC @ resets the printer to default settings
  printerSerial.write(0x1B);
  printerSerial.write('@');
  delay(50);
}

void setBold(bool enable) {
  // ESC E n  — bold on (n=1) or off (n=0)
  printerSerial.write(0x1B);
  printerSerial.write('E');
  printerSerial.write(enable ? 1 : 0);
}

void setAlignCenter() {
  // ESC a 1 — center alignment
  printerSerial.write(0x1B);
  printerSerial.write('a');
  printerSerial.write((uint8_t)1);
}

void setAlignLeft() {
  // ESC a 0 — left alignment
  printerSerial.write(0x1B);
  printerSerial.write('a');
  printerSerial.write((uint8_t)0);
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  printerSerial.begin(PRINTER_BAUD, SERIAL_8N1, PRINTER_RX_PIN, PRINTER_TX_PIN);
  delay(500);  // give printer time to power up

  printerInit();

  setAlignCenter();
  setBold(true);
  printerWriteln("HELLO FROM ESP32");
  setBold(false);

  printerWriteln("------------------------");

  setAlignLeft();
  printerWriteln("Item 1 ........... $1.00");
  printerWriteln("Item 2 ........... $2.50");
  printerWriteln("Item 3 ........... $0.75");
  printerWriteln("------------------------");

  setBold(true);
  printerWriteln("TOTAL ............ $4.25");
  setBold(false);

  feedLines(4);  // advance paper so text is visible past the cutter

  Serial.println("Print job sent.");
}

void loop() {
  // Nothing to do after initial print
}
```

**How it works:** The code uses `HardwareSerial(2)` to access ESP32's UART2 peripheral so the USB debug port (UART0) stays free; printer formatting relies on raw ESC/POS escape byte sequences (e.g., `0x1B, 'E', 1` for bold) sent directly over the serial stream, which is the industry-standard command language understood by virtually all thermal receipt printers.

